FADE IN:

EXT. WINE-DARK SEA - DAY

Grey swell heaves under a hard sky. A black-hulled ship claws west. Far behind, the smoking ruin of a walled city on a windy plain.

(SOUND: wind tearing canvas, oars groaning in their tholes)

NARRATOR (V.O.)
Tell me, O Muse, of that ingenious hero who travelled far and wide after he had sacked the famous town of Troy.

Waves smash the bow. Men cling to the rail. One man stands amidships, dark-bearded, broad-shouldered, eyes fixed on a horizon that will not yield.

NARRATOR (V.O.)
Many cities did he visit, and many were the nations with whose manners and customs he was acquainted.

The same man, later, staggered, salt-white, alone on a raft of lashed timbers.

NARRATOR (V.O.)
Moreover he suffered much by sea while trying to save his own life and bring his men safely home.

A beach of black rock. Ox-bones and empty hides. The sun stands over a slaughtered herd.

NARRATOR (V.O.)
But do what he might he could not save his men, for they perished through their own sheer folly in eating the cattle of the Sun-god Hyperion.

INT. HALL OF OLYMPIAN JOVE - DAY

A vast colonnade of pale stone open to cloud. JOVE sits throned, heavy-limbed, white-bearded, a thunderbolt carved along the arm of his seat. The other immortals stand in attendance. MINERVA waits near him: grey-eyed, tall, a bronze-shod spear in her fist, golden sandals on her feet.

(SOUND: distant thunder rolling under the floor)

NARRATOR (V.O.)
So now all who escaped death in battle or by shipwreck had got safely home except Ulysses.

JOVE
See now, how men lay blame upon us gods for what is after all nothing but their own folly.

He turns the thought in his hand like a stone.

JOVE (CONT'D)
Look at Aegisthus; he must needs make love to Agamemnon's wife unrighteously and then kill Agamemnon, though he knew it would be the death of him.

MINERVA steps forward. Light catches the grey of her eyes.

MINERVA
Father, son of Saturn, King of kings, it served Aegisthus right, and so it would any one else who does as he did.

JOVE watches her.

MINERVA (CONT'D)
But Aegisthus is neither here nor there; it is for Ulysses that my heart bleeds, when I think of his sufferings in that lonely sea-girt island.

She points as if the island hung in the air between them.

MINERVA (CONT'D)
A goddess lives there, daughter of the magician Atlas. She has got hold of poor unhappy Ulysses, and keeps trying to make him forget his home.

JOVE
My child, what are you talking about? How can I forget Ulysses than whom there is no more capable man on earth?

He leans forward.

JOVE (CONT'D)
Bear in mind, however, that Neptune is still furious with Ulysses for having blinded an eye of Polyphemus king of the Cyclopes.

MINERVA
Father, son of Saturn, if the gods now mean that Ulysses should get home, we should first send Mercury to the Ogygian island.

She tightens her grip on the spear.

MINERVA (CONT'D)
In the meantime I will go to Ithaca, to put heart into Ulysses' son Telemachus.

She binds on the glittering golden sandals. She grasps the redoubtable bronze-shod spear. She darts from the topmost summit like a falling star.

EXT. GATEWAY OF ULYSSES HOUSE - DAY

A stone gateway on a rocky Ithacan hillside. Olive trees. Beyond, a courtyard where lordly SUITORS sit on ox-hides playing draughts. Men-servants mix wine, sponge tables, cut meat.

MINERVA stands at the gate in the likeness of a Taphian sea-chief: bearded, travel-cloaked, a bronze spear in her hand.

(SOUND: knucklebones clicking, knives on wood, flies over meat)

TELEMACHUS sits among them, young, dark-haired, wonderfully like his father about the head and eyes, brooding. He sees the stranger first.

He rises at once, vexed that a guest should wait. He takes her right hand and the spear.

TELEMACHUS
Welcome to our house, and when you have partaken of food you shall tell us what you have come for.

He leads her in.

INT. ULYSSES HOUSE - GREAT HALL - DAY

High timbered hall, smoke-dark beams, spear-stand against a bearing-post crowded with a missing man's weapons. TELEMACHUS sets MINERVA's spear among his father's. He seats her on a damask-spread chair with a footstool, and takes a seat near her, away from the noise.

A maid pours water from a golden ewer into a silver basin. Bread, meats, gold cups. Wine.

(SOUND: water into silver, the suitors' roar from the benches)

The SUITORS clatter in and fall on the food. A servant forces a lyre into the hands of a reluctant bard. The bard strikes up.

TELEMACHUS leans close to MINERVA so no man may hear.

TELEMACHUS
I hope, sir, that you will not be offended with what I am going to say. Singing comes cheap to those who do not pay for it.

He glances at the devouring men.

TELEMACHUS (CONT'D)
All this is done at the cost of one whose bones lie rotting in some wilderness or grinding to powder in the surf.

MINERVA
I will tell you truly. I am Mentes, son of Anchialus, and I am King of the Taphians.

She drinks, watching him.

MINERVA (CONT'D)
They told me your father was at home again, and that was why I came, but it seems the gods are still keeping him back.

TELEMACHUS's hands tighten on his knees.

MINERVA (CONT'D)
I am no prophet, but I speak as it is borne in upon me from heaven, and assure you that he will not be away much longer.

She studies his face.

MINERVA (CONT'D)
But tell me, and tell me true, can Ulysses really have such a fine looking fellow for a son? You are indeed wonderfully like him about the head and eyes.

TELEMACHUS
My mother tells me I am son to Ulysses, but it is a wise child that knows his own father.

MINERVA
There is no fear of your race dying out yet, while Penelope has such a fine son as you are. But tell me, what is the meaning of all this feasting?

TELEMACHUS
So long as my father was here it was well with us and with the house, but the gods have hidden him away more closely than mortal man was ever yet hidden.

He swallows.

TELEMACHUS (CONT'D)
The chiefs from all our islands are eating up my house under the pretext of paying their court to my mother, who will neither say that she will not marry, nor yet bring matters to an end.

MINERVA
Is that so? Then you do indeed want Ulysses home again. Give him his helmet, shield, and a couple of lances.

Her voice hardens, still in a man's register.

MINERVA (CONT'D)
If he is the man he was when I first knew him, he would soon lay his hands about these rascally suitors.

She leans in.

MINERVA (CONT'D)
Take my advice, call the Achaean heroes in assembly to-morrow morning. Bid the suitors take themselves off, each to his own place.

TELEMACHUS listens as if the words were iron being put into his hands.

MINERVA (CONT'D)
As for yourself, take the best ship you can get, with a crew of twenty men, and go in quest of your father who has so long been missing.

TELEMACHUS
It has been very kind of you to talk to me in this way, as though I were your own son, and I will do all you tell me.

MINERVA
Do not try to keep me, for I would be on my way at once. As for any present, keep it till I come again.

She is gone like a bird into the air. TELEMACHUS stares at the empty seat, knowing a god has been with him.

The bard still sings the sad return from Troy. PENELOPE comes down the great staircase, attended by two handmaids, a veil before her face, weeping. She is a woman in her prime, dark-haired, stately, worn with waiting. She stands by a bearing-post.

(SOUND: lyre string, her breath catching)

PENELOPE
Phemius, you know many another feat of gods and heroes. Sing the suitors some one of these, and let them drink their wine in silence.

She presses the veil closer.

PENELOPE (CONT'D)
But cease this sad tale, for it breaks my sorrowful heart, and reminds me of my lost husband whom I mourn ever without ceasing.

TELEMACHUS
Mother, let the bard sing what he has a mind to; bards do not make the ills they sing of; it is Jove, not they, who makes them.

PENELOPE looks at her son as if she had not seen him till now.

TELEMACHUS (CONT'D)
Go, then, within the house and busy yourself with your daily duties, your loom, your distaff, and the ordering of your servants; for speech is man's matter, and mine above all others.

She goes wondering back upstairs. The SUITORS grow clamorous, each praying he may be her bedfellow.

TELEMACHUS stands.

TELEMACHUS
Shameless and insolent suitors, let us feast at our pleasure now, and let there be no brawling, for it is a rare thing to hear a man with such a divine voice.

ANTINOUS, handsome, insolent, a young chief with oiled hair and a cruel mouth, lifts his cup.

TELEMACHUS (CONT'D)
But in the morning meet me in full assembly that I may give you formal notice to depart, and feast at one another's houses, at your own cost.

ANTINOUS
The gods seem to have given you lessons in bluster and tall talking; may Jove never grant you to be chief in Ithaca as your father was before you.

TELEMACHUS
Antinous, do not chide with me, but, god willing, I will be chief too if I can. I will be chief in my own house, and will rule those whom Ulysses has won for me.

The suitors return to singing and dancing until night. They go home each to his own abode.

INT. TELEMACHUS BEDROOM - NIGHT

A high room in a tower looking on the outer court. EURYCLEA, a good old woman, grey-haired, straight-backed, devoted, goes before TELEMACHUS with two blazing torches. He sits on the bed, pulls off his shirt, and gives it to her. She folds it and hangs it on a peg.

(SOUND: torch-hiss, silver catch, bolt drawn home by a strap)

She goes out, pulls the door to by a silver catch, and draws the bolt. TELEMACHUS lies under a woollen fleece, eyes open, thinking all night of the voyage and of the counsel Minerva gave him.

EXT. ITHACA ASSEMBLY PLACE - DAY

Rosy-fingered dawn. TELEMACHUS binds on sandals, girds his sword, and walks spear in hand with two hounds. MINERVA has given him a presence of divine comeliness. Old councillors make way as he takes his father's seat.

(SOUND: criers calling, crowd settling, hounds panting)

The people of Ithaca gather. TELEMACHUS stands in the middle. A herald puts a staff into his hand.

TELEMACHUS
It is I who have convened you, for it is I who am the most aggrieved. My grievance turns on two great misfortunes which have fallen upon my house.

He looks from face to face.

TELEMACHUS (CONT'D)
The first is the loss of my excellent father, who was chief among all you here present. The second is much more serious, and ere long will be the utter ruin of my estate.

A stir among the younger men.

TELEMACHUS (CONT'D)
The sons of all the chief men among you are pestering my mother to marry them against her will. Day by day they keep hanging about my father's house, sacrificing our oxen, sheep, and fat goats.

He dashes the staff to the ground and bursts into tears. No one answers angrily save ANTINOUS, who rises sleek and smiling.

ANTINOUS
Telemachus, insolent braggart that you are, how dare you try to throw the blame upon us suitors? It is your mother's fault not ours, for she is a very artful woman.

He paces before them.

ANTINOUS (CONT'D)
This three years past, and close on four, she has been driving us out of our minds. She set up a great tambour frame and began to work on an enormous piece of fine needlework.

INT. ULYSSES HOUSE - UPPER CHAMBER - NIGHT

A great loom. PENELOPE works a pall by torchlight, then, when the house is still, unpicks the stitches with quick, secret fingers. A maid watches from the shadow and slips away.

(SOUND: torch crackle, thread whispering out of cloth)

ANTINOUS (V.O.)
She fooled us in this way for three years and we never found her out, but as time wore on one of her maids told us, and we caught her in the act.

EXT. ITHACA ASSEMBLY PLACE - DAY

ANTINOUS faces TELEMACHUS.

ANTINOUS
Send your mother away, and bid her marry the man of her own and of her father's choice. We will not go back to our lands till she has made her choice and married some one or other of us.

TELEMACHUS
Antinous, how can I drive the mother who bore me from my father's house? My father is abroad and we do not know whether he is alive or dead.

Two eagles drop from the mountain and fly side by side over the assembly. They wheel, glare death, tear at one another, and drive off toward the right over the town.

(SOUND: wingbeats, a cry from the crowd)

TELEMACHUS
Give me, then, a ship and a crew of twenty men, and I will go to Sparta and to Pylos in quest of my father who has so long been missing.

The assembly breaks. Men go home. The suitors return to the house of Ulysses.

EXT. ITHACA SEASHORE - DAY

TELEMACHUS stands alone and washes his hands in the grey waves.

(SOUND: surf, gulls)

TELEMACHUS
Hear me, you god who visited me yesterday, and bade me sail the seas in search of my father who has so long been missing.

MINERVA comes beside him in the likeness and voice of Mentor: an older man, cloak, seasoned face.

MINERVA
Telemachus, if you are made of the same stuff as your father you will be neither fool nor coward henceforward, for Ulysses never broke his word nor left his work half done.

She looks seaward.

MINERVA (CONT'D)
As for your voyage, it shall not be long delayed; your father was such an old friend of mine that I will find you a ship, and will come with you myself.

INT. ULYSSES HOUSE - STORE-ROOM - DAY

Gold and bronze heaped on the floor. Open chests of linen. Casks of old wine against the wall. EURYCLEA keeps the well-made doors. TELEMACHUS calls her in.

(SOUND: door-leaves, the glug of wine drawn into jars)

TELEMACHUS
Nurse, draw me off some of the best wine you have, after what you are keeping for my father's own drinking. Let me have twelve jars, and see that they all have lids.

EURYCLEA
My dear child, what ever can have put such notion as that into your head? Where in the world do you want to go to—you, who are the one hope of the house?

Tears stand in her eyes.

EURYCLEA (CONT'D)
Stay where you are among your own people, and do not go wandering and worrying your life out on the barren ocean.

TELEMACHUS
Fear not, nurse, my scheme is not without heaven's sanction; but swear that you will say nothing about all this to my mother, till I have been away some ten or twelve days.

She swears. She fills the jars and the leathern bags with barley meal.

EXT. ITHACA HARBOUR - NIGHT

Darkness over the land. A ship waits at the end of the harbour, tackle aboard. Crew at the water side. MINERVA, still as Mentor, leads TELEMACHUS. Stores come down from the cloister.

(SOUND: hawsers loosed, sail slatting, then filling)

They sit in the stern. A west wind whistles over the deep blue waves. White sails go aloft on ropes of twisted ox hide. Foam hisses at the bows. Mixing-bowls brim. Drink offerings go over the side, most of all to the grey-eyed daughter of Jove.

The ship speeds through the watches of the night from dark till dawn.

EXT. PYLOS SHORE - DAWN

The sun rises from the fair sea. Nine guilds of Pylian men offer black bulls to Neptune on the sand. Smoke, spits, inward meats.

TELEMACHUS's ship furls sail and anchors. MINERVA leads. TELEMACHUS follows, suddenly shy.

(SOUND: surf, bull-bellow, fat hissing on embers)

TELEMACHUS
But how, Mentor, dare I go up to Nestor, and how am I to address him? I have never yet been used to holding long conversations with people.

MINERVA
Some things, Telemachus, will be suggested to you by your own instinct, and heaven will prompt you further.

NESTOR sits among his sons, very old, white-haired, a knight still, sceptre-minded even at a feast. His people crowd the strangers, take their hands, seat them on sheepskins. Wine in a golden cup goes first to MINERVA.

MINERVA
O thou that encirclest the earth, vouchsafe to grant the prayers of thy servants that call upon thee. Grant Telemachus and myself a happy issue, in respect of the matter that has brought us to Pylos.

She passes the cup. TELEMACHUS prays likewise. When the meats are done, NESTOR turns to them.

NESTOR
Now that our guests have done their dinner, it will be best to ask them who they are. Who, then, sir strangers, are you, and from what port have you sailed?

TELEMACHUS
Nestor, son of Neleus, honour to the Achaean name, we come from Ithaca under Neritum. I seek news of my unhappy father Ulysses, who is said to have sacked the town of Troy in company with yourself.

NESTOR's old eyes sharpen.

TELEMACHUS (CONT'D)
Do not soften things out of any pity for me, but tell me in all plainness exactly what you saw.

NESTOR
My friend, you recall a time of much sorrow to my mind. Our best men all of them fell there—Ajax, Achilles, Patroclus, and my own dear son Antilochus.

He is quiet a moment.

NESTOR (CONT'D)
During all this time there was no one who could compare with your father in subtlety—if indeed you are his son—I can hardly believe my eyes—and you talk just like him too.

TELEMACHUS
Would that heaven might grant me to do like vengeance on the insolence of the wicked suitors, who are ill treating me and plotting my ruin.

NESTOR
Who knows but what Ulysses may come back after all, and pay these scoundrels in full. If Minerva were to take as great a liking to you as she did to Ulysses, these wooers would soon some of them forget their wooing.

MINERVA
Heaven has a long arm if it is minded to save a man. I would rather suffer much before getting home, than get home quickly, and then be killed in my own house as Agamemnon was.

NESTOR tells of Clytemnestra, of Aegisthus, of Orestes' return in the eighth year, and of Menelaus coming home that same day with treasure.

NESTOR
Take my advice then, and do not go travelling about for long so far from home. Still, I should advise you by all means to go and visit Menelaus.

The sun sets. MINERVA bids them cut the tongues and mix wine. Then she and TELEMACHUS would go to the ship, but NESTOR will not hear of it.

NESTOR
Heaven and the immortal gods forbid that you should leave my house to go on board of a ship. I shall not permit the son of my old friend Ulysses to camp down on the deck of a ship—not while I live.

MINERVA
Sir, you have spoken well. Telemachus shall return with you and sleep at your house, but I must go back to give orders to my crew.

She flies away in the form of an eagle. All marvel. NESTOR takes TELEMACHUS by the hand.

NESTOR
My friend, I see that you are going to be a great hero some day, since the gods wait upon you thus while you are still so young.

INT. NESTOR HOUSE - HALL - NIGHT

Sweet wine eleven years old. Drink offerings to Minerva. NESTOR puts TELEMACHUS to sleep in the room over the gateway with his unmarried son. NESTOR himself sleeps in an inner room beside his wife.

(SOUND: mixing-bowl, a house settling, night insects)

EXT. NESTOR HOUSE - DAWN

White marble benches before the house. NESTOR sits sceptre in hand. His sons gather. A heifer is brought from the plain. A goldsmith gilds her horns. MINERVA comes unseen to accept the sacrifice. The blow falls. Women scream with delight. Thigh bones burn in fat. Wine is poured.

(SOUND: axe through tendon, fire taking fat, spit-meat dripping)

Lovely Polycaste washes TELEMACHUS and anoints him. He comes from the bath like a god. Horses are yoked. The housekeeper packs bread, wine, and sweetmeats. TELEMACHUS takes the chariot. They drive all day, sway of the yoke, and lie at Pherae. Another dawn, another drive, until the sun sets on the low-lying city of Lacedaemon.

INT. MENELAUS PALACE - HALL - NIGHT

Splendour like sun and moon: bronze, gold, amber, ivory, silver. A wedding feast is in progress, bard and tumblers. TELEMACHUS and his companion are washed, cloaked, seated. A maid pours water from gold into silver.

(SOUND: lyre, bare feet of tumblers, gold cups)

MENELAUS, a mature king, travel-worn under his wealth, greets them and sets fat roast loin before them.

MENELAUS
Fall to, and welcome; when you have done supper I shall ask who you are, for the lineage of such men as you cannot have been lost.

TELEMACHUS speaks low to his companion, staring at the gleam. MENELAUS overhears.

MENELAUS
No one, my sons, can hold his own with Jove. Among mortal men there may be another who has as much wealth as I have, or there may not.

His face changes.

MENELAUS (CONT'D)
But while I was travelling and getting great riches, my brother was secretly and shockingly murdered through the perfidy of his wicked wife, so that I have no pleasure in being lord of all this wealth.

He looks past them.

MENELAUS (CONT'D)
Yet grieve for these as I may, I do so for one man more than for them all. No one of all the Achaeans worked so hard or risked so much as he did.

Tears fall from TELEMACHUS's eyes. He holds his cloak before his face with both hands.

Helen comes down from her high vaulted room, lovely as Diana, a silver work-box on wheels beside her, a distaff of violet wool. She looks long at TELEMACHUS. MENELAUS sees the likeness as she does: hands, feet, hair, the shape of the head, the eyes.

MENELAUS
Bless my heart, then I am receiving a visit from the son of a very dear friend, who suffered much hardship for my sake.

They weep, all of them. Helen drugs the wine with an Egyptian herb that banishes care. She tells how Ulysses entered Troy in rags, and how she alone knew him. MENELAUS tells of the wooden horse, and how Helen circled it calling the chiefs by name, and how Ulysses clapped his hands over a man's mouth and saved them all.

TELEMACHUS
How sad that all this was of no avail to save him, nor yet his own iron courage. But now, sir, be pleased to send us all to bed.

They are led to rest under the echoing gatehouse of that rich house.

INT. ULYSSES HOUSE - GREAT HALL - NIGHT

Meanwhile in Ithaca the SUITORS feast. ANTINOUS leans into a knot of young men. A ship is to be laid in wait in the strait between Ithaca and Samos, to murder TELEMACHUS on his way home.

(SOUND: cups slammed, a low ugly laugh)

ANTINOUS
He will be like his father and perish far from his friends. In this case we should have plenty to do, for we could then divide up his property amongst us.

PENELOPE hears of the ambush later and lies down fasting, weeping for her son until MINERVA sends a phantom of her sister to comfort her in sleep. The phantom tells her Telemachus has a guide such as other men have not.

INT. MENELAUS PALACE - HALL - DAY

Dawn. MENELAUS sits with TELEMACHUS. No more weeping. Hard news.

(SOUND: courtyard horses, a fountain)

MENELAUS
I will tell you all that the old man of the sea told me, hiding nothing. I was held in Egypt by the gods, and would be there still, had not a goddess taken pity on me.

He speaks of Proteus among the seals, of the ambush in sealskins, of the old one telling who was living and who was dead.

MENELAUS (CONT'D)
He said that he had seen your father in an island weeping. The nymph Calypso was keeping him in her cave and wanted him to marry her, but he would not.

TELEMACHUS's whole body changes.

MENELAUS (CONT'D)
As for Ajax, he was wrecked. Agamemnon your uncle's murderer Aegisthus slew, though he himself paid for it. And your father, if he is not already dead, shall yet come home.

They stay on in Sparta, guest-gifts piling: a mixing-bowl of silver with a gold rim, a chariot, horses. Far away, the suitors' ship waits in the strait like a knife.

INT. HALL OF OLYMPIAN JOVE - DAY

The gods again. MINERVA stands before JOVE. Neptune is still among the Ethiopians.

(SOUND: a low celestial drone, like wind over high stone)

MINERVA
Father, son of Saturn, King of kings, it is for Ulysses that my heart bleeds. Send Mercury to the Ogygian island to tell Calypso that we have made up our minds and that he is to return.

JOVE
My child, you have spoken well. Mercury shall go, and Calypso shall let the man go, and he shall reach the Phaeacians, who will honour him as though he were a god.

A messenger of the gods takes the wand that seals men's eyes, and drops from Olympus toward the far sea.

INT. CALYPSO CAVE - DAY

A great cave on a woodland isle, the navel of the sea. Alder, poplar, cypress. A vine with ripe clusters over the mouth. Four fountains. Meadows of violet and parsley. CALYPSO, a fair goddess, sings at her loom with a golden shuttle. She is beautiful the way deep water is beautiful, and lonely.

ULYSSES sits on the shore beyond, not in the cave. Weathered warrior, dark beard salted with grey, broad-shouldered, a white scar hidden under the rag of his tunic. He weeps, looking over the barren ocean, tired of life, thinking only how he may see the smoke of his own chimneys.

(SOUND: loom, spring-water, surf far down)

The messenger stands in the cave and delivers Jove's command. CALYPSO shudders, then stills.

CALYPSO
You gods are insatiable of mischief. You are jealous if a goddess takes a fancy to a mortal husband. But let him go, if Jove has so ordered it.

She finds ULYSSES where he sits crying. She comes near him.

CALYPSO
My poor friend, you shall not stay here grieving any longer. I have determined to send you away. Cut down some trees, make yourself a large raft, and I will stock it with bread, water, and wine.

ULYSSES looks at her with the caution of a hunted man.

ULYSSES
Goddess, I am sure you mean nothing but mischief. You cannot really mean that you will send me home on a raft. I will not go unless you take a solemn oath that you mean me no harm.

CALYPSO smiles, strokes him with her hand, and swears by earth, heaven, and the water of Styx. She leads him to the cave. He sits where she had sat. Food for mortals is set before him; she is served nectar and ambrosia. Then she speaks more softly.

CALYPSO
Ulysses, noble son of Laertes, so you would start home to your own land at once? Good luck go with you. If you could only know how much suffering is in store for you, you would stay here and be lord of this island, and I would make you immortal.

ULYSSES
Goddess, do not be angry with me about this. I am quite aware that my wife Penelope is nothing like so tall or so beautiful as yourself.

He looks toward the sea.

ULYSSES (CONT'D)
She is only a woman, whereas you are an immortal. Nevertheless, I want to get home, and can think of nothing else.

They sleep, each turned a little from the other, the decision already made.

EXT. OGYGIA SHORE - DAY

Dawn. CALYPSO gives him a great bronze axe, fitted to an olive handle, and an adze. She leads him to tall, dry, long-seasoned trees. He fells twenty. He adzes them true. He bores and fits them with pegs. A deck, a mast, a yard, a rudder. Cloth for a sail. In four days the raft is done.

(SOUND: axe in timber, adze, the sea waiting)

On the fifth she bathes him, gives him fragrant clothes, a skin of dark wine, a smaller one of water, and a bag of meat. A warm, kindly wind. He spreads his sail. He never closes his eyes, but keeps them on the Pleiads, the Bear, and Orion, as she told him.

Seventeen days he sails. On the eighteenth the mountains of the Phaeacians rise like a shield on the sea.

EXT. OPEN SEA - DAY

NEPTUNE, returning from the Ethiopians, sees the raft from a mountain. His face darkens.

(SOUND: wind changing, a sudden hollow in the air)

The sea gathers. Night falls from heaven. East, south, west, and north leap together. A tremendous wave crashes down. The raft spins. ULYSSES is thrown clear. He clings to a timber, strips the heavy clothes Calypso gave him, and spreads himself on the wreck.

A sea-nymph in the likeness of a cormorant gives him a veil and tells him to swim when the timber fails. Wave on wave. Two nights and two days. When Dawn of the third day comes, land shows at last: Phaeacian coast, high rocks, a running river mouth.

EXT. SCHERIA RIVER MOUTH - DAWN

ULYSSES prays to the river, and the current stills. He lets the veil go on the outgoing stream. He crawls from the water, his skin swollen, the sea gushing from his mouth and nostrils. He creeps under two olive trees, one wild, one grafted, grown from the same root, and heaps dead leaves over himself. He sleeps like the dead.

(SOUND: river over stones, his torn breathing, then sleep)

EXT. SCHERIA WASHING POOLS - DAY

NAUSICAA, young princess of the Phaeacians, sleeps in a pompous house. MINERVA stands over her in a dream, in the likeness of a girl friend, and bids her wash the linen, for her wedding day is not far off.

Dawn. NAUSICAA asks her father for the wagon. He does not refuse. Mules are yoked. Her mother gives her food, wine, and a golden cruse of oil. She and her maids drive to the river, wash the clothes in the pits, spread them on the beach, bathe, eat, and play at ball.

(SOUND: wet linen slapped on stone, girls laughing, a ball in air)

The ball falls in the river. The girls shriek. ULYSSES wakes, peers from the leaves, and comes forth, covering his nakedness with a leafy branch, like a weather-beaten lion. The maids flee. NAUSICAA stands her ground, for MINERVA puts courage in her heart.

ULYSSES
O queen, I throw myself on your mercy. Are you a goddess or a mortal woman? If you are a goddess, you are Jove's daughter Diana.

He keeps his distance.

ULYSSES (CONT'D)
Last of all, after much suffering, I have come to this your own country. Show me the way to your town, and let me have anything you may have brought here to wrap myself in.

NAUSICAA
Stranger, you seem to be a sensible, well-disposed person. There is no accounting for luck; Jove gives prosperity to rich and poor just as he chooses.

She calls the maids back.

NAUSICAA (CONT'D)
This man looks like a sensible person; give him something to eat and drink, and wash him in the stream at a place where it is sheltered from the wind.

They set him in the shelter, leave a shirt, cloak, and the golden cruse. He washes the sea off, anoints himself, and puts on the clothes. MINERVA makes him taller and stouter, and thickens his hair like hyacinth. The maids stare. NAUSICAA looks long.

NAUSICAA
Listen to me, my maids. I should like my future husband to be just such another as he is, if he would only stay here and not want to go away.

They give him food. When he has eaten, she tells him how to come to her father's house, and not to walk with her through the town, lest tongues wag.

NAUSICAA
When you have got past the ship yards you will see my father's grove of poplar trees. Sit there and wait till we have got into the town and reached the house.

She tells him to go through the hall to her mother Arete, and to clasp the queen's knees.

NAUSICAA (CONT'D)
If her heart is towards you, you may hope to see your friends again, and get safely back to your own country.

They drive home. ULYSSES follows. At the grove he sits and prays to MINERVA. She hears him, but will not show herself yet, for she fears her uncle Neptune.

INT. ALCINOUS PALACE - HALL - DAY

Walls of bronze, blue enamel dado, golden doors, silver posts, gold and silver dogs that will never grow old. Within, the chiefs of the Phaeacians sit pouring wine. NAUSICAA has gone to her chamber. ULYSSES, still a stranger, walks through the hall in a mist MINERVA sheds about him, and clasps the knees of the queen.

The mist falls. They stare. He is a suppliant at the hearth among the ashes.

(SOUND: a gasp along the benches, fire talking in the hearth)

An old councillor bids Alcinous raise the stranger and set him in a seat of honour. They feast him. When the others go, the king and queen remain. The queen looks at the clothes, which she herself had made, and asks who gave them.

ULYSSES tells of Calypso, the raft, the wreck, the river, and the princess at the washing pools. He names no name of his own. They promise him conveyance home. A bed is made in the portico. He sleeps.

EXT. PHAEACIAN GAMES GROUND - DAY

Next day an assembly. A ship is ordered for the stranger. Then games: running, wrestling, jumping, boxing, throwing the disc. Young Phaeacians shine. One taunts the still-nameless guest for looking like a merchant, not an athlete.

ULYSSES's brow darkens. He catches up a disc bigger than the rest and hurls it far beyond all marks. A shout. MINERVA, in a man's likeness, calls the throw.

(SOUND: disc thudding far out, crowd roar)

They soothe him with dance and song. A bard sings of the loves of Mars and Venus, then of the wooden horse and the sack of Troy. ULYSSES draws his purple cloak over his head and weeps. The king sees, and stops the song.

INT. ALCINOUS PALACE - HALL - NIGHT

A feast of farewell. Gifts: clothes, gold, a sword. The bard is led in again. ULYSSES cuts him a portion of meat and asks for the horse of wood. The bard sings. Again ULYSSES weeps.

The king speaks gently, and asks at last the question the whole house has been circling.

NARRATOR (V.O.)
Then Alcinous said, Tell me, and tell me true, the name by which your father and mother over yonder used to call you.

ULYSSES lowers the cloak. Firelight on the scarred, sea-worn face.

ULYSSES
First of all I will tell you my name, that you may know it, and my fame both here and in my own country.

He looks from face to face.

ULYSSES (CONT'D)
I am Ulysses son of Laertes, renowned among mankind for all manner of subtlety, so that my fame ascends to heaven.

A stillness.

ULYSSES (CONT'D)
I live in Ithaca, where there is a high mountain called Neritum, covered with forests; and I will now tell you of the many hazardous adventures which by Jove's will I met with on my way home.

INT. POLYPHEMUS CAVE - NIGHT

Cut into memory: a high cave, racks of cheeses, pens of lambs and kids, bowls of whey. ULYSSES and his picked men wait in the dark. A vast shadow fills the door. POLYPHEMUS, a giant Cyclops, one eye in his forehead, son of Neptune, drives in his flocks, heaves a slab against the mouth that twenty-two wagons could not shift, and milks his ewes.

(SOUND: the slab grinding home, sheep bells, a voice like rock)

He sees them.

POLYPHEMUS
Strangers, who are you? Where do sail from? Are you traders, or do you sail the sea as rovers?

ULYSSES
We are Achaeans, driven from our course by contrary winds on our way home from Troy. We venture to approach you and make our prayer as suppliants.

The giant answers with no prayer. He snatches up two men, dashes their brains out, and makes his meal of them, leaving neither flesh nor marrow. He lies down among his flocks and sleeps. ULYSSES's hand goes to his sword, then stops: if he kills the giant, they will never move the slab.

Dawn. The Cyclops eats two more, drives out the ewes, and sets the slab again. ULYSSES finds a club of green olive as big as a mast, cuts a fathom's length, sharpens and hardens it in the fire, and hides it. At evening he offers the giant strong wine they had brought from the ship.

ULYSSES
Cyclops, try this wine, now you have eaten your men's flesh, that you may see what kind of liquor we had on board my ship.

POLYPHEMUS drinks, drinks again, and grins.

POLYPHEMUS
Give me more, and tell me your name at once. I want to make you a present that you will be glad to have.

ULYSSES
My name is Noman; this is what my father and mother and my friends have always called me.

POLYPHEMUS
Then I will eat all Noman's comrades before Noman himself, and will keep Noman for the last. This is the present that I will make him.

He reels and vomits wine and human flesh, then sprawls on his back. ULYSSES heats the stake till it glows. Four men lift it. He drives it into the one eye and leans on it, twisting, as a shipwright bores a timber.

(SOUND: hiss of burning jelly, a scream that shakes the cave)

POLYPHEMUS wrenches the stake out, bellowing. Other Cyclopes gather outside.

POLYPHEMUS
What ails you, Polyphemus, that you make such a noise, breaking the stillness of the night, and preventing us from being able to sleep?

From within, half-blind, roaring:

POLYPHEMUS
Noman is killing me by fraud! Noman is killing me by force!

The others shrug and go. If no man is killing him, it is a sickness Jove has sent. In the morning the blinded giant opens the cave and sits in the door, feeling the backs of his sheep. ULYSSES lashes the rams in threes and ties a man under each middle beast. He himself clings under the great bellwether.

They pass. At the ship he cannot keep from shouting.

ULYSSES
Cyclops, you should have taken better measure of your man before eating up his comrades in your cave. Jove and the other gods have punished you.

POLYPHEMUS hurls a hill-peak. The wave drives the ship back, then out. ULYSSES shouts again, against all counsel.

ULYSSES
Cyclops, if any one asks you who it was that put your eye out and spoiled your beauty, say it was the valiant warrior Ulysses, son of Laertes, who lives in Ithaca.

POLYPHEMUS lifts his hands to the starry heaven.

POLYPHEMUS
Hear me, great Neptune, if I am indeed your own true-begotten son. Grant that Ulysses may never reach his home alive.

The sea heaves as if it heard. He hurls a second rock. The ship escapes toward the island where the rest of the squadron waits.

EXT. AEOLUS ISLAND - DAY

A floating isle of bronze wall and smooth cliff. AEOLUS, steward of the winds, feasts them a month. At parting he gives ULYSSES a bag of ox-hide with all contrary winds tied inside, and a west wind to take them home.

Nine days they sail. On the tenth they raise the fires of Ithaca. ULYSSES, exhausted, sleeps. His men whisper that the bag is gold, and open it.

(SOUND: a scream of wind, the bag ripping)

All the winds leap out. The ship is flung back, weeping, to the same bronze island. Aeolus drives them off as men hated by the gods.

EXT. LAESTRYGONIAN HARBOUR - DAY

A fair-seeming fjord. All the ships but ULYSSES's own enter. Giants come down. They spear men like fish and carry them home for dinner. Stones smash the trapped hulls. ULYSSES cuts his own cable and rows for open sea, alone now with one black ship and a broken crew.

(SOUND: timber bursting, men screaming, oars beating panic)

EXT. CIRCE PALACE GROUNDS - DAY

An island of oak and stone. Smoke from a house in a clearing. Stags and wolves that fawn like dogs. ULYSSES divides his last men. One party goes inland under Eurylochus and finds CIRCE, a beautiful dread goddess, singing at her loom.

She mixes cheese, honey, meal, and Pramnian wine, and drugs it. The men drink. She strikes them with her wand. They become pigs, with pigs' heads and bristles, but the minds of men. She shuts them in sties and throws them acorns.

(SOUND: her song, a wand-stroke, panicked squealing)

Eurylochus flees back. ULYSSES goes alone. On the way the god Mercury meets him in the likeness of a young man and gives him a black-rooted, milk-white flower, moly, against her drugs.

INT. CIRCE HALL - DAY

CIRCE, lovely and terrible, receives ULYSSES, mixes the cup, and strikes. He draws his sword. She shrieks, clasps his knees.

(SOUND: cup falling, her cry)

CIRCE
Who and whence are you? I am amazed at you. You have drunk my drugs and are not bewitched. Surely you must be the Ulysses of whom Mercury used to tell me.

She looks up at him.

CIRCE (CONT'D)
Sheathe your sword and let us go to bed, that we may make friends and learn to trust each other.

ULYSSES
Circe, how can you expect me to be friendly with you when you have turned my men into pigs? I will not go to bed with you unless you take a solemn oath that you mean me no mischief.

She swears. The handmaids prepare a bath. He will not eat. She restores the men: they become younger, taller, more handsome than before. They weep together. For a year they stay in her halls, feasting.

Then his men remind him of home. CIRCE no longer holds him.

CIRCE
Ulysses, noble son of Laertes, you shall no longer stay in my house against your will. But you must first complete another journey, and go to the house of Hades.

ULYSSES sits on the bed and weeps. His heart will not live.

CIRCE (CONT'D)
When you have got there, the prophet Teiresias will come to you. He will tell you about your voyage, and how you may get back to your own country over the teeming deep.

She tells him the rites: a trench, honey, wine, water, barley, the blood of a black ram and a black ewe, and the ghosts that will come.

EXT. SHORE OF OCEANUS - DAY

The ship runs to the limits of the world, to the land of the Cimmerians, wrapped in mist and cloud. They beach. ULYSSES digs the trench with his sword, pours the drink-offerings, cuts the victims. Dark blood runs. The souls of the dead come up out of Erebus, brides, youths, old men, girls with hearts new-torn, men killed in battle, armour still bloody.

(SOUND: a thin enormous wailing, blood dripping into earth)

He holds them off with his sword until TEIRESIAS comes, the Theban prophet, gold sceptre in hand. The prophet drinks, and speaks.

NARRATOR (V.O.)
Teiresias said that Neptune was angry for the blinding of his son, yet Ulysses might still reach home if he and his men left the cattle of the Sun-god unharmed.

The unseen prophet's words ride the steam of the blood.

NARRATOR (V.O.)
If they harmed them, his ship and men would perish, and he would come home late, in evil case, upon a foreign ship, to find trouble in his house.

ULYSSES's mother comes, Anticlea, whom he left alive. He weeps and would hold her. Three times he springs toward her, three times she is as shadow or a dream. She tells him Penelope still waits, and Telemachus holds his lands, and Laertes lives in grief upon the farm. She died of longing for him.

The wives and daughters of heroes come. Then Agamemnon, still torn by the manner of his death, warns him about women and about coming home by stealth. Achilles asks of his son and will not be comforted for death, saying he would rather be a hireling on earth than king among the dead. Ajax turns away, still angry for the armour.

The innumerable dead press in with a terrible cry. Pale fear takes ULYSSES. He and his men flee to the ship, and the stream of Oceanus bears them back to Circe's isle.

EXT. SIREN SEA - DAY

CIRCE sends them on with more counsel. They pass the Sirens' island. ULYSSES stops his men's ears with beeswax and has himself lashed to the mast.

(SOUND: a singing so sweet the sea itself seems to lean)

He nods and begs to be set free. The men bind him tighter and row on until the song dies astern.

EXT. SCYLLA STRAIT - DAY

On one side the Wanderers, rocks that smash even doves. On the other, a fig tree over a whirlpool, Charybdis, who sucks down the black water and vomits it again. Opposite, a cavern: SCYLLA, twelve feet, six long necks, three rows of teeth.

ULYSSES arms and stands in the bows, forgetting Circe's word that fighting will not help. They watch Charybdis. From the cave six heads dart. Six men, the strongest, are lifted writhing, calling to him by name, and are eaten at the mouths of the cave.

(SOUND: water roaring down, then the shrieks of the six)

He sees their hands and feet still moving as they are drawn up. It is the most pitiful sight of all his voyaging.

EXT. THRINACIA SHORE - DAY

The island of the Sun. Cattle and sheep of the god Hyperion, unharmed, immortal, herded by nymphs. ULYSSES would pass by. His men, exhausted, force a landing. He makes them swear not to touch the herds.

A south wind binds them a month. Meal and wine fail. They fish and hunt and starve. ULYSSES goes inland to pray and falls asleep. Eurylochus urges the others. They kill the best of the cattle, wrap the thigh bones, and make burnt offering with water for wine.

(SOUND: cattle lowing as they die, fat in fire)

ULYSSES wakes and smells the roast. He cries out to the immortals. The hides crawl. The meat on the spits bellows. For six days they feast. On the seventh the wind falls. They put to sea.

Jove sends a dark cloud. The ship shivers in the wind. A thunderbolt strikes the deck. Men are washed over like sea-crows. ULYSSES lashes keel to mast and rides the wreck. Charybdis sucks it down; he clings to the fig tree until the timbers come up again. Nine days more. On the tenth night the gods bring him to Ogygia, to Calypso's cave, where this telling began.

INT. ALCINOUS PALACE - HALL - NIGHT

The fire is lower. The Phaeacians sit still. ULYSSES's voice is hoarse.

ULYSSES
And now I have told you all. There is no need to linger over the tale of my voyage from Calypso's island to your own, for I told it you yesterday.

They heap more gifts. At dawn a ship waits, a picked crew. They lay him on the deck with rugs and linen. He sleeps a sleep so deep it is almost death, while the Phaeacian oarsmen drive the ship like a four-horse team.

EXT. ITHACA COVE - DAWN

A small harbour with two headlands, an olive at the head, a cave of the nymphs with stone looms and stone jars, two mouths, one for men, one for gods. The crew lift the sleeping man, set him on the sand with his gifts beside him, and put to sea.

(SOUND: keel on pebbles, then oars receding)

Neptune turns their ship to stone within sight of their own city, and roots it in the sea. ULYSSES wakes and does not know his own land. Mist over everything. He thinks the Phaeacians have betrayed him. He counts his gifts and finds them whole, and walks groaning by the sea.

MINERVA comes in the likeness of a young shepherd, fine-spoken.

MINERVA
You are simple, stranger, or you must come from a long way off, if you ask what this country is. It is rugged, not fit for horses, but it is not so very poor.

She names the island.

MINERVA (CONT'D)
The name of this country, stranger, is Ithaca, and it is known even as far as Troy, which I understand is a long way off.

ULYSSES's heart leaps, but he will not say so. He invents a tale of Crete, of murder, of Phoenician sailors. MINERVA smiles and takes the form of a tall, handsome woman, skilled in needlework.

MINERVA
He must be indeed a shifty lying fellow who could surpass you in all manner of craft, even if you had a god for your antagonist. Dare-devil, fox-cunning, never tired of fraud.

She touches him, half scolding, half proud.

MINERVA (CONT'D)
Nay, you did not know me, Pallas Minerva, daughter of Jove, who am always with you in all your troubles. But let us say no more of this. I have come here to help you both and your son.

She scatters the mist. He knows the land: the harbour, the cave, the trees. He kisses the earth. Together they hide the gifts in the cave of the nymphs. Then they sit at the foot of the olive and plan the death of the suitors.

MINERVA
I will begin by disguising you, so that no human being shall know you. I will cover your body with wrinkles, take the bright hair from your head, and clothe you in rags.

She strikes him with her wand. His flesh withers. His hair goes. His eyes dull. She puts a vile wrap about him, a tattered wallet, and a staff. She sends him to the swineherd, and herself to Sparta, to fetch TELEMACHUS home.

EXT. EUMAEUS STEADING - DAY

High in the woods, a yard of quarried stone, a palisade of split oak, twelve sties, six hundred sows, the boars apart. EUMAEUS, loyal swineherd, older, weathered, sits cutting out a pair of sandals. Four dogs, fierce as wild beasts, fly at the beggar. EUMAEUS runs, shouts, drives them off with stones, and leads the stranger in.

(SOUND: dogs snarling, then a fire of brushwood)

INT. EUMAEUS HUT - NIGHT

He sets ULYSSES down, strews brushwood, throws over it the shaggy wild-goat skin that is his own bed. He kills a young pig, roasts it, and sets it before the beggar with wine.

EUMAEUS
Eat, stranger, and make the best of what is here. The suitors eat the fat hogs, and care nothing for the eye of heaven, nor for pity.

ULYSSES
My friend, you are a man of sense and feeling. May Jove and the other gods grant you your heart's desire for the kind way in which you have treated me.

EUMAEUS
Stranger, though a still poorer man should come here, it would not be right for me to insult him. All strangers and beggars are from Jove.

He speaks of his absent master, of Penelope, of Telemachus. ULYSSES swears the master will return this very moon. EUMAEUS will not believe, though he loves the man who is sitting at his fire.

They talk long. EUMAEUS tells how he was a king's son, stolen by Phoenicians, bought by Laertes. ULYSSES invents another Cretan tale, with a cloak in it, so that the swineherd shall give him a wrap for the night. EUMAEUS makes him a bed and sleeps outside with the swine, faithful even in the dark.

EXT. EUMAEUS STEADING - DAY

Days pass. MINERVA goes to Lacedaemon, stands over TELEMACHUS as he lies beside Nestor's son, and bids him haste home, for the suitors lie in wait in the strait. She tells him to send the ship on and go himself first to the swineherd.

TELEMACHUS takes leave of MENELAUS and Helen. Gifts are brought. An eagle flies to the right with a white goose from the yard. Helen reads the omen: Ulysses shall come home and take vengeance.

The ship runs by night. Off Ithaca TELEMACHUS lands at the first point, sends the crew to town, and walks inland toward the steading, as the goddess told him.

INT. EUMAEUS HUT - DAY

The dogs fawn and do not bark. TELEMACHUS stands in the doorway. EUMAEUS lets fall the cups and kisses the young man's head, eyes, and hands, as a father kisses a son returned from far away.

(SOUND: the dogs' soft whine, EUMAEUS weeping)

EUMAEUS
So you are come, Telemachus, light of my eyes that you are. I made sure I was never going to see you any more. Come in, my dear child, and sit down.

TELEMACHUS
I will tell you truly. I have come from Pylos and have brought my ship to Ithaca. I have come here to see you first, and to hear whether my mother is still in the house.

He sees the beggar.

TELEMACHUS (CONT'D)
Father, who is this stranger? How did you come by him? Did some ship bring him, or did he come here on foot?

EUMAEUS tells what he knows. TELEMACHUS will not take the stranger into the press of the suitors, but he will send clothes and food. He sends EUMAEUS to town to tell Penelope he is safe.

When the swineherd is gone, MINERVA stands in the doorway, visible only to ULYSSES and the dogs, and signs to him. She touches him with her golden wand. The rags fall from his presence. He is taller, younger, the dark beard restored.

TELEMACHUS stares and drops his eyes, afraid it is a god.

TELEMACHUS
Stranger, you are no longer like what you were when I first saw you. You are changed. You are now like some one of the gods who live in heaven.

ULYSSES
I am no god. Why take me for one? I am your father, on whose account you grieve and suffer so much at the hands of lawless men.

He kisses his son and lets fall a tear. TELEMACHUS cannot yet believe. ULYSSES tells him it is Minerva's doing. Then TELEMACHUS throws his arms about his father, and both cry aloud, more loudly than birds of prey whose young have been taken.

When they can speak, they plan. The armour must be taken from the hall. The women must be tested. The suitors will be in the house. Two men and a goddess against a hundred.

MINERVA touches ULYSSES again. The beggar's wrinkles and rags return before EUMAEUS comes back.

EXT. ROAD TO TOWN - DAY

Dawn. TELEMACHUS goes first to the house. ULYSSES and EUMAEUS follow. At the fountain they meet the goatherd Melanthius, who insults the beggar and kicks him on the hip. ULYSSES considers killing him, and does not. They come to the town. A dog lifts his head from a dungheap: ARGOS, whom Ulysses bred, neglected now, full of ticks.

The old dog wags his tail and drops his ears. He has no strength to come nearer. ULYSSES wipes away a tear, secretly. When they have passed, ARGOS dies.

(SOUND: a faint tail in dust, then stillness)

INT. ULYSSES HOUSE - GREAT HALL - DAY

TELEMACHUS is already among the suitors. PENELOPE has come down and kissed her son, asking what he heard. He tells her to bathe and pray, and that he will speak later.

The beggar ULYSSES enters his own hall and sits on the ashen threshold. TELEMACHUS sends him bread and meat. He goes among the suitors asking alms, to know them. ANTINOUS rebukes the swineherd for bringing another mouth.

ANTINOUS
What demon has brought this plague to trouble us? Keep your distance, or you shall be haled to Egypt or to Cyprus for a slave.

ULYSSES answers mildly, then more sharply. ANTINOUS seizes a footstool and strikes him on the right shoulder. ULYSSES stands like a rock, says nothing, and goes back to the threshold.

(SOUND: wood on bone, a hush, then the suitors' uneasy laugh)

PENELOPE in her chamber hears of the blow and prays. She sends for the swineherd: she would speak with the stranger, for he may have news of Ulysses.

A public beggar, Irus, huge and lazy, tries to drive ULYSSES from his own door. The suitors egg them on. ULYSSES binds his rags and shows a thigh of unexpected muscle. He drops Irus with a single blow that crushes the bone. The suitors hold their sides laughing. They give the winner a haggis. ANTINOUS still smiles with no kindness in it.

Night comes on. The women of the house go to and fro. Some of them, faithless, laugh with the suitors. ULYSSES tells the maids to go to Penelope. One of them, Melantho, whom Penelope raised as her own, taunts him. He threatens. TELEMACHUS, stronger now, orders the suitors home. They are amazed, and go, each to his own house.

INT. ULYSSES HOUSE - GREAT HALL - NIGHT

Father and son take the armour from the walls by the light of MINERVA, who holds a golden lamp. The hall shines as if with fire. TELEMACHUS stares.

(SOUND: bronze lifted from pegs, a soft immortal hum)

TELEMACHUS
Father, I see a great marvel. The walls and the beams and the pillars are all glowing as with fire. This must be the work of a god.

ULYSSES
Hush, and keep your thoughts to yourself. This is the manner of the gods who live in heaven. Go now to bed. I will stay here and provoke the maids and your mother.

TELEMACHUS goes to his tower room. EURYCLEA lights him as before.

INT. ULYSSES HOUSE - UPPER CHAMBER - NIGHT

PENELOPE comes down again, like Diana or Venus, and sits by the fire. Her maids clear the feast. She has the stranger brought to her. A chair is set. She asks who he is.

(SOUND: fire, the late house, her voice held steady)

PENELOPE
Friend, let me ask you first of all, who and whence are you? Tell me of your town and parents.

ULYSSES
Do not ask me. If you do, you will only give me more pain. I have more trouble than any other man living.

PENELOPE
Stranger, heaven robbed me of all beauty the day my husband sailed for Troy. If he were to return, my fame would be greater, and I should give you more honour.

She tells him of the web, of the suitors, of the pressure to marry. He tells her a long Cretan tale, and that he once entertained Ulysses. He describes the mantle and the golden brooch of a hound killing a fawn, and a herald with rounded shoulders.

PENELOPE weeps as she hears, as snow melts on a mountain. She believes the stranger knew her husband. He swears Ulysses is near, in Thesprotia, alive, and will be here this very moon.

PENELOPE
May Jove so grant it. I will have you washed and given a good bed. In the morning you shall sit with Telemachus and dine.

She calls EURYCLEA to wash the stranger's feet.

PENELOPE (CONT'D)
Come, wash the feet of this man, who is of an age with your master. Ulysses must by this time have just such hands and feet as he has, for men grow old quickly in misfortune.

EURYCLEA brings the basin, looks at the beggar, and her eyes fill.

EURYCLEA
My dear child, I cannot think of any one who has suffered as you have. Many strangers have come here, but I say that I have never seen one so like Ulysses as you are.

ULYSSES
Old woman, that is what every one says who has seen us both.

She washes him. He turns from the fire, toward the dark, fearing she will know the scar. She comes to the old wound: the boar's tusk on Parnassus, when he hunted with Autolycus. She knows it at once. The basin falls. His foot strikes the cup. Water spills.

(SOUND: bronze basin ringing on stone, water spreading)

EURYCLEA
It is Ulysses. I did not know you till I had handled the very feet of my master.

She looks toward PENELOPE. ULYSSES's hand is on the old woman's throat, pulling her close.

ULYSSES
Nurse, do you wish to be the ruin of me, you who nursed me at your own breast? Be silent, and keep it to yourself.

EURYCLEA
My child, what are you talking about? You know me too well. I will keep as close as stone or iron.

He lets her finish the washing in a fresh basin. PENELOPE, whom MINERVA has turned aside, has seen nothing. She tells the stranger a dream of an eagle killing her twenty geese, and the eagle saying he was her husband. He reads it as she hopes. Still she will set the contest of the axes for the next day, and marry whoever strings Ulysses' bow and shoots through the twelve.

ULYSSES
Wife of Ulysses, do not put off this contest, for Ulysses will return before ever they can string the bow and shoot through the iron.

She goes up to her room and weeps for her husband until MINERVA sheds sleep upon her eyelids.

INT. ULYSSES HOUSE - GREAT HALL - NIGHT

ULYSSES lies in the portico. The faithless women go out laughing to the suitors' beds. He tosses like a man turning a paunch of blood and fat before a fire, thinking how, single-handed, he shall kill so many. MINERVA comes and stands over him, rebuking his unbelief. He sleeps at last.

Toward dawn he hears PENELOPE weeping. He prays for a sign. Jove thunders from a clear sky. A woman grinding at the mill, the weakest, leftover at her task, takes it for a sign and prays that this may be the suitors' last day. ULYSSES is glad.

(SOUND: thunder in a clear night, the grindstone, her bitter prayer)

INT. ULYSSES HOUSE - GREAT HALL - DAY

The suitors come again, drive the sheep and goats in, mix wine, kindle fires. TELEMACHUS sets a humble table for the beggar inside the hall, and warns them not to lay hands on him. They cannot keep from insult. One throws an ox-foot. TELEMACHUS speaks out more boldly than they have ever heard.

PENELOPE goes to the store-room, to the treasure of her lord. She takes from its peg the great back-bent bow and the quiver, and a chest of bronze and iron for the axes. She weeps as she takes them. Then she comes down to the suitors, veiled, a maid on either side, and stands by the bearing-post.

(SOUND: the hall falling quiet, the bow unwrapped)

PENELOPE
Listen to me, you suitors. You have been feasting in this house to win me for your wife. I bring you the bow of divine Ulysses.

She holds it so they all may see.

PENELOPE (CONT'D)
The man who strings this bow most easily, and sends an arrow through each of the twelve axes, him will I follow, and quit this house of my wedded life, so beautiful, so filled with wealth.

TELEMACHUS sets up the axes in a straight line, all in the ground, one trench, stamped firm. He tries the bow himself, three times, and would have strung it on the fourth, but ULYSSES signs to him. He sets it down.

The suitors try, in order, after they have warmed and greased the bow. They fail. ANTINOUS would leave it till tomorrow and pour a libation. The beggar asks to try.

ANTINOUS
Wretched beggar, you have no sense. Are you not content to eat in peace? The wine has got into your head.

PENELOPE
Antinous, it is not right that you should ill-use any guest of Telemachus who comes to this house. If the stranger strings the bow, do you suppose I shall leave my son and marry him?

TELEMACHUS
Mother, no one has a better right than I to give or to refuse the bow. Go to your room and see to your own work, the loom and the distaff.

She goes, wondering, and weeps upstairs until sleep takes her. EUMAEUS, weeping, takes up the bow. The suitors shout at him. TELEMACHUS threatens. The swineherd puts the bow into the beggar's hands.

EURYCLEA is told to bar the doors of the women's rooms. The stockman bars the outer gate.

ULYSSES turns the bow every way, fearing worms have eaten the horn. The suitors mock. He strings it as a skilled bard strings a lyre, without effort. He plucks the string. It sings like a swallow.

(SOUND: the bowstring's clear note, then a sudden thunder from Jove)

He takes an arrow that lies by, lays it on the bridge, and from his seat sends it clean through every axe-ring from the first onward. He says to TELEMACHUS:

ULYSSES
Telemachus, the stranger who sits in your hall has not disgraced you. The hour has come to get supper ready for the Achaeans, while it is daylight, and then we will dance and be merry.

TELEMACHUS girds on his sword, takes his spear, and stands by his father's seat, armed with gleaming bronze.

ULYSSES strips his rags. He leaps onto the threshold, pours the arrows out at his feet, and looks along the hall.

ULYSSES
Dogs, did you think that I should not come back from Troy? You have wasted my substance, have forced my women servants to lie with you, and have wooed my wife while I was still living.

He nocks.

ULYSSES (CONT'D)
You have feared neither the gods nor the indignation of men hereafter. The hand of death is now upon you.

He shoots ANTINOUS through the throat as the young man lifts a two-handled cup. The shaft goes clean through. Blood bursts from his nostrils. He kicks the table from him and knocks the food to the floor. The cup falls from his hand.

(SOUND: the bow's snap, the cup ringing, benches going over)

The suitors spring up, looking to the walls for armour that is not there. They think it an accident. ULYSSES tells them they are wrong.

ULYSSES
You dogs, you never thought I would come back. Now you shall pay for all.

EURYMACHUS tries to bargain, to lay the blame on dead Antinous, to repay the cattle. ULYSSES will take no ransom.

They rush him. TELEMACHUS kills one. He runs to the armoury and brings shields, spears, helmets for his father, for EUMAEUS, for the faithful stockman. Melanthius the goatherd slips out to fetch armour for the suitors. They catch him on the second trip, bind him, and hoist him to a beam to suffer.

MINERVA appears as Mentor, then as a swallow in the roof-beam. Spears miss ULYSSES by her will. The suitors fall in heaps. The floor runs with blood. Groans fill the cloisters.

(SOUND: bronze on bone, men slipping in blood, the swallow's cry)

When it is done, they lie in the blood and dust like fishes hauled onto a beach. PHEMIUS the bard and MEDON the herald are spared at TELEMACHUS's word; they creep to the altar of Jove in the outer court.

ULYSSES calls EURYCLEA. She would raise the cry of triumph. He stops her.

ULYSSES
Old woman, rejoice in silence; restrain yourself, and do not make any noise about it. It is an unholy thing to vaunt over dead men.

He asks which of the women have been faithless. She names twelve of fifty. TELEMACHUS, EUMAEUS, and the stockman make the twelve clean the gore, then take them out and hang them in a row from a ship's cable, their feet twitching a little, not for long. They take Melanthius into the courtyard and cut off his nose, ears, hands, and feet, and throw his body to the dogs.

Fire and sulphur cleanse the hall. EURYCLEA fetches the faithful women. They crowd ULYSSES, kissing his head and shoulders, and a longing comes over him to weep, for he knows them all.

INT. ULYSSES HOUSE - UPPER CHAMBER - NIGHT

EURYCLEA goes up, trembling with joy, to wake PENELOPE.

EURYCLEA
Wake up, Penelope, my dear child, and see with your own eyes something that you have been wanting this long time past. Ulysses is in the house, and has killed the suitors.

PENELOPE
My good nurse, the gods have made you foolish. Why do you wake me out of a sleep that had taken possession of my eyes? If any other of the maids had told me this, I should have had her well punished.

EURYCLEA
I am not laughing at you, dear child. It is quite true. The stranger whom they insulted in the cloister is Ulysses. Telemachus knew, but kept his father's secret.

PENELOPE comes down, doubtful still. She sits opposite ULYSSES in the light of the fire, and is silent, looking at him. Sometimes she thinks she knows him, sometimes the rags and the misery defeat her. TELEMACHUS rebukes her hardness.

PENELOPE
My son, I am stupefied. I cannot speak, nor ask him anything, nor look him in the face. If he is indeed Ulysses, we shall know one another better than others can, for there are tokens which we two alone know.

ULYSSES smiles.

ULYSSES
Let your mother test me. She is cold because I am filthy and in rags. Let us consider how it will be when the town hears of the killing.

He bathes. MINERVA sheds beauty over him again, taller, stouter, hair like hyacinth. He comes back in a clean tunic, like an immortal. PENELOPE still holds off.

ULYSSES
Madam, the gods have given you a harder heart than any other woman. No other wife could bear to keep her husband at arm's length when he had come back after twenty years of toil.

PENELOPE
Stranger, I am not proud or indifferent. I know what you were like when you left Ithaca. Euryclea, take his bed outside the room that he himself built.

ULYSSES
Wife, I am much displeased at what you have just been saying. Who has been taking my bed from the place in which I left it?

Anger lights him. This is the token.

ULYSSES (CONT'D)
It was I who made it, and no other man. There was a young olive growing in the inner court. I built the bedpost of it, inlaid it with gold, silver, and ivory, and stretched ox-hide thongs dyed purple.

PENELOPE's knees fail. Her heart melts. She runs to him, throws her arms about his neck, and kisses his head.

PENELOPE
Do not be angry with me, Ulysses. I have been shuddering all the time through fear that someone might come and deceive me. Now you have told me the secret of our bed, which no one ever saw but you and I and one maid.

He weeps as he holds the wife he has won. She clings as a swimmer clings to land after the sea. Dawn would have risen on their tears, but MINERVA holds the night long in the west, and stays the gold-throned Dawn at Oceanus.

They go at last to the bed the living olive still anchors. He tells her, in the dark, of Calypso, of the Phaeacians, of the wanderings, until a deep sleep takes him. Then MINERVA lets Dawn rise.

EXT. MEADOW OF ASPHODEL - NIGHT

Meanwhile MERCURY leads the ghosts of the suitors down, gibbering like bats in a cave. They pass Oceanus, the rock Leucas, the gates of the sun, to the meadow of asphodel.

(SOUND: a thin squealing of souls, as of bats in a hollow)

The ghost of ACHILLES and the ghost of AGAMEMNON stand among the famous dead. The suitors' ghosts tell how the beggar strung the bow and how the floor seethed with their blood. AGAMEMNON's ghost blesses Ulysses for such a wife, and curses the memory of Clytemnestra.

EXT. LAERTES ORCHARD - DAY

ULYSSES, TELEMACHUS, EUMAEUS, and the stockman leave the town in a darkness MINERVA sheds, and come to the well-tilled farm of LAERTES, reclaimed with infinite labour. A lean-to for slaves. An old Sicel woman within.

ULYSSES sends the others to kill a pig for dinner. He finds his father alone in the great vineyard, hoeing. LAERTES is very old, a dirty patched shirt, oxhide thongs on his legs, leather sleeves, a goatskin cap, looking very woe-begone.

(SOUND: hoe in earth, bees in the vines, a distant pig-squeal)

ULYSSES stands under a tall pear and weeps. Then he goes forward, crafty still, and praises the garden, and asks whose bondman this old man is, and whether this is Ithaca, and speaks of a guest he once entertained: Ulysses, son of Laertes.

LAERTES sheds tears. He fills both hands with dust and pours it over his grey head. ULYSSES's nostrils quiver. He springs forward, flings his arms about him, and kisses him.

ULYSSES
I am he, father, about whom you are asking—I have returned after having been away for twenty years. But cease your sighing. I have been killing the suitors in my house.

LAERTES
If you really are my son Ulysses, and have come back again, you must give me such manifest proof of your identity as shall convince me.

ULYSSES
First observe this scar, which I got from a boar's tusk when I was hunting on Mt. Parnassus.

He points along the rows.

ULYSSES (CONT'D)
Furthermore I will point out to you the trees which you gave me. You gave me thirteen pear trees, ten apple trees, and forty fig trees; you also said you would give me fifty rows of vines.

LAERTES's strength fails. He would swoon if his son did not hold him. When he comes to himself he lifts his hands.

LAERTES
O father Jove, then you gods are still in Olympus after all, if the suitors have really been punished for their insolence and folly.

They go to the house. The old woman washes LAERTES and anoints him. MINERVA makes him taller and stouter. They sit to meat. DOLIUS and his sons come in from the thorn-fence and stand astonished. ULYSSES bids them sit and eat. They kiss his hands.

EXT. ITHACA FIELD BEFORE LAERTES HOUSE - DAY

Rumour runs through the town. The people take up their dead. In the place of assembly, Eupeithes, father of Antinous, calls for vengeance. Some listen to wiser counsel and go home. The rest arm and follow Eupeithes toward the farm.

(SOUND: a town in grief, then bronze, then feet on the road)

MINERVA asks JOVE what he will do. JOVE answers that they shall swear a solemn covenant: Ulysses shall rule, and the others shall forgive the massacre of their sons and brothers. Peace and plenty shall reign.

She darts down from Olympus.

At the farm a son of Dolius sees the armed men close. They arm: ULYSSES, TELEMACHUS, the two herdsmen, Dolius and his six sons, LAERTES and Dolius grey-haired but armed. They sally. MINERVA comes in the form and voice of Mentor.

ULYSSES
Telemachus, now that you are about to fight in an engagement which will show every man's mettle, be sure not to disgrace your ancestors.

TELEMACHUS
You say truly, my dear father, and you shall see, if you will, that I am in no mind to disgrace your family.

LAERTES
Good heavens, what a day I am enjoying. My son and grandson are vying with one another in the matter of valour.

MINERVA comes close to the old man.

MINERVA
Son of Arceisius—best friend I have in the world—pray to the blue-eyed damsel, and to Jove her father; then poise your spear and hurl it.

She infuses vigour. LAERTES prays, poises, and hurls. The spear goes through Eupeithes' helmet. He falls, armour rattling. ULYSSES and TELEMACHUS fall on the front line.

They would kill them all, but MINERVA raises her voice.

MINERVA
Men of Ithaca, cease this dreadful war, and settle the matter at once without further bloodshed.

Pale fear seizes them. Their arms drop. They flee toward the city. ULYSSES gives a great cry and swoops after them like an eagle. JOVE sends a thunderbolt that falls just in front of MINERVA.

(SOUND: thunderbolt, bronze hitting earth, the run for the city)

MINERVA
Ulysses, noble son of Laertes, stop this warful strife, or Jove will be angry with you.

He obeys her gladly. She takes again the form and voice of Mentor, and makes a covenant of peace between the two contending parties.

EXT. GATEWAY OF ULYSSES HOUSE - EVENING

The same stone gateway. Evening light on the olives. Smoke rises now from the chimneys of the house, the smoke a man might long to see from the middle of the sea. Within, a man and a woman sit where the blood has been washed away. The night MINERVA lengthened is over. The island holds its peace.

(SOUND: a single night-bird, the hush after rain)

NARRATOR (V.O.)
So Minerva, daughter of Jove, made peace between them, and Ulysses came to his own again.

> FADE OUT.

THE END

