INT. TELEMACHUS BEDROOM - DAWN

Rosy light climbs the tower wall. TELEMACHUS, young, dark-haired, wonderfully like his father about the head and eyes, rises from the woollen fleece. He binds sandals onto his comely feet and girds his sword about his shoulder.

(SOUND: a cock crowing in the outer court, leather pulled taut)

NARRATOR (V.O.)
Now when the child of morning, rosy-fingered Dawn, appeared, Telemachus rose and dressed himself.

He leaves the room looking like an immortal god. Below, criers start through the lanes.

EXT. ITHACA ASSEMBLY PLACE - DAY

An open meeting-ground above the harbour, worn stone and packed earth. The people of Ithaca gather. TELEMACHUS comes spear in hand, not alone: his two hounds trot at his heel. MINERVA has endowed him with a presence of such divine comeliness that all marvel as he goes by.

(SOUND: criers calling, a crowd settling, hounds panting)

Even the oldest councillors make way when he takes his place in his father's seat.

A man bent double with age, of infinite experience, is the first to rise. His face is still wet for a son who sailed with Ulysses and never came home. He asks who has convened them, and whether a host is approaching.

TELEMACHUS takes the speech as of good omen and rises at once, bursting with what he has to say. He stands in the middle of the assembly. A good herald brings him a staff.

TELEMACHUS
Sir, it is I, as you will shortly learn, who have convened you, for it is I who am the most aggrieved.

He turns the staff in his hands.

TELEMACHUS (CONT'D)
I have not got wind of any host approaching about which I would warn you, nor is there any matter of public moment on which I would speak.

Faces wait.

TELEMACHUS (CONT'D)
My grievance is purely personal, and turns on two great misfortunes which have fallen upon my house.

He looks from the old men to the young.

TELEMACHUS (CONT'D)
The first of these is the loss of my excellent father, who was chief among all you here present, and was like a father to every one of you.

A murmur.

TELEMACHUS (CONT'D)
The second is much more serious, and ere long will be the utter ruin of my estate. The sons of all the chief men among you are pestering my mother to marry them against her will.

He steps nearer the front ranks.

TELEMACHUS (CONT'D)
They are afraid to go to her father Icarius, asking him to choose the one he likes best, and to provide marriage gifts for his daughter.

(SOUND: a restless scrape of sandals, someone spitting in the dust)

TELEMACHUS (CONT'D)
But day by day they keep hanging about my father's house, sacrificing our oxen, sheep, and fat goats for their banquets, and never giving so much as a thought to the wine they drink.

He swallows.

TELEMACHUS (CONT'D)
No estate can stand such recklessness; we have now no Ulysses to ward off harm from our doors, and I cannot hold my own against them.

The staff shakes in his grip.

TELEMACHUS (CONT'D)
I shall never all my days be as good a man as he was, still I would indeed defend myself if I had power to do so, for I cannot stand such treatment any longer.

He lifts his voice.

TELEMACHUS (CONT'D)
Have respect, therefore, to your own consciences and to public opinion. Fear, too, the wrath of heaven, lest the gods should be displeased and turn upon you.

He looks them full in the face.

TELEMACHUS (CONT'D)
I pray you by Jove and Themis, who is the beginning and the end of councils, do not hold back, my friends, and leave me singlehanded.

A long breath.

TELEMACHUS (CONT'D)
Unless it be that my brave father Ulysses did some wrong to the Achaeans which you would now avenge on me, by aiding and abetting these suitors.

He dashes the staff to the ground and bursts into tears.

(SOUND: wood cracking on stone, then a thick hush)

Every one is very sorry for him. They all sit still. No one ventures an angry answer save only ANTINOUS, handsome, insolent, oiled hair, a cruel mouth, who rises sleek among the young chiefs.

ANTINOUS
Telemachus, insolent braggart that you are, how dare you try to throw the blame upon us suitors?

He smiles without kindness.

ANTINOUS (CONT'D)
It is your mother's fault not ours, for she is a very artful woman. This three years past, and close on four, she had been driving us out of our minds.

He paces before them.

ANTINOUS (CONT'D)
By encouraging each one of us, and sending him messages without meaning one word of what she says. And then there was that other trick she played us.

The suitors lean in. The older men go still.

ANTINOUS (CONT'D)
She set up a great tambour frame in her room, and began to work on an enormous piece of fine needlework.

He mimics a softer voice, cruelly exact.

ANTINOUS (CONT'D)
Sweethearts, said she, Ulysses is indeed dead, still do not press me to marry again immediately, wait.

A laugh from the young benches.

ANTINOUS (CONT'D)
For I would not have skill in needlework perish unrecorded, till I have completed a pall for the hero Laertes, against the time when death shall take him.

ANTINOUS (CONT'D)
He is very rich, and the women of the place will talk if he is laid out without a pall. This was what she said, and we assented.

He spreads his hands.

ANTINOUS (CONT'D)
We could see her working on her great web all day long, but at night she would unpick the stitches again by torchlight.

(SOUND: a low ugly laugh along the suitors' bench)

ANTINOUS (CONT'D)
She fooled us in this way for three years and we never found her out, but as time wore on and she was now in her fourth year, one of her maids told us.

He lets that land.

ANTINOUS (CONT'D)
We caught her in the act of undoing her work, so she had to finish it whether she would or no.

He turns so the whole assembly hears the terms.

ANTINOUS (CONT'D)
The suitors therefore make you this answer, that both you and the Achaeans may understand. Send your mother away, and bid her marry the man of her own and of her father's choice.

ANTINOUS (CONT'D)
I do not know what will happen if she goes on plaguing us much longer with the airs she gives herself on the score of the accomplishments Minerva has taught her.

He glances at the silent townsmen.

ANTINOUS (CONT'D)
We never yet heard of such a woman; we know all about Tyro, Alcmena, Mycene, and the famous women of old, but they were nothing to your mother.

ANTINOUS (CONT'D)
As long as she continues in the mind with which heaven has now endowed her, so long shall we go on eating up your estate; and I do not see why she should change.

He points at TELEMACHUS.

ANTINOUS (CONT'D)
She gets all the honour and glory, and it is you who pay for it, not she. Understand, then, that we will not go back to our lands, neither here nor elsewhere.

ANTINOUS (CONT'D)
Till she has made her choice and married some one or other of us.

TELEMACHUS
Antinous, how can I drive the mother who bore me from my father's house? My father is abroad and we do not know whether he is alive or dead.

He holds himself upright though the tears have not dried.

TELEMACHUS (CONT'D)
It will be hard on me if I have to pay Icarius the large sum which I must give him if I insist on sending his daughter back to him.

TELEMACHUS (CONT'D)
Not only will he deal rigorously with me, but heaven will also punish me; for my mother when she leaves the house will call on the Erinyes to avenge her.

TELEMACHUS (CONT'D)
Besides, it would not be a creditable thing to do, and I will have nothing to say to it.

He looks past ANTINOUS to the rest.

TELEMACHUS (CONT'D)
If you choose to take offence at this, leave the house and feast elsewhere at one another's houses at your own cost turn and turn about.

TELEMACHUS (CONT'D)
If, on the other hand, you elect to persist in spunging upon one man, heaven help me, but Jove shall reckon with you in full.

TELEMACHUS (CONT'D)
And when you fall in my father's house there shall be no man to avenge you.

As he speaks, JOVE sends two eagles from the top of the mountain. They fly on and on with the wind, sailing side by side in their own lordly flight.

(SOUND: heavy wingbeats crossing the sun)

When they are right over the middle of the assembly they wheel and circle, beating the air, glaring death into the eyes of them that are below. Then, fighting fiercely and tearing at one another, they fly off towards the right over the town.

(SOUND: a raw cry from the crowd, feathers ripped on the wind)

The people wonder and ask each other what all this might be. The best prophet and reader of omens among them stands and speaks plainly, more particularly to the suitors.

NARRATOR (V.O.)
Hear me, men of Ithaca. I see mischief brewing for them. Ulysses is not going to be away much longer; indeed he is close at hand.

The old prophet's mouth moves. The suitors shift.

NARRATOR (V.O.)
He will deal out death and destruction, not on them alone, but on many another of us who live in Ithaca. Let us then be wise in time.

A sleek young chief, son of Polybus, answers him with a laugh, telling the old man to go home and prophesy to his own children. Birds, he says, are always flying about in the sunshine, and they seldom mean anything.

NARRATOR (V.O.)
Ulysses has died in a far country, and it is a pity you are not dead along with him, instead of prating here about omens.

The same young chief warns TELEMACHUS, in the presence of them all, to send his mother back to her father, and vows they will go on eating up the estate without paying.

TELEMACHUS
I shall say no more, and entreat you no further, for the gods and the people of Ithaca now know my story.

He does not sit yet.

TELEMACHUS (CONT'D)
Give me, then, a ship and a crew of twenty men to take me hither and thither, and I will go to Sparta and to Pylos in quest of my father.

TELEMACHUS (CONT'D)
Some one may tell me something, or some heaven-sent message may direct me. If I can hear of him as alive and on his way home I will put up with the waste for yet another twelve months.

TELEMACHUS (CONT'D)
If on the other hand I hear of his death, I will return at once, celebrate his funeral rites with all due pomp, build a barrow to his memory, and make my mother marry again.

With these words he sits down.

MINERVA rises in the likeness of Mentor, an older man, cloaked, seasoned of face, who was a friend of Ulysses and was left in charge of everything with full authority over the servants. She addresses them plainly and in all honesty.

MINERVA
Hear me, men of Ithaca, I hope that you may never have a kind and well-disposed ruler any more, nor one who will govern you equitably.

A stir of anger, then shame.

MINERVA (CONT'D)
I hope that all your chiefs henceforward may be cruel and unjust, for there is not one of you but has forgotten Ulysses, who ruled you as though he were your father.

She does not look at the suitors first. She looks at the silent many.

MINERVA (CONT'D)
I am not half so angry with the suitors, for if they choose to do violence in the naughtiness of their hearts, and wager their heads that Ulysses will not return, they can take the high hand.

MINERVA (CONT'D)
But as for you others I am shocked at the way in which you all sit still without even trying to stop such scandalous goings on.

MINERVA (CONT'D)
Which you could do if you chose, for you are many and they are few.

A son of Evenor answers her with open scorn, calling it folly to set the people on, and swearing that even Ulysses himself would have his blood upon his own head if he fought such odds for his victuals.

NARRATOR (V.O.)
Now, therefore, do you people go about your business, and let his father's old friends speed this boy on his journey, if he goes at all.

On this the speaker breaks up the assembly. Every man goes back to his own abode. The suitors return to the house of Ulysses.

EXT. ITHACA SEASHORE - DAY

Grey waves, dark sand, the town behind. TELEMACHUS goes all alone by the sea side, washes his hands in the water, and prays to MINERVA.

(SOUND: surf dragging pebbles, a gull's cry)

TELEMACHUS
Hear me, you god who visited me yesterday, and bade me sail the seas in search of my father who has so long been missing.

Water runs off his wrists.

TELEMACHUS (CONT'D)
I would obey you, but the Achaeans, and more particularly the wicked suitors, are hindering me that I cannot do so.

As he thus prays, MINERVA comes close up to him in the likeness and with the voice of Mentor: older, cloaked, the seasoned face.

MINERVA
Telemachus, if you are made of the same stuff as your father you will be neither fool nor coward henceforward, for Ulysses never broke his word nor left his work half done.

She studies him.

MINERVA (CONT'D)
If, then, you take after him, your voyage will not be fruitless, but unless you have the blood of Ulysses and of Penelope in your veins I see no likelihood of your succeeding.

MINERVA (CONT'D)
Sons are seldom as good men as their fathers; they are generally worse, not better; still, as you are not going to be either fool or coward henceforward, I look with hope upon your undertaking.

Wind lifts her cloak.

MINERVA (CONT'D)
But mind you never make common cause with any of those foolish suitors, for they have neither sense nor virtue, and give no thought to death and to the doom that will shortly fall on them.

MINERVA (CONT'D)
As for your voyage, it shall not be long delayed; your father was such an old friend of mine that I will find you a ship, and will come with you myself.

She nods toward the town.

MINERVA (CONT'D)
Now, however, return home, and go about among the suitors; begin getting provisions ready for your voyage; see everything well stowed, the wine in jars, and the barley meal in leathern bags.

MINERVA (CONT'D)
While I go round the town and beat up volunteers at once. There are many ships in Ithaca both old and new; I will run my eye over them for you and will choose the best.

MINERVA (CONT'D)
We will get her ready and will put out to sea without delay.

Thus speaks MINERVA, daughter of Jove. TELEMACHUS loses no time in doing as the goddess told him.

EXT. GATEWAY OF ULYSSES HOUSE - DAY

The stone gateway and outer court. SUITORS flay goats and singe pigs among the buildings. Smoke, bristles, blood in the dust. TELEMACHUS, dark-haired, still wearing the comeliness the goddess gave him, comes moodily home.

(SOUND: bristles crackling in flame, a knife through hide)

ANTINOUS comes up at once, handsome, insolent, the cruel mouth smiling, and laughs as he takes TELEMACHUS's hand in his own.

ANTINOUS
Telemachus, my fine fire-eater, bear no more ill blood neither in word nor deed, but eat and drink with us as you used to do.

He squeezes the hand.

ANTINOUS (CONT'D)
The Achaeans will find you in everything—a ship and a picked crew to boot—so that you can set sail for Pylos at once and get news of your noble father.

TELEMACHUS snatches his hand away.

TELEMACHUS
Antinous, I cannot eat in peace, nor take pleasure of any kind with such men as you are.

The smile dies a little.

TELEMACHUS (CONT'D)
Was it not enough that you should waste so much good property of mine while I was yet a boy? Now that I am older and know more about it, I am also stronger.

TELEMACHUS (CONT'D)
Whether here among this people, or by going to Pylos, I will do you all the harm I can.

He does not step back.

TELEMACHUS (CONT'D)
I shall go, and my going will not be in vain—though, thanks to you suitors, I have neither ship nor crew of my own, and must be passenger not captain.

Meanwhile the others go on getting dinner ready about the buildings, jeering at him tauntingly as they do so. One youngster mocks that Telemachus means to be the death of them, and will bring friends from Pylos or Sparta, or poison from Ephyra for their wine. Another says that if he perishes far from his friends they can divide his property, and let his mother and the man who marries her have the house.

This is how they talk. TELEMACHUS turns from them toward the inner doors.

INT. ULYSSES HOUSE - STORE-ROOM - DAY

A lofty, spacious room. Gold and bronze lie heaped upon the floor. Linen and spare clothes wait in open chests. Fragrant olive oil. Casks of old, well-ripened wine, unblended and fit for a god, stand against the wall in case Ulysses should come home again after all.

(SOUND: well-made doors opening in the middle, a cool hollow echo)

The room is closed with those doors. EURYCLEA, grey-haired, straight-backed, the faithful old house-keeper, daughter of Ops the son of Pisenor, is in charge of everything both night and day. TELEMACHUS calls her in.

TELEMACHUS
Nurse, draw me off some of the best wine you have, after what you are keeping for my father's own drinking, in case, poor man, he should escape death, and find his way home again after all.

He keeps his voice low.

TELEMACHUS (CONT'D)
Let me have twelve jars, and see that they all have lids; also fill me some well-sewn leathern bags with barley meal—about twenty measures in all.

TELEMACHUS (CONT'D)
Get these things put together at once, and say nothing about it. I will take everything away this evening as soon as my mother has gone upstairs for the night.

TELEMACHUS (CONT'D)
I am going to Sparta and to Pylos to see if I can hear anything about the return of my dear father.

When EURYCLEA hears this she begins to cry, and speaks fondly to him.

EURYCLEA
My dear child, what ever can have put such notion as that into your head? Where in the world do you want to go to—you, who are the one hope of the house?

Tears run the seams of her face.

EURYCLEA (CONT'D)
Your poor father is dead and gone in some foreign country nobody knows where, and as soon as your back is turned these wicked ones here will be scheming to get you put out of the way.

EURYCLEA (CONT'D)
And will share all your possessions among themselves; stay where you are among your own people, and do not go wandering and worrying your life out on the barren ocean.

(SOUND: her crying against the casks, wine shifting in the dark)

TELEMACHUS
Fear not, nurse, my scheme is not without heaven's sanction; but swear that you will say nothing about all this to my mother, till I have been away some ten or twelve days.

He watches her eyes.

TELEMACHUS (CONT'D)
Unless she hears of my having gone, and asks you; for I do not want her to spoil her beauty by crying.

The old woman swears most solemnly that she will not. When she has completed her oath, she begins drawing off the wine into jars, and getting the barley meal into the bags. TELEMACHUS goes back toward the suitors.

EXT. ITHACA HARBOUR - DAY

Ships old and new lie off the open country, away from the town. MINERVA takes the shape of TELEMACHUS himself—young, dark-haired, the same head and eyes—and goes round the town to each one of a crew, telling them to meet at the ship by sundown.

(SOUND: harbour water slapping hulls, a mallet on a thole-pin)

She goes also to a ship-owner, son of Phronius, and asks him to let her have a ship, which he is very ready to do. She runs her eye over the hulls and chooses the best.

INT. ULYSSES HOUSE - GREAT HALL - NIGHT

Darkness is over all the land. The SUITORS sit over their wine in the high timbered hall. MINERVA throws them into a deep slumber. She causes their drink to fuddle them. Cups drop from their hands.

(SOUND: a cup rolling on stone, then another, heavy breathing)

Instead of sitting over their wine they go back into the town to sleep, eyes heavy and full of drowsiness.

Then she takes the form and voice of Mentor again—older, cloaked, seasoned—and calls TELEMACHUS to come outside.

MINERVA
Telemachus, the men are on board and at their oars, waiting for you to give your orders, so make haste and let us be off.

On this she leads the way. TELEMACHUS follows in her steps.

EXT. ITHACA HARBOUR - NIGHT

The chosen ship is already in the water at the end of the harbour, all the tackle aboard that ships generally carry. The crew wait by the water side. Night wind off the hills. TELEMACHUS, dark-haired, his father's look about the head and eyes, comes down with MINERVA in Mentor's likeness.

(SOUND: water against a hull, oars shipped, a hawser groaning)

TELEMACHUS
Now my men, help me to get the stores on board; they are all put together in the cloister, and my mother does not know anything about it, nor any of the maid servants except one.

With these words he leads the way and the others follow after. They bring the twelve lidded jars and the leathern bags of barley meal as he told them.

TELEMACHUS goes on board. MINERVA goes before him and takes her seat in the stern of the vessel. TELEMACHUS sits beside her. The men loose the hawsers and take their places on the benches.

MINERVA sends them a fair wind from the West that whistles over the deep blue waves.

TELEMACHUS tells them to catch hold of the ropes and hoist sail, and they do as he told them. They set the mast in its socket in the cross plank, raise it, and make it fast with the forestays. Then they hoist their white sails aloft with ropes of twisted ox hide.

(SOUND: the west wind in canvas, foam hissing against the bows)

As the sail bellies out with the wind, the ship flies through the deep blue water. They make all fast throughout the ship, fill the mixing bowls to the brim, and make drink offerings to the immortal gods that are from everlasting, but more particularly to the grey-eyed daughter of Jove.

NARRATOR (V.O.)
Thus, then, the ship sped on her way through the watches of the night from dark till dawn.

Black water. White wake. The island drops astern, and the prow holds south toward Pylos.

