EXT. MENELAUS PALACE - GATE - NIGHT

Low-lying Lacedaemon. A chariot stands at the gate of a house that throws light like a second sunset. Music and the thud of tumblers leak into the yard. TELEMACHUS, young, dark-haired, wonderfully like his father about the head and eyes, holds the rail beside Nestor's unmarried son.

(SOUND: a lyre within, hooves stamping, bare feet slapping a floor)

A servant comes out, sees them, and runs hurrying back into the house. He goes close up to his master and asks whether they shall take the horses out, or tell the strangers to find friends elsewhere as they best can.

INT. MENELAUS PALACE - HALL - NIGHT

Splendour like the sun and moon: bronze, gold, amber, ivory, silver. MENELAUS, a mature king, travel-worn under his wealth, sits among many clansmen. A bard sings. Two tumblers go about in the midst when the man strikes up. The feast honours a son's wedding and a daughter's, the girl being sent with chariots to the Myrmidons, the son taking a bride from Sparta.

(SOUND: lyre, tumblers' feet, gold cups)

MENELAUS is very angry when the servant's question reaches him.

MENELAUS
Eteoneus, son of Boethous, you never used to be a fool, but now you talk like a simpleton.

He does not rise. He does not need to.

MENELAUS (CONT'D)
Take their horses out, of course, and show the strangers in that they may have supper.

MENELAUS (CONT'D)
You and I have staid often enough at other people's houses before we got back here, where heaven grant that we may rest in peace henceforward.

The servant bustles back and bids the other servants come with him. They take the sweating steeds from under the yoke, make them fast to the mangers, and give them a feed of oats and barley mixed. They lean the chariot against the end wall of the courtyard and lead the way into the house.

TELEMACHUS and Nestor's son are astonished when they see it. Then, when they have admired everything to their heart's content, they go into the bath room and wash themselves.

Servants wash them and anoint them with oil, and bring them woollen cloaks and shirts. The two take their seats by the side of MENELAUS. A maid-servant brings water in a beautiful golden ewer and pours it into a silver basin for them to wash their hands. She draws a clean table beside them. An upper servant brings bread and many good things of what there is in the house. The carver fetches plates of all manner of meats and sets cups of gold by their side.

MENELAUS
Fall to, and welcome; when you have done supper I shall ask who you are, for the lineage of such men as you cannot have been lost.

He looks at their hands, their heads, the way they sit.

MENELAUS (CONT'D)
You must be descended from a line of sceptre-bearing kings, for poor people do not have such sons as you are.

On this he hands them a piece of fat roast loin, which has been set near him as being a prime part. They lay their hands on the good things that are before them. As soon as they have had enough to eat and drink, TELEMACHUS speaks to Nestor's son with his head so close that no one may hear.

TELEMACHUS
Look, Pisistratus, man after my own heart, see the gleam of bronze and gold—of amber, ivory, and silver.

His eyes move along the walls.

TELEMACHUS (CONT'D)
Everything is so splendid that it is like seeing the palace of Olympian Jove. I am lost in admiration.

MENELAUS overhears him.

MENELAUS
No one, my sons, can hold his own with Jove, for his house and everything about him is immortal.

He turns the gold cup in his fingers and does not smile.

MENELAUS (CONT'D)
But among mortal men—well, there may be another who has as much wealth as I have, or there may not.

MENELAUS (CONT'D)
But at all events I have travelled much and have undergone much hardship, for it was nearly eight years before I could get home with my fleet.

MENELAUS (CONT'D)
I went to Cyprus, Phoenicia and the Egyptians; I went also to the Ethiopians, the Sidonians, and the Erembians, and to Libya where the lambs have horns as soon as they are born.

MENELAUS (CONT'D)
The sheep lamb down three times a year. Every one in that country, whether master or man, has plenty of cheese, meat, and good milk, for the ewes yield all the year round.

His face changes.

MENELAUS (CONT'D)
But while I was travelling and getting great riches among these people, my brother was secretly and shockingly murdered through the perfidy of his wicked wife, so that I have no pleasure in being lord of all this wealth.

MENELAUS (CONT'D)
Whoever your parents may be they must have told you about all this, and of my heavy loss in the ruin of a stately mansion fully and magnificently furnished.

MENELAUS (CONT'D)
Would that I had only a third of what I now have so that I had stayed at home, and all those were living who perished on the plain of Troy, far from Argos.

He looks past them, into the bronze.

MENELAUS (CONT'D)
I often grieve, as I sit here in my house, for one and all of them. At times I cry aloud for sorrow, but presently I leave off again, for crying is cold comfort and one soon tires of it.

MENELAUS (CONT'D)
Yet grieve for these as I may, I do so for one man more than for them all. I cannot even think of him without loathing both food and sleep, so miserable does he make me.

MENELAUS (CONT'D)
For no one of all the Achaeans worked so hard or risked so much as he did. He took nothing by it, and has left a legacy of sorrow to myself, for he has been gone a long time, and we know not whether he is alive or dead.

MENELAUS (CONT'D)
His old father, his long-suffering wife Penelope, and his son Telemachus, whom he left behind him an infant in arms, are plunged in grief on his account.

Thus speaks MENELAUS, and the heart of TELEMACHUS yearns as he bethinks him of his father. Tears fall from his eyes as he hears him thus mentioned, so that he holds his cloak before his face with both hands.

(SOUND: a caught breath behind wool, a gold cup set down)

MENELAUS sees this and doubts whether to let him choose his own time for speaking, or to ask him at once.

While he is thus in two minds HELEN comes down from her high vaulted and perfumed room, looking as lovely as Diana herself. One woman brings her a seat, another a soft woollen rug. A third fetches the silver work-box which Alcandra wife of Polybus gave her in Egyptian Thebes: it runs on wheels, with a gold band round the top, full of fine spun yarn, a distaff of violet coloured wool laid upon it.

HELEN takes her seat, puts her feet upon the footstool, and begins to question her husband.

HELEN
Do we know, Menelaus, the names of these strangers who have come to visit us? Shall I guess right or wrong?—but I cannot help saying what I think.

She looks long at TELEMACHUS.

HELEN (CONT'D)
Never yet have I seen either man or woman so like somebody else as this young man is like Telemachus.

HELEN (CONT'D)
Indeed when I look at him I hardly know what to think. Ulysses left him as a baby behind him, when you Achaeans went to Troy with battle in your hearts, on account of my most shameless self.

MENELAUS
My dear wife, I see the likeness just as you do. His hands and feet are just like Ulysses; so is his hair, with the shape of his head and the expression of his eyes.

MENELAUS (CONT'D)
Moreover, when I was talking about Ulysses, and saying how much he had suffered on my account, tears fell from his eyes, and he hid his face in his mantle.

Nestor's son speaks for the modest guest: the young man is Telemachus; Nestor sent him to see whether Menelaus could give him any counsel; a son has always trouble at home when his father has gone away leaving him without supporters.

MENELAUS
Bless my heart, then I am receiving a visit from the son of a very dear friend, who suffered much hardship for my sake.

His voice roughens.

MENELAUS (CONT'D)
I had always hoped to entertain him with most marked distinction when heaven had granted us a safe return from beyond the seas. I should have founded a city for him in Argos, and built him a house.

MENELAUS (CONT'D)
I should have made him leave Ithaca with his goods, his son, and all his people, and should have sacked for them some one of the neighbouring cities that are subject to me.

MENELAUS (CONT'D)
We should thus have seen one another continually, and nothing but death could have interrupted so close and happy an intercourse.

MENELAUS (CONT'D)
I suppose, however, that heaven grudged us such great good fortune, for it has prevented the poor fellow from ever getting home at all.

Thus does he speak, and his words set them all a weeping. HELEN weeps. TELEMACHUS weeps. MENELAUS weeps. Nestor's son cannot keep his eyes from filling, remembering his brother Antilochus whom the son of bright Dawn killed.

(SOUND: no lyre now, only breath and a dog shifting on stone)

NARRATOR (V.O.)
Sir, my father Nestor told me you were a person of rare and excellent understanding. I am not fond of crying while I am getting my supper.

NARRATOR (V.O.)
Morning will come in due course. I had a brother who died at Troy; he was by no means the worst man there. His name was Antilochus.

MENELAUS
Your discretion, my friend, is beyond your years. It is plain you take after your father.

MENELAUS (CONT'D)
We will put an end therefore to all this weeping, and attend to our supper again. Let water be poured over our hands. Telemachus and I can talk with one another fully in the morning.

A servant pours water over their hands. They lay their hands on the good things that are before them.

Then HELEN bethinks her of another matter. She drugs the wine with an herb that banishes all care, sorrow, and ill humour. Whoever drinks wine thus drugged cannot shed a single tear all the rest of the day, not even though his father and mother both of them drop down dead, or he sees a brother or a son hewn in pieces before his very eyes.

The drug, of such sovereign power and virtue, was given her by a woman of Egypt, where there grow all sorts of herbs, some good to put into the mixing bowl and others poisonous. When HELEN has put this drug in the bowl, and has told the servants to serve the wine round, she speaks.

HELEN
Menelaus, son of Atreus, and you my good friends, sons of honourable men, feast here as you will, and listen while I tell you a tale in season.

She draws the violet wool through her fingers.

HELEN (CONT'D)
I cannot indeed name every single one of the exploits of Ulysses, but I can say what he did when he was before Troy, and you Achaeans were in all sorts of difficulties.

HELEN (CONT'D)
He covered himself with wounds and bruises, dressed himself all in rags, and entered the enemy's city looking like a menial or a beggar, and quite different from what he did when he was among his own people.

HELEN (CONT'D)
In this disguise he entered the city of Troy, and no one said anything to him. I alone recognised him and began to question him, but he was too cunning for me.

HELEN (CONT'D)
When, however, I had washed and anointed him and had given him clothes, and after I had sworn a solemn oath not to betray him to the Trojans till he had got safely back to his own camp and to the ships, he told me all that the Achaeans meant to do.

HELEN (CONT'D)
He killed many Trojans and got much information before he reached the Argive camp, for all which things the Trojan women made lamentation, but for my own part I was glad.

HELEN (CONT'D)
For my heart was beginning to yearn after my home, and I was unhappy about the wrong that Venus had done me in taking me over there, away from my country, my girl, and my lawful wedded husband.

MENELAUS
All that you have been saying, my dear wife, is true. I have travelled much, and have had much to do with heroes, but I have never seen such another man as Ulysses.

MENELAUS (CONT'D)
What endurance too, and what courage he displayed within the wooden horse, wherein all the bravest of the Argives were lying in wait to bring death and destruction upon the Trojans.

MENELAUS (CONT'D)
At that moment you came up to us; some god who wished well to the Trojans must have set you on to it and you had Deiphobus with you.

MENELAUS (CONT'D)
Three times did you go all round our hiding place and pat it; you called our chiefs each by his own name, and mimicked all our wives.

MENELAUS (CONT'D)
Diomed, Ulysses, and I from our seats inside heard what a noise you made. Diomed and I could not make up our minds whether to spring out then and there, or to answer you from inside.

MENELAUS (CONT'D)
But Ulysses held us all in check, so we sat quite still, all except Anticlus, who was beginning to answer you, when Ulysses clapped his two brawny hands over his mouth, and kept them there.

MENELAUS (CONT'D)
It was this that saved us all, for he muzzled Anticlus till Minerva took you away again.

TELEMACHUS
How sad that all this was of no avail to save him, nor yet his own iron courage. But now, sir, be pleased to send us all to bed, that we may lie down and enjoy the blessed boon of sleep.

A housekeeper lights them out with torches. They lie down to sleep under the echoing gatehouse of that rich house, TELEMACHUS still seeing, behind his lids, a man in rags inside a city that hates him.

INT. MENELAUS PALACE - HALL - DAY

Rosy-fingered Dawn. MENELAUS, travel-worn under his wealth, sits with TELEMACHUS, dark-haired, his father's look about the head and eyes. No more weeping. Water has been poured. The wedding guests are gone.

(SOUND: courtyard horses, a fountain running)

MENELAUS
And now, Telemachus, since you have come to my house, tell me what is the purpose of your visit. Are you on public or private business? Tell me truly.

TELEMACHUS
Menelaus, son of Atreus, I have come to see if you can tell me anything about my father. My house is being devoured and my estate ruined.

TELEMACHUS (CONT'D)
The sons of the chief men among us are suing my mother against her will, and are wearing my house out. I come to your knees, if haply you will tell me of his melancholy end.

TELEMACHUS (CONT'D)
Do not soften things out of any pity for yourselves or for me, but tell me in all plainness exactly what you saw.

MENELAUS
Good heavens, to think that they should sleep at ease in a man of such heroic worth's bed, as though they were a fawn's sucklings that a lion has left in his lair.

His hand closes until the knuckles show.

MENELAUS (CONT'D)
If only Ulysses could show himself among them in all his old might, they would have a short shrift and a sorry wedding.

MENELAUS (CONT'D)
As surely as Jove's wife Minerva loved him and stood by him in the land of the Trojans, I wish he would come in among the suitors as he then was.

MENELAUS (CONT'D)
I will tell you all that the old man of the sea told me, hiding nothing. I was held in Egypt by the gods, and would be there still, had not a goddess taken pity on me.

EXT. ISLAND OF PHAROS - DAY

A low island in the offing, one day's sail from the Nile. Empty hulls on a beach of white sand. MENELAUS and his men starve. Fish fail. The king walks alone, away from the others.

(SOUND: a dry wind, hulls grinding, no birds)

NARRATOR (V.O.)
There is an island in the offing, in front of Egypt, and men call it Pharos. Here the gods kept me twenty days, and I should never have got out.

A goddess rises from the sea in the likeness of a woman, daughter to the old man of the sea. She stands before MENELAUS and pities him.

MENELAUS (V.O.)
Stranger, she said, you must be a fool, or else you are very fond of suffering. There is no longer any way of getting home unless you can waylay Proteus, the old man of the sea, my father.

The sun stands at noon. The sea wrinkles. Seals come up from the brine, the flock of the sea's shepherd, and lie down to sleep on the hot sand. The stench of the deep is thick as wool.

(SOUND: seals coughing and slapping, a reek so strong the breath stops)

MENELAUS (V.O.)
She said he will be among the seals at high noon, and if you can hold him he will turn into every kind of living thing that moves upon the earth, and into water, and into a tree.

She brings four fresh sealskins from the sea. MENELAUS and three trusty companions creep under them. She puts ambrosia under each man's nose, very sweet, to kill the stench of the brine-soaked skins.

They wait. The old man of the sea comes up and counts his seals, counting the four false ones among them, and lies down to sleep in the midst.

MENELAUS and the three spring with a shout and seize him. The old one's body heaves. He becomes a lion, maned and hot. They hold. He becomes a serpent. They hold. A leopard. A huge boar. Running water that tries to slide through their arms. A high, leafy tree. They hold.

(SOUND: a seal-roar changing in the throat, sand tearing, men's breath raw)

MENELAUS (V.O.)
When he was tired of all his magic, he turned again into himself and asked me which of the gods had taught me to waylay him. I told him I had been held here so long I had lost all heart.

MENELAUS (V.O.)
He said I must go back to Egypt and offer hecatombs to the immortal gods, and then they would let me go home. I asked him also of those who had sailed with me from Troy.

On the sand, the old man of the sea speaks without a cue of his own. MENELAUS hears him, eyes shut, still pinning the wet shoulders.

MENELAUS (V.O.)
Ajax, he said, was wrecked. Neptune drove him onto the great rocks of Gyrae, and would have saved him, but he boasted that he had escaped the sea in spite of the gods.

MENELAUS (V.O.)
Then Neptune took his trident and struck the rock asunder, and Ajax fell into the sea and was drowned. Your brother Agamemnon escaped the sea, but death was waiting for him in his own house.

MENELAUS (V.O.)
Aegisthus set a watchman to look out for him, and when he had feasted him murdered him as a man fells an ox at the manger. Your brother died a miserable death.

MENELAUS (V.O.)
I wept on the sand and would hear no more, but I asked him of the third man, whether he was alive or dead. I mean Ulysses, son of Laertes.

INT. MENELAUS PALACE - HALL - DAY

MENELAUS, travel-worn under his wealth, looks straight at TELEMACHUS. The fountain still runs in the court.

(SOUND: the fountain, a single fly against gold)

MENELAUS
He said that he had seen your father in an island weeping. The nymph Calypso was keeping him in her cave and wanted him to marry her, but he would not.

TELEMACHUS's whole body changes.

MENELAUS (CONT'D)
He cannot get away to his own country, for he has no ships, nor yet any sailors to take him over the sea. As for my own end, the old man said I should not die in Argos, but the gods would send me to the Elysian plain.

MENELAUS (CONT'D)
I went back to Egypt, offered the hecatombs, raised a barrow to Agamemnon that his fame might never die, and the gods gave me a fair wind, so that I got home quickly.

He sets his hands on his knees.

MENELAUS (CONT'D)
And now stay on as my guest. I will give you three horses and a chariot, and will escort you myself, and will give you a noble cup that you may pour drink-offerings to the gods and remember me all your days.

TELEMACHUS
Son of Atreus, do not keep me with you long. I could stay the whole year round and never want to go back, but my men are already impatient in Pylos.

TELEMACHUS (CONT'D)
As for any present you may make me, let it be a keepsake. I will not take horses to Ithaca. Your plains are wide and you have plenty of lotus and galingale.

TELEMACHUS (CONT'D)
Ithaca is an island fit for breeding goats, and more delectable than it would be if it were fit for breeding horses; for not one of the islands is good driving ground, nor well meadowed.

MENELAUS smiles and takes his hand.

MENELAUS
You are of a good breed and a true son of Ulysses. I will change the presents. Of all the treasures in my house I will give you the finest.

MENELAUS (CONT'D)
I will give you a mixing bowl of beaten silver with a gold rim to it. It is the work of Vulcan, and was given me by the king of the Sidonians when I was on my way home.

Far away, in another hall, other young men hear that a ship has left Ithaca.

EXT. GATEWAY OF ULYSSES HOUSE - DAY

The stone gateway and outer court. SUITORS sit on ox-hides playing draughts, as before. ANTINOUS, handsome, insolent, the cruel mouth idle, throws the pieces. A ship-owner from the town comes among them and asks, as of a friend, when TELEMACHUS will be back from Pylos, for he wants his ship.

(SOUND: knucklebones clicking, then a sudden stop)

The SUITORS look at one another. They had not thought the boy would go. ANTINOUS questions the man hard. The man says he saw the crew embark, and Mentor go on board—or a god, for he met Mentor yesterday at sunrise, and yet Mentor had gone onto the ship at sundown.

ANTINOUS rises. The cruel mouth is no longer idle.

ANTINOUS
Good heavens, this voyage of Telemachus is a very serious matter; we had made sure that it would come to nothing, and now the young fellow has got away in spite of us.

He looks toward the sea-lane between the islands.

ANTINOUS (CONT'D)
Give me a fast ship and a crew of twenty men, that I may lie in wait for him in the strait between Ithaca and Samos. He shall die for this voyage of his.

They agree. They rise and go down toward the water.

EXT. ITHACA HARBOUR - NIGHT

A ship is drawn into the water. Tackle, oars, arms. Twenty men. They moor her off the coast in the gut of the sea and eat supper on the beach, waiting for a boy to come home.

(SOUND: a hull taking water, arms stacked, the strait running)

INT. ULYSSES HOUSE - UPPER CHAMBER - DAY

PENELOPE, dark-haired, stately, worn with waiting, sits with her women at the loom. A herald from the court comes up and tells her what he has overheard: TELEMACHUS has gone, and the suitors mean to lie in wait and kill him on his way home.

(SOUND: the loom stopping dead, a shuttle falling)

PENELOPE's knees fail. She sits a long time without a word, then finds her voice among the women.

PENELOPE
Why has my son left me? Did he want to die, and does he wish the name of Ulysses to be forgotten from among men?

Her women gather. EURYCLEA, grey-haired, straight-backed, stands before her.

PENELOPE (CONT'D)
Herald, why has he done this? He is too young to take to the sea. Did he go against his will, or did he go on purpose?

The herald has already said what he knows. EURYCLEA does not lie.

EURYCLEA
Kill me if you will, but I will tell you the truth. I knew all about it. I gave him the bread and wine he asked for, and he made me take my oath that I would not tell you for ten or twelve days.

EURYCLEA (CONT'D)
Or until you missed him yourself and asked me, for he did not want you to spoil your beauty by crying. Wash yourself, put on clean clothes, and go upstairs to pray to Minerva.

EURYCLEA (CONT'D)
She can save him even from the suitors. Do not trouble Laertes. He has grief enough already.

PENELOPE does as she is told. She washes, puts on clean clothes, and goes up with her women. She fills a basket with barley meal and prays to MINERVA.

PENELOPE
Hear me, Aegis-bearing Jove's child, unweariable. If ever Ulysses burned you fat thigh bones of sheep or heifer, hear me now. Save my son, and keep the suitors from him.

She breaks into a long, hard weeping. The women of the house make lament with her. Below, the SUITORS raise a clamour in the cloisters, each praying he may be her bedfellow.

(SOUND: women lamenting, a coarse laugh from the cloister)

MINERVA sheds sleep upon PENELOPE's eyelids at last.

INT. ULYSSES HOUSE - UPPER CHAMBER - NIGHT

PENELOPE, dark-haired, veiled even in sleep, lies on her bed. The door is pulled to by its thong. MINERVA bethinks her of another matter. She makes a phantom in the likeness of IPHTHIME, daughter of Icarius, sister to PENELOPE, who married Eumelus and lives in Pherae.

(SOUND: night insects, the thong-hole whispering)

The phantom is sent to the house of Ulysses to make PENELOPE leave off crying. It comes into the room by the hole through which the thong goes for pulling the door to, and hovers over her head.

IPHTHIME
You are asleep, Penelope: the gods who live at ease will not suffer you to weep and be so sad. Your son has done them no wrong, so he will yet come back to you.

