INT. HALL OF OLYMPIAN JOVE - DAY

Dawn rises from her couch beside Tithonus. The colonnade of pale stone stands open to cloud. JOVE sits throned, heavy-limbed, white-bearded, thunder carved along the arm of his seat. MINERVA waits near him, grey-eyed, tall, the bronze-shod spear in her fist, golden sandals on her feet.

(SOUND: distant thunder under the floor, a hush of high wind)

NARRATOR (V.O.)
And now, as Dawn rose, harbinger of light alike to mortals and immortals, the gods met in council and with them Jove the lord of thunder.

MINERVA
Father Jove, and all you other gods who live in everlasting bliss, I hope there may never be such a thing as a kind and well-disposed ruler any more, nor one who will govern equitably.

She does not look away from him.

MINERVA (CONT'D)
I hope they will be all henceforth cruel and unjust, for there is not one of his subjects but has forgotten Ulysses, who ruled them as though he were their father.

MINERVA (CONT'D)
There he is, lying in great pain in an island where dwells the nymph Calypso, who will not let him go; and he cannot get back to his own country, for he can find neither ships nor sailors.

MINERVA (CONT'D)
Furthermore, wicked people are now trying to murder his only son Telemachus, who is coming home from Pylos and Lacedaemon, where he has been to see if he can get news of his father.

JOVE
My child, what are you talking about? Was not this your own doing? You told Ulysses to come home, and now you are complaining.

He turns the thought like a stone.

JOVE (CONT'D)
Go and take Telemachus home yourself, for you can do so quite easily. As for Ulysses, I will send Mercury to the Ogygian island to tell Calypso that we have made up our minds he is to return.

JOVE (CONT'D)
He is to have no escort, neither of gods nor men, but shall sail on a raft in great distress for twenty days, and on the twenty-first he shall reach Scheria, the country of the Phaeacians.

JOVE (CONT'D)
They will honour him as though he were a god, and send him in a ship to his own country, giving him more bronze, gold, and raiment than he would have got from Troy if he had brought his share of the spoil home without mishap.

A messenger of the gods takes up the wand that seals men's eyes in sleep or wakes them as he pleases. He binds on golden sandals, imperishable, with which he can fly like the wind over land or sea, and drops from the topmost summit toward Pieria and the open water.

INT. CALYPSO CAVE - DAY

A great cave on a woodland isle, the navel of the sea. A fire of cleft cedar and juniper throws fragrance through the stone. CALYPSO, a fair goddess, beautiful the way deep water is beautiful, and lonely, sings at her loom with a golden shuttle. A vine with ripe clusters hangs over the mouth. Four fountains. Meadows of violet and parsley.

(SOUND: loom, cedar popping, spring-water)

The messenger stands in the cave. The goddess knows him at once, for the gods know one another however far apart they live. She sets him in a seat and asks why he has come, for he has not been in the habit of visiting her.

NARRATOR (V.O.)
Jove sent me, he said. He says you are to send Ulysses away at once, for it is not ordained that he shall perish here, far from his friends.

The messenger does not sit long. The wand hangs still in his hand.

NARRATOR (V.O.)
He is to return to his own house. This is how Jove says it is to be. I am only his messenger, and must tell you what he told me.

CALYPSO shudders, then stills. The shuttle rests.

CALYPSO
You gods are insatiable of mischief. You are jealous if a goddess takes a fancy to a mortal husband and lives with him in open matrimony.

She looks past him, toward the light at the cave-mouth.

CALYPSO (CONT'D)
So it was when Dawn wanted Orion, and you of the easy lives were vexed till Diana went and killed him in Ortygia.

CALYPSO (CONT'D)
So again when Ceres fell in love with Iasion, and yielded to him in a thrice-ploughed fallow field, Jove came to hear of it and killed Iasion with his thunderbolts.

CALYPSO (CONT'D)
And now you are angry with me too because I have a man here. I found the poor creature sitting all alone astride of a keel, for Jove had struck his ship with lightning and sunk it in mid ocean.

CALYPSO (CONT'D)
I got fond of him and cherished him, and had set my heart on making him immortal, so that he should never grow old all his days.

She bows her head. The cedar smoke moves.

CALYPSO (CONT'D)
But it is not possible for any other god to baffle the will of Jove. Let him go, if Jove has so ordered it, over the barren waters. I cannot send him, for I have no ships.

CALYPSO (CONT'D)
I will, however, give him such advice and will not hide from him how he may get back to his own country and be safe.

The messenger goes. The golden sandals take him from the cave-mouth like a thought. CALYPSO binds a veil about her head and sets out to find ULYSSES.

EXT. OGYGIA SHORE - DAY

The barren ocean. ULYSSES sits on the rocks, not in the cave: a weathered warrior, dark beard salted with grey, broad-shouldered, a white scar hidden under the rag of his tunic. His eyes are worn with watching the empty water. He weeps, as he has wept through the days, tired of life, thinking only how he may see the smoke of his own chimneys.

(SOUND: surf far down, a dry wind in scrub)

CALYPSO comes near him, the fair goddess, lonely even here. She stands over the hunched man.

CALYPSO
My poor friend, you shall not stay here grieving any longer. I have determined to send you away. Come, then, cut down some trees, make yourself a large raft, and I will stock it with bread, water, and wine.

CALYPSO (CONT'D)
I will put good clothes upon you and send you a fair wind, so that you may get home to your own country, if the gods in heaven so will it, and they are stronger than I am.

ULYSSES shudders. He looks at her with the caution of a hunted man.

ULYSSES
Goddess, it is no use your trying to persuade me to go home on a raft, unless you will first take a solemn oath that you mean me no mischief.

ULYSSES (CONT'D)
A raft is a thing that even the best of sailors would not trust in a high sea, and I will not go unless you swear you are not planning some fresh harm against me.

CALYPSO smiles, strokes him with her hand, and answers.

CALYPSO
You are a hard-hearted one. I am not proposing to do anything that I should not do myself if I were in your place.

She lifts her right hand toward earth and heaven.

CALYPSO (CONT'D)
I swear by earth, and heaven, and by the water of the river Styx—which is the most solemn oath that a blessed god can take—that I mean you no harm.

CALYPSO (CONT'D)
I am only thinking of what will be best for yourself. I am dealing with you quite straightforwardly.

When she has sworn, she leads him back toward the cave. He follows, still watching the sea as if it might close behind him.

INT. CALYPSO CAVE - NIGHT

The cedar fire is lower. CALYPSO seats ULYSSES in the chair from which the messenger has gone. Food for mortals is set before him: bread, roast meat, wine. She is served nectar and ambrosia. They put forth their hands. When they have had enough, she speaks more softly.

(SOUND: a small fire of juniper, wine into a cup)

CALYPSO
Ulysses, noble son of Laertes, so you would start home to your own land at once? Good luck go with you.

She looks at the mortal food, then at his face.

CALYPSO (CONT'D)
If you could only know how much suffering is in store for you before you get back to your own country, you would stay here with me and be lord of this island, and I would make you immortal.

CALYPSO (CONT'D)
I know you want to see your wife again, though you are not to think I am less tall or less beautiful than she is, for it is not to be expected that a mortal woman should compare in beauty with an immortal.

ULYSSES
Goddess, do not be angry with me about this. I am quite aware that my wife Penelope is nothing like so tall or so beautiful as yourself.

He looks toward the dark beyond the vine.

ULYSSES (CONT'D)
She is only a woman, whereas you are an immortal. Nevertheless, I want to get home, and can think of nothing else.

ULYSSES (CONT'D)
If some god wrecks me when I am on the sea, I will bear it and make the best of it. I have had infinite trouble both by land and sea already, so let this go with the rest.

They sleep, each turned a little from the other, the decision already made. Night holds the woodland isle.

EXT. OGYGIA SHORE - DAY

Rosy-fingered Dawn. CALYPSO gives him a great bronze axe, fitted on to an olive handle, and an adze. She leads him to the far end of the island, where tall, long-seasoned, dry-hearted trees stand: alder, poplar, and pine that will float lightly.

(SOUND: axe in timber, a trunk going over)

ULYSSES, dark beard salted with grey, broad-shouldered, fells twenty trees. He adzes them true and works them by line. He bores them and fits them with pegs. CALYPSO brings him a wimble. He makes a deck, a mast, a yard, a steering-oar. He fences the raft with wicker and green saplings against the sea. She brings cloth for a sail. He makes it, and binds braces, halyards, and sheets upon her.

Four days the work lasts. Dawn, then noon, then the same trees shorter. On the fifth day she bathes him, gives him fragrant clothes, a skin of dark wine, a smaller one of water, and a bag of savoury meat. She sends a warm and kindly wind.

He spreads his sail. He never closes his eyes, but keeps them on the Pleiads, on the Bear that turns about in one place watching Orion, and on the Hunter, as she told him, leaving the Bear on his left hand.

The woodland isle drops astern.

EXT. OPEN SEA - DAY

Grey swell under a hard sky. The raft of lashed timbers runs before the warm wind. ULYSSES, weathered, grey in the dark beard, fragrant clothes already salted, holds the steering-oar. Seventeen dawns pass over the same water. On the eighteenth the shadowy mountains of the land of the Phaeacians rise like a shield upon the misty sea.

(SOUND: wind in a single sail, the raft's pegs working)

NEPTUNE, returning from the Ethiopians, sees him from the mountains of the Solymi. The god's face darkens. He gathers the clouds and troubles the waters, trident in hand, and speaks over the waste as a man speaks to a thing he means to break.

NARRATOR (V.O.)
There now, swim up and down as you best can among the waves. I do not think you will be able to say that I have let you off too lightly.

East, south, west, and north leap together. Night falls from heaven. A tremendous wave crashes down. The raft spins. ULYSSES is thrown clear. He clings to a timber, salt gushing from his mouth.

ULYSSES
Alas, Jove is giving me a hard time. Happy were the Danaans who perished on the plain of Troy, fighting for the son of Atreus.

Water slams the wreck back under his chest.

ULYSSES (CONT'D)
Would that I had died that day the Trojans hurled their bronze-shod spears at me over the body of Achilles. I should have had my rites, and the Achaeans would have spread my fame, whereas now I shall die a pitiful death.

Another sea strikes. The steering-oar is wrenched from his hands. The mast snaps. Sail and yard go overboard. He is left on the broken decking like a man on a door.

(SOUND: timber bursting, wind tearing cloth, a sea falling like a wall)

A sea-nymph, Ino of the fair ankles, Leucothea who was once a mortal, rises in the likeness of a cormorant and sits upon the wreck. She pities him. She takes a veil from her head and holds it out.

NARRATOR (V.O.)
My poor good man, why is Neptune so furiously angry with you? He will not kill you quite. Strip off those clothes, leave the raft to its fate, and swim for it.

NARRATOR (V.O.)
Take my veil and tie it round your chest. As soon as you touch land, take it off and throw it back into the sea as far as you can, and then go away.

He does not trust even this. He thinks: if some god is laying a snare, I will stay with the wreck while it still holds. The next wave comes on, arching, terrible, and bursts the timbers apart. He climbs astride one beam as a man rides a horse. He strips the heavy clothes Calypso gave him. He ties the veil about his chest and throws himself headlong into the sea.

EXT. OPEN SEA - NIGHT

Black water. No raft. ULYSSES swims. NEPTUNE sees him still and shakes his head, then turns his well-shod horses toward Aegae, where he has his palace. MINERVA, grey-eyed, stills all the winds but the North, and breaks the waves before him so that he may come among the Phaeacians without dying.

(SOUND: a long fetch of sea, his torn breathing, wind falling one quarter at a time)

Two nights and two days he is driven through the swell. The veil holds his chest. His skin swells with the water. He loses the count of strokes, then finds them again. When Dawn of the third day comes, land shows at last: high rocks, a running coast, no beach that he can trust.

He fears he will be dashed to pieces. A great wave takes him in toward the cliffs. He would have been flayed there, but he clings to a rock with both hands until the sea goes back, and the skin is stripped from his palms. The next backwash drags him out again, deep.

He swims outside the surf, looking along the land for a place where the waves will not grind him. He hears a river running.

EXT. SCHERIA RIVER MOUTH - DAWN

Fresh water meets the sea. ULYSSES, weathered, grey in the dark beard, skin swollen, the sea gushing from his mouth and nostrils, prays in the current.

(SOUND: river over stones, surf dying at the bar)

ULYSSES
Hear me, O King, whoever you may be, and save me from the anger of the sea-god Neptune, for I declare myself your suppliant.

ULYSSES (CONT'D)
Even the gods respect a man who comes to them as I now come to your stream, after much suffering. Pity me, O King, for I call myself your suppliant.

The current stills. He lets his knees find bottom. He crawls from the water and kisses the earth, the veil still bound about his chest. He takes it off and throws it into the seaward stream. A wave carries it out. Ino receives it into her hands.

He lies in the reeds, swollen, breathless, and considers. If he keeps watch on the river bank, the cold and the fresh dew will be too much for him, for a wind is blowing from the river. If he goes up inland into the scrub, he may sleep and become a prey to wild beasts.

He goes toward a wood and finds two olive trees growing from the same root, one wild, one grafted, so close that no wet wind can blow through them, nor sun strike, nor rain soak. He creeps under them and heaps dead leaves over himself, a thick bed, enough to cover two or three men in winter time.

(SOUND: dry leaves gathered, his breathing slowing)

Then, as one who lives alone in the country, far from any neighbour, hides a brand as fire-seed in the ashes to save himself from having to get a light elsewhere, even so does ULYSSES cover himself up with leaves.

MINERVA sheds a sweet sleep upon his eyes, closes his eyelids, and makes him lose all memories of his sorrows.

