INT. ALCINOUS PALACE - NAUSICAA CHAMBER - NIGHT

A pompous room in the Phaeacian house. NAUSICAA, a young princess, lovely, sleeps. MINERVA stands over her in the likeness of a girl friend, daughter to Dymas, grey eyes hidden in a mortal face.

(SOUND: a night hush, one lamp-wick ticking)

NARRATOR (V.O.)
So here Ulysses slept, overcome by sleep and toil; but Minerva went off to the country and city of the Phaeacians.

MINERVA
Nausicaa, what can your mother have been about, to have such a lazy daughter? Here are your clothes lying in disorder, yet you are going to be married almost immediately.

The sleeping girl draws a breath.

MINERVA (CONT'D)
You ought to have good clothes both to wear yourself, and to give out to those who will take you to your new home. This is the way to get yourself a good name.

MINERVA (CONT'D)
Let us go and wash these clothes early to-morrow morning. I will help. Ask your father to have a waggon and mules ready for us at daybreak.

The likeness fades. NAUSICAA sleeps on, the dream already working in her face.

INT. ALCINOUS PALACE - HALL - DAWN

Rosy light on bronze and cloth. NAUSICAA, young, lovely, finds her father and mother. The queen sits at the hearth spinning yarn of sea-purple. ALCINOUS, king of the Phaeacians, is going out to a council of the lords.

(SOUND: a spindle, sandals on stone)

NAUSICAA
Papa dear, could you manage to let me have a good waggon? I want to take all our dirty clothes to the river and wash them.

She colours, and does not say wedding.

NAUSICAA (CONT'D)
You are the chief man here, so it is only proper that you should have a clean shirt when you attend meetings of the council.

NAUSICAA (CONT'D)
Moreover, you have five sons at home, two of them married, while the other three are good-looking bachelors; you know they always like to have clean linen when they go to a dance.

ALCINOUS
You shall have the mules, my love, and whatever else you have a mind for. Be off with you, and the men shall get you a good strong waggon with a body to it that will hold all your clothes.

He calls to the men. They yoke the mules. The queen puts bread, wine, and all sorts of good things together in a basket, and gives NAUSICAA a golden cruse of oil, that she and her maids may anoint themselves.

EXT. SCHERIA WASHING POOLS - DAY

A river with washing pits above the sea. The mule-waggon stands in the shade. NAUSICAA and her maids take the clothes from the waggon, tread them in the pits, and spread them on the beach where the sea washes the shingle clean. Then they bathe, anoint themselves with oil from the golden cruse, and eat, while the clothes dry.

(SOUND: wet linen slapped on stone, mules cropping, girls laughing)

They take off their head-dresses and play at ball, NAUSICAA leading the song. MINERVA causes the ball to fall into the river. The girls shriek.

Under two olive trees growing from one root, ULYSSES starts awake: weathered, dark beard salted with grey, broad-shouldered, skin still marked by the sea. He peers from the leaves.

ULYSSES
What kind of people can they be? Are they cruel and savage, not fearing the gods, or are they kindly disposed and hospitable?

He comes forth, covering his nakedness with a leafy branch, like a weather-beaten lion driven by hunger toward a homestead. The maids flee along the beach. NAUSICAA stands her ground, for MINERVA puts courage into her heart.

(SOUND: the shriek dying, shingle under a man's bare feet)

He debates whether to clasp her knees or speak from a distance. He speaks from a distance.

ULYSSES
O queen, I throw myself on your mercy. Are you a goddess or a mortal woman? If you are a goddess, you are Jove's daughter Diana, for your face and figure resemble none but hers.

He keeps the branch between them.

ULYSSES (CONT'D)
If you are a mortal and live on earth, thrice happy are your father and mother, thrice happy your brothers. Their hearts must be glad when they see so fair a plant as you going out to a dance.

ULYSSES (CONT'D)
And happiest of all will he be whose wedding gifts have been the richest, and who takes you to his own home.

ULYSSES (CONT'D)
I never yet saw any one so beautiful, neither man nor woman, and am lost in admiration as I behold you. I can only compare you to a young palm tree which I saw when I was at Delos growing near the altar of Apollo.

ULYSSES (CONT'D)
I dare not clasp your knees, and yet I am in great distress. Yesterday I was wrecked and spent twenty days in the sea, till at last the waves have flung me upon this coast.

ULYSSES (CONT'D)
Show me the way to your town, and let me have anything you may have brought here to wrap myself in. May the gods grant you your heart's desire; a husband, a house, and a happy agreement of minds between you.

NAUSICAA
Stranger, you seem to be a sensible, well-disposed person. There is no accounting for luck; Jove gives prosperity to rich and poor just as he chooses, so you must take what he has seen fit to send you, and make the best of it.

She does not step back.

NAUSICAA (CONT'D)
Now, however, that you have come to this our country, you shall not want for clothes nor for anything else that a foreigner in distress may reasonably look for.

NAUSICAA (CONT'D)
I will show you the town and will tell you the name of our people. We are the Phaeacians, and I am daughter to Alcinous, in whom the whole power of the state is vested.

She calls to the maids, who still huddle down the beach.

NAUSICAA (CONT'D)
Stop, my girls. Where are you flying to at the sight of a man? Do you take him for an enemy? This is some unlucky wanderer whom we must take care of, for all strangers and beggars are from Jove.

NAUSICAA (CONT'D)
Give him something to eat and drink, and wash him in the stream at a place where it is sheltered from the wind.

They set him in the shelter and leave a shirt, a cloak, and the golden cruse. He will not let them wash him.

ULYSSES
I will wash in the stream, and I will rub myself with oil. There is plenty of it on the beach. I will not wash before you, for I am ashamed to be naked in the presence of a lot of young women.

They go apart. He washes the sea crust from his skin and the brine from his back and shoulders, and anoints himself. He puts on the clothes the princess has given him. MINERVA makes him taller and stouter, and thickens the hair on his head like a hyacinth flower. He comes from the shelter like a skilled workman's gold over silver.

The maids stare. NAUSICAA looks long.

NAUSICAA
Listen to me, my maids. I should like my future husband to be just such another as he is, if he would only stay here and not want to go away.

They give him food and wine. He eats and drinks greedily, for it is a long time since he has had any food. When he is satisfied, NAUSICAA folds the clothes, sets them on the waggon, and yokes the mules.

NAUSICAA
Stranger, rise and let us be going back to the town. I will instruct you near the town how you are to go to the house of my father.

They start. She drives. He walks with the maids. When they come near the houses she pulls up.

NAUSICAA
Stranger, we are now coming near the town. I will not have our people saying things about me, for there are a good many ill-natured people in the place.

NAUSICAA (CONT'D)
Some low fellow might meet me and say, Who is this fine-looking stranger with Nausicaa? Where did she find him? I suppose she is going to marry him.

NAUSICAA (CONT'D)
I could not complain, for I should myself be scandalised at any girl who did such a thing, and went about with men before she was married, against her father's and mother's wish.

She points inland, away from the ship yards.

NAUSICAA (CONT'D)
When you have got past the ship yards you will see my father's grove of poplar trees, and a spring that runs into a meadow. Sit there and wait till we have got into the town and reached the house.

NAUSICAA (CONT'D)
When you think we have had time to get home, come into the town and ask for the house of my father Alcinous. You will have no difficulty, for it is easily known.

NAUSICAA (CONT'D)
When you have got past the courtyard, go straight through the inner part of the house till you come to my mother. You will find her sitting by the fire and spinning her purple wool.

NAUSICAA (CONT'D)
My father's throne is close against her. If you can gain her good will, you may hope to see your friends again, and get safely back to your own country.

She lashes the mules. The waggon runs on toward the town. ULYSSES turns aside toward the poplars.

EXT. ALCINOUS POPLAR GROVE - DAY

Poplar trees, a spring, a meadow. ULYSSES, now taller, hair like hyacinth, the princess's cloak on his salted skin, sits down and prays to MINERVA.

(SOUND: spring-water, leaves, the waggon dying away)

ULYSSES
Hear me, queen, unweariable, daughter of Jove. Hear me now, for you gave me no help when I was shipwrecked. Grant that I may find friends and be hospitably received by the Phaeacians.

MINERVA hears him, but she will not show herself yet, for she fears her uncle Neptune, who is still furious with ULYSSES.

The sun moves. When he judges the princess has reached home, he rises.

A young girl comes toward the grove with a pitcher—MINERVA in that likeness, grey eyes quiet in a child's face. She meets him as if by chance.

MINERVA
My friend, will you show me the house of King Alcinous? I am a stranger here. I have come from a long way off, and I do not know one of the people.

ULYSSES
I will show you the house, my dear. My father lives near it. But say nothing about my having met you, for the people here do not like us to be too friendly with foreigners.

She leads him. A mist that she sheds clings about him so that no Phaeacian stares. They pass the harbour and the long oars, the grove of Neptune, and the stone seats where the sailors mend their tackle.

MINERVA
This is the house, sir. You will find the chiefs of the Phaeacians drinking their wine. Go in, and have no fear. The bold man is better in all things.

MINERVA (CONT'D)
You will see the queen. Her name is Arete, and she comes of the same family as her husband. If you can gain her good will, you may hope to see your friends, and get back to your own country.

The girl is gone. The mist stays.

INT. ALCINOUS PALACE - HALL - DAY

Walls of bronze, a blue enamel dado, golden doors, silver door-posts, gold and silver dogs that will never grow old, made by Vulcan to keep watch. Within, the chiefs of the Phaeacians sit pouring wine. Along the walls, seats with coverlets. Fifty women grind meal, weave, and turn the distaff. Outside the court, a great garden: pears, pomegranates, apples, figs, olives, grapes that ripen one after another the year through. Two springs, one for the house, one for the town.

(SOUND: wine into bowls, looms, a fountain in the orchard)

ULYSSES, hidden in the mist, walks through the hall to the queen. ARETE sits by the fire spinning purple wool, a stately woman. ALCINOUS's throne is close against hers. ULYSSES clasps the queen's knees. The mist falls. They stare. He is a suppliant at the hearth among the ashes.

ULYSSES
Arete, daughter of great Rhexenor, after much suffering I throw myself at your knees.

Ash greys his cloak.

ULYSSES (CONT'D)
May heaven grant you a prosperous life in this house, and may you leave your children to inherit after you. But as for me, send me to my own country as soon as may be, for I have been a wanderer for many years far from my friends.

A long silence. An old councillor, the most eloquent among them, speaks to ALCINOUS and bids him raise the stranger and set him in a seat of honour, for it is not seemly that a guest should sit in the ashes.

ALCINOUS takes ULYSSES by the hand, raises him from the hearth, and seats him in the chair of his favourite son, who gives place. A maid brings water in a golden ewer and pours it into a silver basin. A table is drawn. Bread and meat. The king pours a drink-offering to Jove, who takes all suppliants under his protection.

ALCINOUS
To-morrow we will hold an assembly and consider how we may take this stranger back to his own country, without any further trouble to himself.

ALCINOUS (CONT'D)
As for the others, go now, every one of you, to your own houses. I will provide for this my guest.

The chiefs go. When the hall is quieter, ARETE looks long at the cloak and shirt, which she herself had made with her maids. She asks the stranger who he is, and who gave him those clothes, for she is sure she made them.

ULYSSES
It would be too painful to tell you all the troubles the gods have given me. I will tell you, however, about the clothes.

He keeps his eyes from no one.

ULYSSES (CONT'D)
There is an island far off in the sea called Ogygia, where the daughter of Atlas, crafty Calypso, lives. No god nor mortal man goes near her, but some power took me to her hearth, alone, for Jove had struck my ship with lightning.

ULYSSES (CONT'D)
She took me in and cherished me. She wanted me to marry her, and promised to make me immortal so that I should never grow old, but she could not persuade me.

ULYSSES (CONT'D)
I stayed with her seven years, and watered the immortal clothes she gave me with my tears. When the eighth came round she bade me go, either because Jove had told her, or because she had changed her mind.

ULYSSES (CONT'D)
She sent me from her island on a raft, with bread and wine, and good clothes, and a fair wind. For seventeen days I sailed, and on the eighteenth I saw the mountains of your country.

ULYSSES (CONT'D)
Then Neptune raised the winds against me and broke my raft. I swam, and your daughter found me when I was at the last gasp. I never saw any one so sensible.

ULYSSES (CONT'D)
She gave me bread and wine, had me washed in the river, and gave me these clothes. I wanted her to bring me here with her, but she would not, for she was afraid of the scandal.

ALCINOUS
My child was not far wrong. She is a sensible girl. I only wish that a man like you, and of your age, would stay here and marry her. I would give you a house and an estate if you would stay.

He lifts a hand, not to hold, only to offer.

ALCINOUS (CONT'D)
I will not, however, do anything to put you out if you wish to go. As for your sending, I appoint it for to-morrow. You shall lie down and sleep while the crew of the ship sail you through the night.

ALCINOUS (CONT'D)
You shall see your own country and your house, even though it be much farther off than Euboea, which those of my people who saw it when they took yellow-haired Rhadamanthus say is the farthest of all lands.

ULYSSES
Father Jove, grant that Alcinous may do all as he has said. So shall he win a name that shall never perish, and I shall return to my own country.

INT. ALCINOUS PALACE - HALL - NIGHT

Torches. The same bronze walls, quieter. Women come with their hands full of bedding and go to make a bed in a room over the echoing gateway: rugs, blankets, and a coverlet.

(SOUND: torch-hiss, linen shaken out)

They come back to ULYSSES and tell him to rise, for his bed is ready. Glad indeed is he to go to his rest.

He sleeps in that bed over the echoing gateway. ALCINOUS lies in the inner part of the house, with the queen his wife by his side.

