EXT. PHAEACIAN ASSEMBLY - DAY

Rosy-fingered Dawn. Near the ships, a place of assembly paved with stone. ALCINOUS, king of the Phaeacians, leads ULYSSES, taller now, hair like hyacinth, still a nameless guest. They sit side by side on a seat of polished stone.

(SOUND: a crowd gathering, hulls knocking at the quay)

MINERVA takes the form of one of Alcinous's servants and goes through the city. Wherever she passes, men turn.

MINERVA
Come here, you Phaeacian chiefs and councillors, to the assembly, and learn about the stranger who has lately come to the house of Alcinous.

MINERVA (CONT'D)
He looks like an immortal god. Come and see him.

The seats fill. When the gathering is complete, ALCINOUS speaks.

ALCINOUS
Hear me, chiefs and councillors of the Phaeacians, that I may speak even as I am minded. This stranger, whoever he may be, has found his way to my house from somewhere or other either East or West.

ALCINOUS (CONT'D)
He wants an escort and wishes to have the matter settled. Let us then get one ready for him, as we have done for others before.

ALCINOUS (CONT'D)
Let us draw a ship into the sea—one that has never yet made a voyage—and man her with two and fifty of our smartest young sailors.

ALCINOUS (CONT'D)
Then when you have made fast your oars each by his own seat, leave the ship and come to my house. We will feast these young men. I will find them in everything.

ALCINOUS (CONT'D)
I am doing this that the stranger may be sent away as he would wish. I will also tell Demodocus to come, that he may sing to us. There is no bard like him.

The young men do as he bids. A new ship is drawn down. Two and fifty sailors make her ready and moor her out in the water. Then they go to the house of ALCINOUS.

INT. ALCINOUS PALACE - HALL - DAY

The bronze walls, the gold dogs. Sheep, pigs, and a full-grown ox are led in and flayed. The hall fills with the smell of roast meat. A herald leads in DEMODOCUS, the blind bard. The Muse has loved him and has given him both good and evil: she took his eyes, and she gave him a sweet voice.

(SOUND: a lyre settled against a pillar, fat in the fire)

They set him in a silver-studded chair against a bearing-post, hang his lyre on a peg over his head, and show him how to find it with his hands. A table is drawn beside him. Bread, meat, wine. When they have had enough to eat and drink, the Muse moves the bard to sing of the deeds of heroes.

NARRATOR (V.O.)
He sang of the quarrel between Ulysses and Achilles, how they wrangled at a feast of the gods, while Agamemnon was glad at heart, for Apollo had foretold it.

ULYSSES draws his purple cloak over his head and weeps. Whenever the bard leaves off, he wipes his eyes, raises his cup, and pours a drink-offering. When the song begins again, he covers his face once more. The others do not see. ALCINOUS, sitting beside him, hears the caught breath and sees the cloak shake.

(SOUND: the lyre, a man weeping under wool)

ALCINOUS
Chiefs and councillors of the Phaeacians, we have had enough now both of the feast and of the minstrelsy that is its due accompaniment. Let us go out and try our skill at games.

ALCINOUS (CONT'D)
The stranger may then tell his friends, when he gets home, how much we surpass all other nations as boxers, wrestlers, jumpers, and runners.

They rise. The blind bard's hands rest on the silent strings.

EXT. PHAEACIAN GAMES GROUND - DAY

An open ground near the assembly, marked for sport. The Phaeacian young men run, wrestle, jump, throw the disc, and box. One wins the foot-race. Another the wrestling. A son of ALCINOUS, tall and fair, looks toward the stranger.

(SOUND: bare feet on packed earth, a disc thudding, a crowd shout)

The king's son comes up to ULYSSES and invites him, as a friend, to try his skill, saying there is no greater glory for a man while he is still alive than that which he wins with his own hands and feet.

ULYSSES
Laodamas, why do you taunt me in this way? My mind is set rather on cares than on contests. I have been through many hardships, and am now sitting in your assembly, longing to get home.

A young Phaeacian, handsome and thick-set, laughs and looks him up and down.

NARRATOR (V.O.)
I gather, then, that you are unskilled in any of the many sports that men generally delight in.

NARRATOR (V.O.)
I suppose you are one of those grasping traders that go about in ships as captains or merchants. You seem to be more of a skipper than an athlete.

ULYSSES's brow darkens. He keeps the cloak on a moment longer.

ULYSSES
You are an insolent fellow—so true is it that the gods do not grace all men alike in speech, person, and understanding.

ULYSSES (CONT'D)
One man may be of weak presence, but heaven has adorned him with such a good conversation that he charms every one who sees him.

ULYSSES (CONT'D)
Another may be as handsome as a god, but his conversation is empty. You are good-looking, but your manners are bad.

He lets the cloak fall and stands.

ULYSSES (CONT'D)
You have made me angry. I am no worse than you, and I will try my strength. Your words have stung me to the quick.

He goes straight to a disc that lies there, bigger and thicker than those the Phaeacians have been using. He whirls it and hurls it from his brawny hand. The stone hums. It flies over the marks of all the other throws.

(SOUND: the great disc humming, then a far thud in the dust)

MINERVA, in the likeness of a man, sets a mark and calls the throw.

MINERVA
Even a blind man, sir, could find your throw by groping for it, for it is a long way ahead of all the others. You may take heart. No Phaeacian will make a better.

ULYSSES is glad to hear a friendly voice.

ULYSSES
Now then, young men, who wants to take me on? I will box, wrestle, or run a match with any of you except Laodamas, for he is my host.

ULYSSES (CONT'D)
I am not a bad hand at any kind of sport. I can throw a spear farther than any other man can shoot an arrow. It is only in running that I am afraid some of the Phaeacians may beat me, for I have been much knocked about at sea.

ALCINOUS
Sir, we like what you have said. A man should take his own part when he is insulted. Euryalus has not done you honour, and I see that you are angry.

ALCINOUS (CONT'D)
We are not first-rate boxers or wrestlers, but we can run fast and are first-rate sailors. We love feasts, the harp, the dance, changes of clothes, hot baths, and beds.

ALCINOUS (CONT'D)
Let some of you who are the best dancers, dance, that the stranger may tell his friends when he gets home how much we surpass all other nations as sailors, runners, dancers, and minstrels.

ALCINOUS (CONT'D)
Fetch Demodocus his lyre. It is lying in my house. And make haste.

A herald runs for the lyre.

INT. ALCINOUS PALACE - HALL - LATER

The dancing floor is cleared. DEMODOCUS sits with his lyre. Young men in their first bloom stand round him and beat the ground with their feet. He sings of the love of Mars and Venus: how they lay together in Vulcan's house, how the Sun saw them and told the smith, how Vulcan spread a net as fine as a spider's web about the bed, how the gods stood in the doorway and laughed, and how Neptune pledged himself that Mars should pay the fine.

(SOUND: lyre, bare feet in time, a laugh along the benches)

Then two dancers throw a purple ball from one to the other, one on the ground, one leaping. The hall watches. ULYSSES turns to ALCINOUS.

ULYSSES
King Alcinous, you said your people were the best dancers in the world, and they have proved it. I am struck with admiration.

ALCINOUS is pleased. He bids the twelve kings who rule around him each bring a clean cloak, a shirt, and a talent of fine gold, and that the young man who insulted the stranger bring a gift as well, to make amends.

They go and fetch the gifts and set them before ULYSSES. The thick-set young man puts a bronze sword with a silver hilt and an ivory scabbard into his hand and asks him to forget the hard words. ULYSSES takes it and wishes him well.

The queen has a chest brought, and packs in it the gold and the clothes. ALCINOUS adds a gold cup, that ULYSSES may remember him all his days when he pours drink-offerings. The queen tells the stranger to look to the lid himself and tie the knot, lest some one rob him on the ship. He ties it with a cunning knot that Circe once taught him.

A hot bath is made ready. When he comes from it, refreshed, the women have taken the gifts to the chest. NAUSICAA, young, lovely, stands by a bearing-post and looks at him as he passes.

(SOUND: bath-water running off, then a still hall)

NAUSICAA
Sir, good-bye. Do not forget me when you are safe at home again, for it is to me first that you owe a ransom for having saved your life.

ULYSSES
Nausicaa, daughter of great Alcinous, may Jove, the mighty husband of Juno, grant me to reach my house and see my friends.

He looks at her as at an altar.

ULYSSES (CONT'D)
So shall I bless you as a goddess all my days, for it was you who saved me.

INT. ALCINOUS PALACE - HALL - NIGHT

Torches. A second feast. ULYSSES, bathed, the hyacinth hair still thick, sits beside ALCINOUS. The herald leads DEMODOCUS back to his pillar. ULYSSES cuts a portion from the chine of a white-tusked boar, rich with fat, and tells the herald to give it to the bard.

(SOUND: torch-hiss, the lyre lifted down from its peg)

ULYSSES
Herald, take this to Demodocus. I will give it him, for bards are honoured by all men upon earth, because the Muse has taught them song, and loves them.

The blind man takes the meat, glad. When they have eaten, ULYSSES turns toward him.

ULYSSES
Demodocus, there is no one in the world whom I admire more than I do you. You must have studied under the Muse, Jove's daughter, or under Apollo.

ULYSSES (CONT'D)
You have sung the story of the fate of the Achaeans with perfect truth, as if you had been there yourself or heard it from one who was.

ULYSSES (CONT'D)
Now, however, change your song and tell us of the construction of the wooden horse which Epeus made with the assistance of Minerva, and which Ulysses got by stratagem into the citadel of Troy.

ULYSSES (CONT'D)
If you will sing this tale aright, I will tell all the world how magnificently heaven has endowed you.

The bard begins. He sings how the Argives burned their camps and sailed, how the horse stood in the city, how the Trojans sat around it and could not agree, how they would have cut it open or hurled it down or left it as an offering, and how it was fate that they should leave it, for it was pregnant with death. He sings how the Achaeans poured out, and how ULYSSES went with Menelaus to the house of Deiphobus, and there fought his grimmest fight, and won it by MINERVA's help.

ULYSSES weeps. The tears run down his cheeks. As a woman throws herself upon her husband who has fallen before his city, and shrieks while the enemy beat her back to slavery, even so does he let the pitiful tears fall from his eyes.

(SOUND: the lyre going on, then a cup ringing as his hand shakes)

The others do not see. ALCINOUS, sitting by him, hears and sees. He speaks at once.

ALCINOUS
Let Demodocus cease his song, for this subject does not seem to give pleasure to every one. From the moment the bard began, our guest has been weeping as if his heart would break.

The lyre stops.

ALCINOUS (CONT'D)
Let the bard leave off, that we may all enjoy ourselves, hosts and guest alike. This is more right and proper, for we have prepared a feast and made ready a ship for our worthy guest.

ALCINOUS (CONT'D)
And now, sir, tell me your name and that of your father and mother. Tell me your country, that our ships may shape their course accordingly.

ALCINOUS (CONT'D)
Our ships themselves understand what we are thinking about and want. They know all the cities in the world and can cross the sea even when it is covered with mist.

ALCINOUS (CONT'D)
Tell me also why you weep and take it so hardly when you hear of the fate of the Argives and of Troy. The gods arranged all this, and sent the people to their deaths in order that future generations might have something to sing about.

ALCINOUS (CONT'D)
Did you lose some brave kinsman of your wife's when you were before Troy? a son-in-law or father-in-law—which are the nearest relations a man has outside his own flesh and blood?

ALCINOUS (CONT'D)
Or was it some brave and kindly-natured comrade—for a good friend is as dear to a man as his own brother?

ULYSSES sits with the cloak in his hands and does not yet give his name.

---VISION_META---
