INT. ALCINOUS PALACE - HALL - NIGHT

Torches. The lyre is still. ALCINOUS waits. ULYSSES, hyacinth-haired, the purple cloak in his hands, lifts his face to the king and to the listening hall.

(SOUND: a torch-wick, wine settling in a cup)

ULYSSES
King Alcinous, it is a good thing to hear a bard with such a divine voice as this man has.

ULYSSES (CONT'D)
There is nothing better or more delightful than when a whole people make merry together, with the guests sitting orderly to listen, while the table is loaded with bread and meats, and the cup-bearer draws wine and fills his cup for every man.

He sets the cloak aside.

ULYSSES (CONT'D)
This is indeed as fair a sight as a man can see. Now, however, since you are inclined to ask the story of my sorrows, and I must mourn still more, I will tell you.

ULYSSES (CONT'D)
First of all I will tell you my name, that you may know it, and my fame both here and in my own country.

A stillness.

ULYSSES (CONT'D)
I am Ulysses son of Laertes, renowned among mankind for all manner of subtlety, so that my fame ascends to heaven.

ULYSSES (CONT'D)
I live in Ithaca, where there is a high mountain called Neritum, covered with forests; and I will now tell you of the many hazardous adventures which by Jove's will I met with on my way home.

EXT. CICONS SHORE - DAY

A walled town on a Thracian beach. Black ships in the shallows. ULYSSES, dark-bearded, broad-shouldered, a captain among his men, leads the raid. They sack the city, kill the men, take women and treasure, and divide them so that none may go short of his proper share.

(SOUND: bronze on gates, a town crying out)

A priest of Apollo they spare. He gives them fine wine, twelve jars, red and honey-sweet, and other gifts. ULYSSES bids his men go back to the ships at once. They will not listen. They drink and slaughter sheep and oxen on the shore.

The inland Cicons gather, more and braver, and fall on them at dawn as leaves or flowers in their season. The battle holds all day. When the sun goes down, the Cicons prevail. Six men from each ship are lost. The rest outrun death and put to sea.

NARRATOR (V.O.)
We sailed on with sorrow in our hearts, but glad to have escaped death though we had lost our comrades.

A north wind, Jove's wind, tears the sails. They lie up two days. On the third they run, and would have reached home, but the current and a north wind, and the force of the west, take them past Cythera and off the map they know.

EXT. LOTUS LAND - DAY

A low coast. No fight. The Lotus-eaters come down with no thought of killing, and give the fruit of the lotus to three men sent inland. The three eat. They will not come back. They want only to stay and eat lotus with the people of the land, and to forget the way home.

(SOUND: a soft surf, flies in sweet pulp)

ULYSSES drives them to the ships weeping, binds them under the benches, and bids the rest go on board at once, lest more should taste the lotus. They smite the grey sea with their oars.

EXT. GOAT ISLAND - DAY

A wooded island in the offing, unpeopled, goats in the scrub. Opposite, across a strait, a mainland shore of caves and smoke. ULYSSES's twelve ships run into a harbour so still that no cable is needed. They hunt the goats. They sleep by the sea.

(SOUND: goats crashing in brush, a still harbour)

NARRATOR (V.O.)
We looked across at the land of the Cyclopes, a violent and lawless folk who neither plant nor plough, but leave all to the deathless gods.

Dawn. ULYSSES will not take all the ships. He mans his own with the picked crew, and carries a goat-skin of the dark, sweet wine the priest gave him, twelve measures of water to one of wine, so strong that no man could drink it neat.

EXT. CYCLOPS ISLAND - DAY

A cave close to the sea, tall laurels, a yard of rough stone and tall pines and oaks. Cheeses in racks. Lambs and kids penned by age. Bowls of whey. ULYSSES, dark-bearded, broad-shouldered, takes twelve of his best men and the skin of wine. The rest guard the ship.

(SOUND: sheep bells far inland, flies over whey)

His men beg him to take cheeses and kids and go. He will not. He wants to see the owner and whether he will give them hospitality.

They light a fire, eat, and wait.

INT. POLYPHEMUS CAVE - NIGHT

A high cave. Dusk comes in at the mouth, then is shut out. POLYPHEMUS, a giant Cyclops, one eye in his forehead, son of Neptune, drives in his flocks, heaves a slab against the door that two and twenty wagons could not shift, and milks his ewes, putting the lambs to each.

(SOUND: the slab grinding home, sheep bells, a voice like rock)

He sees them as he lights his fire. The flame shows how huge he is.

POLYPHEMUS
Strangers, who are you? Where do sail from? Are you traders, or do you sail the seas as rovers with your hands against every man, and every man's hand against you?

ULYSSES
We are Achaeans driven out of our course by contrary winds on our way home from Troy. We belong to the forces of Agamemnon, and are proud to think that we have sacked a city second to none.

He stands forward, small as a child before the giant.

ULYSSES (CONT'D)
We venture to approach you and make our prayer as suppliants, that you will show us some hospitality and direct us on our way. Have a fear of the gods, for we are your suppliants, and Jove takes all suppliants under his protection.

POLYPHEMUS
Stranger, you are a fool, or else you come from a long way off. Do you tell me to fear the gods? We Cyclopes do not care about Jove or any of your blessed gods, for we are ever so much stronger than they.

POLYPHEMUS (CONT'D)
I shall not spare either yourself or your companions out of any regard for Jove, unless I am in the humour for doing so. And now tell me where you left your ship.

ULYSSES sees the trap.

ULYSSES
Neptune broke my ship all to pieces, for he threw her onto the rocks at the headland and brought her in two by the waves. But I and those who are with me escaped the jaws of death.

The giant answers with no prayer. He snatches up two men, dashes their brains out on the ground as though they had been puppies, cuts them limb from limb, and makes his meal of them, leaving neither flesh nor marrow, washing it down with unmixed milk. The others lift their hands to Jove and stand helpless.

(SOUND: bone on stone, a thick chewing, men trying not to cry out)

POLYPHEMUS lies down among his flocks and sleeps. ULYSSES's hand goes to his sword, then stops: if he kills the giant, they will never move the slab.

Dawn comes even here. The Cyclops lights the fire, milks, then seizes two more men and makes his meal of them. He drives the ewes out, sets the slab again as easily as a man sets the lid on a quiver, and goes whistling to the pasture.

ULYSSES finds a club of green olive wood the giant had laid by, as big as the mast of a twenty-oared merchantman. He cuts off a fathom's length, his men smooth it, he sharpens the end and hardens it in the fire, and hides it under dung. They cast lots. Four men and ULYSSES himself will drive the stake.

Evening. The giant comes in with all his flocks, as if he smelled something. He milks. He seizes two more. Then ULYSSES fills a wooden bowl with the dark wine and goes to him.

ULYSSES
Cyclops, try this wine, now you have eaten your men's flesh, that you may see what kind of liquor we had on board my ship. I was bringing it to you as a drink-offering, in the hope that you would take compassion upon me and direct me home.

POLYPHEMUS takes it, drinks, and grins for more.

POLYPHEMUS
Give me more, and tell me your name at once. I want to make you a present that you will be glad to have. We have wine even in this country, for our grapes yield a good juice, but this is fit for an immortal.

ULYSSES pours again, and a third time. The giant's head goes loose.

ULYSSES
Cyclops, you ask my name and I will tell it you. Give me, then, the present you promised me. My name is Noman; this is what my father and mother and my friends have always called me.

POLYPHEMUS
Then I will eat all Noman's comrades before Noman himself, and will keep Noman for the last. This is the present that I will make him.

He reels and vomits wine and human flesh, then sprawls on his back, his great neck bent, sleep taking him. ULYSSES heats the stake till it glows. The four men lift it. He drives it into the one eye and leans on it, twisting, as a shipwright bores a timber with a drill that others twirl with a strap.

(SOUND: hiss of burning jelly, a scream that shakes the cave)

POLYPHEMUS wrenches the stake out, bellowing, and hurls it from him. He shouts to the other Cyclopes who live in the caves along the windy heights. They gather outside and ask what ails him, that he makes such a noise in the night and prevents them from sleeping. Is some one driving off his flocks, or trying to kill him by fraud or by force?

POLYPHEMUS
Noman is killing me by fraud! Noman is killing me by force!

NARRATOR (V.O.)
If no man is assaulting you, they said, and you are alone, there is no help for it; you must pray to your father Neptune.

They go. POLYPHEMUS, groaning in agony, gropes and takes the slab from the door, sits in the mouth, and spreads his hands to catch anyone who tries to get out among the sheep.

ULYSSES weaves the rams in threes, a man tied under each middle beast. The biggest ram of all he takes himself, and hangs under its shaggy belly, clinging to the fleece.

INT. POLYPHEMUS CAVE - DAWN

The ewes bleat for milking. POLYPHEMUS, one eye a wound, feels along the backs as the beasts go out. He does not find the men. Last of all the great ram comes. He lays a hand on it and speaks.

(SOUND: hooves on stone, the giant's breath raw)

POLYPHEMUS
My good ram, what is it that makes you the last to leave my cave this morning? You are not usually behind. You are always first.

POLYPHEMUS (CONT'D)
You are the first out to run and pasture, and first to come back to the fold at night. You must be grieved for your master's eye, which a villain has put out, with the help of his accursed drink, even Noman, who I declare has not yet got out of my cave.

POLYPHEMUS (CONT'D)
If you could understand and talk, you would tell me where he is hiding. I would dash his brains upon the ground, and it would ease my heart of the trouble that Noman has brought me.

He lets the ram go. ULYSSES clings underneath until they are out of reach, then looses himself and his men.

EXT. CYCLOPS ISLAND - DAY

They drive the fat rams to the ship. The crew, left behind, would cry out. ULYSSES frowns them down. They take the beasts aboard, sit to the oars, and smite the sea. When they are as far out as a man's shout will carry, ULYSSES will not keep still.

(SOUND: oars, then his voice across water)

ULYSSES
Cyclops, you should have taken better measure of your man before eating up his comrades in your cave. You are punished now, you wretch, for having devoured your guests. Jove and the other gods have punished you.

POLYPHEMUS breaks off the peak of a great mountain and hurls it. It falls in front of the ship. The wave drives them back toward the land. They pole off with the oars. ULYSSES's men beg him not to enrage the giant again. He will not hear.

ULYSSES
Cyclops, if any one asks you who it was that put your eye out and spoiled your beauty, say it was the valiant warrior Ulysses, son of Laertes, who lives in Ithaca.

POLYPHEMUS groans with a sick pain.

POLYPHEMUS
I remember now the old prophecy. A great man named Telemus, a soothsayer, told me that I should lose my sight at the hands of Ulysses. I thought he would have been a tall handsome man, and of great strength.

POLYPHEMUS (CONT'D)
Instead of which he has proved to be a paltry little fellow, who has managed to blind me by taking advantage of me in my drink. Come here, Ulysses, that I may make you presents to show my hospitality, and urge Neptune to help you forward.

ULYSSES
I wish I could be as sure of killing you outright and sending you down to the house of Hades, as I am that it will take more than Neptune to cure that eye of yours.

POLYPHEMUS lifts his hands to the starry heaven.

POLYPHEMUS
Hear me, great Neptune, if I am indeed your own true-begotten son. Grant that Ulysses may never reach his home alive.

POLYPHEMUS (CONT'D)
Or if he must get back to his friends at last, let him do so late and in sore plight, after losing all his men, in the ship of strangers, and find trouble in his house.

The sea heaves as if it heard. He hurls a second rock. It falls behind them. The wave carries the ship on, to the island where the other vessels wait.

(SOUND: a second hill-peak hitting water, the sea lifting the hull)

EXT. GOAT ISLAND - NIGHT

The other crews weep to see them, then weep again for the men who are not there. They divide the sheep so that none may go short. The ram is given to ULYSSES alone. He sacrifices it on the shore to Jove, dark-clouded son of Saturn. The god will not accept the offering.

(SOUND: a ram's last breath, fire that will not please heaven)

They feast on the goats and the sheep. Wine is not wanting. When the sun goes down and it comes on dark, they camp upon the beach. When the child of morning, rosy-fingered Dawn, appears, ULYSSES bids his men on board and loose the hawsers. They take their places and smite the grey sea with their oars.

So they sail on with sorrow in their hearts, but glad to have escaped death though they have lost their comrades.

