EXT. AEOLUS ISLAND - DAY

An island that rides as if it floated, iron-bound with a wall of bronze. Inside, a smooth cliff and a house of polished stone. ULYSSES, dark-bearded, broad-shouldered, a captain among his remaining men, is received by the steward of the winds and by his twelve children, six daughters married to six sons, who feast all day.

(SOUND: wind hitting bronze, then a closed hall, a lyre)

NARRATOR (V.O.)
Thence we went on to the Aeolian island where lives Aeolus son of Hippotas, dear to the immortal gods.

They stay a whole month. ULYSSES tells of Troy, the Cicons, the lotus, the Cyclops. At parting, Aeolus gives him a bag of ox-hide, nine years old, with all the contrary winds tied inside, and a west wind to take them home. ULYSSES stows the bag under the thwarts and will not let another man touch it.

EXT. OPEN SEA - NIGHT

Nine days they sail. On the tenth, the fires of Ithaca show on the horizon. ULYSSES, exhausted, holds the sheet himself and will not give it to a shipmate. Sleep takes him at last.

(SOUND: a soft home-wind, then whispering at the bag)

His men put their heads together. They say he is bringing gold and silver from Aeolus, and that it is not fair. They open the bag.

All the winds leap out. The ship is flung about. The men weep. ULYSSES wakes, thinks of throwing himself into the sea, then endures, and covers his face while the storm drives them back the way they came.

(SOUND: the bag ripping, a scream of wind, canvas striking)

EXT. AEOLUS ISLAND - DAY

The same bronze wall. They land and go up again, sit on the threshold, and ask for help a second time. Aeolus stares.

(SOUND: wind along the bronze, a door not fully opened)

NARRATOR (V.O.)
Get you gone, he said, vilest of mankind. It is not for me to help a man whom the gods hate. Be off, for you come here as one abhorred of heaven.

They row, worn out, with no more gift of wind.

EXT. LAESTRYGONIAN HARBOUR - DAY

Six days and nights they pull. On the seventh a fair-seeming fjord, cliffs on either hand, a narrow mouth. All the ships but ULYSSES's own enter and moor close together. He keeps his vessel outside, made fast to a rock, and climbs a headland. No ploughland. Only smoke.

(SOUND: a still inner harbour, then a well-rope)

He sends two men and a runner inland. They meet a girl drawing water, daughter to the king of that place. She shows them her father's house. The mother is huge as a mountain peak and fills them with horror. She calls her husband from the place of assembly. He seizes one man and makes his meal of him.

The other two run. The king raises a hue and cry. Giants come down from the rocks. They spear men like fish and carry them home for dinner. They smash the trapped hulls with boulders.

(SOUND: timber bursting, men screaming, stones into ships)

ULYSSES cuts his own cable and shouts to his crew to row for their lives. They pull, afraid of death, and the ship runs clear of the cliffs. The other eleven ships are gone.

EXT. CIRCE PALACE GROUNDS - DAY

Aeaea. A low island, oak and stone, smoke from a house in a clearing. ULYSSES's last ship beaches. Two days and two nights they lie on the shore, eating their hearts out with grief and weariness.

(SOUND: a thin surf, men not speaking)

On the third dawn ULYSSES takes his spear and sword and goes inland. A great stag, high-antlered, is coming down to the river to drink, driven by the heat. He hits it in the spine. It falls, screaming. He makes a rope of withies, slings the beast over his shoulders, and staggers back to the ship. He flings it down and cheers his men with the sight of food.

They feast till sundown. Next morning he stands among them, dark-bearded, the captain still.

ULYSSES
My friends, we do not know east from west. We must think what we had better do. I saw smoke rising from the middle of the island. Shall we send to see who lives there?

The men weep, remembering the Cyclops and the Laestrygonians. He divides them into two companies. He leads one, a trusted mate the other. They shake lots in a helmet. The mate's lot jumps out.

Twenty-two men go inland, weeping. They find wolves and lions in a glade that fawn like dogs. Within, CIRCE, a beautiful dread goddess, sings at her loom in a voice a goddess might have, weaving fine, graceful, dazzling work.

The men call. She opens the shining doors and bids them in. They all follow except the mate, who suspects a trap and stays outside. She seats them, mixes cheese, honey, meal, and Pramnian wine, and drugs it. When they have drunk she strikes them with her wand.

They become pigs, with pigs' heads, bristles, and grunts, but the minds of men. She shuts them in sties and throws them acorns and beech-mast.

(SOUND: her song, a wand-stroke, panicked squealing)

The mate runs back to the ship, so stricken he cannot speak at first. Then he tells what he has seen: the house, the woman, the men who went in and never came out, the pigs.

The others would go at once to the work. ULYSSES slings his silver-studded sword about his shoulders, takes his bow, and orders the mate to lead him back. The man clasps his knees and begs to be left at the ship. ULYSSES bids him stay and eat and drink. He goes alone.

On the way, in the oak-wood, the god Mercury meets him in the likeness of a young man with the first down on his lip. He asks where ULYSSES is going, and tells him of the pigs. He pulls a plant from the ground: black at the root, a flower like milk. The gods call it moly. It is hard for mortals to dig. He gives it, and tells him how Circe will mix the cup, and how he must rush upon her with his sword and make her swear.

The young likeness goes up into the oak-wood.

INT. CIRCE HALL - DAY

CIRCE, lovely and terrible, receives ULYSSES, seats him in a chair silver-studded, and mixes the cup. He drinks. She strikes. He draws his sword and rushes at her as though he would kill her. She shrieks, clasps his knees, and weeps.

(SOUND: cup falling, her cry, bronze clearing the scabbard)

CIRCE
Who and whence are you? From what place and people have you come? I am amazed at you. You have drunk my drugs and are not bewitched.

She looks up at him.

CIRCE (CONT'D)
Never yet did any man taste my cup and not fall under it. Surely you must be the Ulysses of whom Mercury used to tell me, the man who was to come on his way home from Troy.

CIRCE (CONT'D)
Sheathe your sword and let us go to bed, that we may make friends and learn to trust each other.

ULYSSES
Circe, how can you expect me to be friendly with you when you have turned my men into pigs? You have me here in your house. You will unman me and make me fit for nothing.

ULYSSES (CONT'D)
I will not go to bed with you unless you will first take a solemn oath that you mean me no mischief.

She swears at once, the oath he makes her take. When she has sworn, he puts up his sword. Her handmaids—nymphs of the springs and groves and sacred rivers—prepare a bath, a chair, a table, bread, and meats. He will not eat. He sits thinking of his men.

CIRCE
Ulysses, why do you sit like that as though you were dumb, eating your heart out and never touching food or drink? Is it that you still distrust me? I have sworn, and I will not hurt you.

ULYSSES
Circe, no man with any sense would taste food or drink before he had got his men out of you and seen them with his own eyes. If you want me to eat and drink, set them free.

She takes her wand, goes to the sty, and drives them out. They stand before her as pigs. She rubs another drug upon them. The bristles fall. They become men again, younger, taller, more handsome than before. They know him. They cling to him and weep, and the house rings with their sobbing. CIRCE herself is moved.

CIRCE
Ulysses, noble son of Laertes, go back now to your ship with your men, and bring the rest of your comrades here.

He goes. At the shore his remaining crew weep for joy as calves about their mothers. The mate still fears a trap and would have them sail at once. ULYSSES frowns him down. They all go up to CIRCE's house except that one man, who follows at last.

She bathes them, anoints them, sets a feast. So they stay, day after day, until a year has run out, feasting on meat without stint and mellow wine.

INT. CIRCE HALL - LATER

The same hall, the same dread goddess, the year visible only in the men's faces. ULYSSES's comrades take him aside.

(SOUND: a low fire, wine that has lost its celebration)

NARRATOR (V.O.)
Sir, they said, it is time you began thinking about going home, if it is heaven's will that you should be saved and return to your house and to your own country.

He sits on CIRCE's bed and clasps her knees.

ULYSSES
Circe, keep the promise you made me about sending me home. I want to get back, and my men will not let me rest. They wear me out with their lamentation whenever you are not by.

CIRCE
Ulysses, noble son of Laertes, you shall no longer stay in my house against your will. But you must first complete another journey, and go to the house of Hades and of dread Proserpine.

ULYSSES's heart is broken. He sits on the bed and weeps. He has no longer any wish to live or see the light of the sun.

ULYSSES
Who shall guide us upon this journey? No man ever yet sailed to the house of Hades in a black ship.

CIRCE
Do not trouble about a guide. Raise your mast, set your white sails, and sit quite still. The north wind will blow you there.

CIRCE (CONT'D)
When you have crossed the river of Ocean, you will see a wild coast and the groves of Proserpine—tall poplars and willows that shed their fruit before it ripens.

CIRCE (CONT'D)
Beach your ship by Ocean's eddying stream and go on, into the house of Hades. There is a rock, and the meeting of two roaring rivers.

CIRCE (CONT'D)
Draw your sword, dig a trench as long and as broad as a man can make in a day, and pour a drink-offering to all the dead—first with honey and milk, then with wine, and thirdly with water, and sprinkle white barley meal over the whole.

CIRCE (CONT'D)
Then offer many prayers to the poor feeble ghosts, and promise that when you get back to Ithaca you will sacrifice a barren heifer, and will fill the pyre with treasure.

CIRCE (CONT'D)
If you would have Teiresias speak to you, promise him a black ram, the finest in all your flocks. When you have finished your prayers, offer a ram and a black ewe, bending their heads towards Erebus.

CIRCE (CONT'D)
Then the ghosts will come up in crowds. Bid your men flay the sheep and burn them, and pray to the gods, to mighty Hades and to dread Proserpine.

CIRCE (CONT'D)
Draw your sword and sit there, and do not let the poor feckless ghosts come near the blood till Teiresias shall have answered your questions.

CIRCE (CONT'D)
The prophet will presently come to you, and will tell you about your voyage, and how you may get back to your own country over the teeming deep.

Dawn is already near. She puts on them a shirt and cloak each. She wears a long robe, graceful and lovely, a golden girdle, and a veil over her head.

EXT. AEAEA SHORE - DAY

The ship. The men are glad, thinking they are bound for home. ULYSSES tells them they must go to the house of Hades to consult Teiresias. Their hearts are broken. They sit down where they are and weep, tearing their hair, but they do not mend matters by crying.

(SOUND: men groaning, a rope on a ram)

One of them, the youngest, no great fighter, has gone up onto the roof of CIRCE's house to sleep in the cool, heavy with wine. He hears the noise of the departure, starts up, forgets the long ladder, falls headlong, and breaks his neck. His soul goes down to the house of Hades. They leave him where he lies.

When they reach the sea shore, weeping and lamenting their fate, CIRCE brings the ram and the ewe, and they make them fast hard by the ship. She passes through the midst of them without their knowing it, for who can see the comings and goings of a god, if the god does not wish to be seen?

