EXT. OPEN SEA - DAY

The black ship runs dead aft on a wind CIRCE sent, that stays steadily with them and keeps the sail full. ULYSSES, dark-bearded, broad-shouldered, sits at the steering-oar. The ram and the black ewe huddle amidships. His men weep and do not speak.

(SOUND: a fair wind in canvas, the sheep shifting)

NARRATOR (V.O.)
Then, when we had got down to the sea shore we drew our ship into the water and got her mast and sails into her; we also put the sheep on board and took our places, weeping and in great distress of mind.

All day the sail is stretched. The sun sets. The ways of night and day come near. They reach the deep-flowing Oceanus, and the land of the Cimmerians, wrapped in mist and cloud. The sun never looks down on them with his rays, neither when he climbs the starry heavens nor when he turns again to earth, but a cimmerian darkness is over the wretched people.

They beach the ship, take out the sheep, and go along the stream of Ocean until they come to the place CIRCE described.

EXT. SHORE OF OCEANUS - DAY

Mist. A wild coast. Tall poplars and willows that shed their fruit unripe. A rock where two roaring rivers meet. ULYSSES draws his sword and digs a trench as long and as broad as a man can make with one pull of the arm. He pours the drink-offering to all the dead: first honey and milk, then wine, then water, and sprinkles white barley meal over the whole.

(SOUND: iron in wet earth, liquid hitting dirt, two rivers roaring)

He prays to the poor feeble ghosts, and promises that when he gets back to Ithaca he will sacrifice a barren heifer, the best he has, and will fill the pyre with treasure, and to TEIRESIAS alone a black ram, the finest in all his flocks.

When he has finished his prayers he cuts the throats of the ram and the black ewe, bending their heads toward Erebus. Dark blood runs into the trench.

The souls of the dead come up out of Erebus: brides, unmarried youths, old men who have suffered much, girls with hearts new-torn, and men killed in battle, still in their blood-stained armour. They flock from every side about the trench with a terrible cry.

(SOUND: a thin enormous wailing, blood dripping)

ULYSSES bids his men flay the sheep that lie slaughtered, killed by the pitiless sword, and burn them, and pray to the gods, to mighty Hades and to dread Proserpine. He himself draws his sword and sits, and will not let the poor feckless ghosts come near the blood.

First comes the soul of Elpenor, their comrade, not yet laid under the earth, for they left his body unwept and unburied in CIRCE's house, and another need drove them. ULYSSES weeps when he sees him, and his heart is touched.

ULYSSES
Elpenor, how did you come down here into this gloom and darkness? You have got here on foot, quicker than I have, with my black ship.

ELPENOR, young, a ghost still in the shape of the man who fell from the roof, wrings his hands.

ELPENOR
Sir, it was unmixed wine, and the devilish luck that follows it, that undid me. I lay down to sleep on the roof of Circe's house, and I never thought of coming down again by the great staircase.

ELPENOR (CONT'D)
I fell headlong from the roof, and my neck was broken, and my soul came down to the house of Hades.

ELPENOR (CONT'D)
And now I beseech you by all those whom you have left behind you, by your wife, and by the father who brought you up when you were a child, and by Telemachus who is the one hope of your house.

ELPENOR (CONT'D)
Do not go away and leave me behind unwept and unburied when you leave this place, or I may bring heaven's anger upon you. Burn me with whatever armour I have.

ELPENOR (CONT'D)
Raise a barrow for me on the shore of the grey sea, in memory of an unhappy man, that those who see it may tell the tale hereafter. Plant upon my tomb the oar I used to row with when I was alive and among my comrades.

ULYSSES
My poor unhappy friend, I will do all that you have asked of me.

They sit and talk sadly, ULYSSES on one side of the blood, the ghost on the other. Then the soul of ULYSSES's mother, ANTICLEA, daughter of Autolycus, comes up. He had left her alive when he set out for Troy. He weeps, and is touched to the heart, but even so he will not let her come near the blood till he has asked TEIRESIAS his questions.

The Theban prophet comes with a gold sceptre in his hand. He knows ULYSSES, and drinks of the dark blood.

TEIRESIAS
Ulysses, noble son of Laertes, why, poor man, have you left the light of day and come down to visit the dead in this sad place? Stand back from the trench and put your sword up, that I may drink of the blood and tell you the truth.

ULYSSES sheathes his sword. The prophet drinks.

TEIRESIAS
You want to know about your return. Neptune is still angry with you for having blinded his son. Nevertheless you may yet reach home, though not without suffering, if you will restrain yourself and your men.

TEIRESIAS (CONT'D)
When your ship first leaves the waters of the open sea and comes to the island of Thrinacia, you will find the cattle and sheep of the Sun-god.

TEIRESIAS (CONT'D)
If you leave them unharmed and think of nothing but of getting home, you may yet after much hardship reach Ithaca. If you harm them, then I forewarn you of the destruction both of your ship and of your comrades.

TEIRESIAS (CONT'D)
Even though you may yourself escape, you will return late, in evil plight, after losing all your men, in another man's ship, and you will find trouble in your house.

TEIRESIAS (CONT'D)
Insolent men eating up your estate and paying court to your wife. You will have to avenge yourself upon them by craft or in open fight.

The gold sceptre does not shake.

TEIRESIAS (CONT'D)
When you have killed these suitors in your own house, go out, taking an oar, until you come to a country where the people have never heard of the sea, and do not even mix salt with their food.

TEIRESIAS (CONT'D)
They know nothing about ships, nor oars that are as the wings of a ship. I will give you this certain token which cannot escape your notice.

TEIRESIAS (CONT'D)
A wayfarer will meet you and will say it must be a winnowing shovel that you have got upon your shoulder. Then fix the oar in the ground and sacrifice a ram, a bull, and a boar to Neptune.

TEIRESIAS (CONT'D)
Then go home and offer hecatombs to all the gods in heaven one after the other. As for yourself, death shall come to you from the sea, quite gently, and shall take you when you are full of years and peace of mind, and your people shall be happy around you.

TEIRESIAS (CONT'D)
I have told you the truth as it was shown to me.

ULYSSES
Teiresias, these are the fates which heaven has spun for me. And now tell me about my mother, who is sitting there by the blood without saying a word.

ULYSSES (CONT'D)
She does not know me. Tell me, is there no way of making her know me?

TEIRESIAS
Any ghost that you let taste of the blood will talk with you like a reasonable being, but if you do not let them have any blood they will go away again.

The prophet goes back into the house of Hades. ULYSSES keeps his seat till his mother comes up and drinks the black blood. She knows him then at once, and speaks.

ANTICLEA
My son, how did you come down to this abode of darkness while you are still alive? It is a hard thing for the living to see these places.

ANTICLEA (CONT'D)
Are you still going about from city to city, and have you not yet got back to Ithaca, nor seen your wife in your own house?

ULYSSES
Mother, I had to come down here to consult the ghost of the Theban prophet Teiresias. I have not yet been near the Achaean land, nor set foot on my native country.

ULYSSES (CONT'D)
I have been wandering all this time under great trouble, from the very first day that I set out with Agamemnon for Ilius, the land of noble steeds, to fight the Trojans.

ULYSSES (CONT'D)
But tell me how you came by your end. Did you have a long illness, or did heaven vouchsafe you a gentle easy passage to eternity?

ULYSSES (CONT'D)
Tell me also about my father, and the son whom I left behind me. Is my property still in their hands, or has some one else got hold of it, who thinks that I shall not return?

ULYSSES (CONT'D)
Tell me again what my wife intends doing, and in what mind she is. Does she live with my son and guard my estate securely, or has she made the best match she could and married again?

ANTICLEA
Your wife still remains in your house, but she is in great distress of mind and spends her whole time in tears both night and day.

ANTICLEA (CONT'D)
No one as yet has got possession of your estate, and Telemachus holds your lands undisturbed. He has to entertain largely, as of course he must, considering his position as a magistrate.

ANTICLEA (CONT'D)
Your father stays out in the country, and never goes near the town. He has no comfortable bed nor bedding; in the winter he sleeps on the floor in front of the fire with the labourers, and lives in dirt and rags.

ANTICLEA (CONT'D)
When summer comes and the harvest, he has a few boughs of a vine to sleep under in any part of the garden that he can find. He is very unhappy, and nurses his sorrow.

ANTICLEA (CONT'D)
As for my own end it was in this wise: heaven did not take me swiftly and painlessly in my own house, nor was I attacked by any illness such as those that generally wear people out.

ANTICLEA (CONT'D)
It was my longing to know what had become of you, my glorious Ulysses, and my anxiety for you, that cost me my life.

ULYSSES wants to take her in his arms. Three times he springs toward her, meaning to hold her; three times she flits from his grasp like a shadow or a dream. The pain that he feels is even greater.

(SOUND: his hands closing on air, a sob he cannot keep)

ULYSSES
Mother, why do you not stay still when I would embrace you? If we could throw our arms around one another we might find sad comfort in the house of Hades.

ULYSSES (CONT'D)
Does Proserpine want to lay a still further load of grief upon me by mocking me with a phantom only?

ANTICLEA
My son, the most ill-fated of all mankind, it is not Proserpine that is beguiling you. All people are like this when they are dead.

ANTICLEA (CONT'D)
The sinews no longer hold the flesh and bones together. These perish in the fierceness of consuming fire as soon as life has left the body, and the soul flits away as though it were a dream.

ANTICLEA (CONT'D)
Now, however, go back to the light of day as soon as you can, and note all these things that you may tell them to your wife hereafter.

Proserpine then sends up the wives and daughters of great men. ULYSSES holds them off with his sword and will let them drink only one at a time. They come: Tyro, Antiope, Alcmene, Epicaste who married her own son, Chloris, Leda, Iphimedeia, Phaedra, Procris, Ariadne, Maera, Clymene, Eriphyle who took gold for her own husband's life. He tells the Phaeacians he will not name every one.

NARRATOR (V.O.)
Then the ghost of Agamemnon son of Atreus came up, sorrowing, and round him were gathered the ghosts of those who had perished with him in the house of Aegisthus.

The ghost drinks the blood and knows him. They weep. Agamemnon tells of the feast and the slaughter, and of Clytemnestra, who would not even close her husband's eyes. He asks of his son. ULYSSES can tell him nothing. The ghost warns him to come home in secret, and not openly, for there is no trusting in women.

NARRATOR (V.O.)
Then I saw Achilles, and Patroclus, and Antilochus, and Ajax, who was the handsomest and best all-round man among the Danaans after the son of Peleus.

Achilles drinks and asks of his son and of his father. He will not be comforted for death.

NARRATOR (V.O.)
Say not a word, he answered, in death's favour. I would rather be a paid servant in a poor man's house and be above ground than king of kings among the dead.

When ULYSSES tells him that Neoptolemus fought like a hero at Troy and boarded the wooden horse, and was not hurt, the ghost of the swift-footed one goes off with great strides through the meadow of asphodel, rejoicing.

The ghost of Ajax stands apart, still angry for the armour of Achilles that the Trojans and Pallas MINERVA awarded to ULYSSES. ULYSSES speaks gently and would make peace. Ajax will not answer, and goes away into Erebus after the other ghosts.

ULYSSES sees Minos judging the dead with his golden sceptre; Orion driving the wild beasts he killed in the lonely hills; Tityus, earth-born, spread over nine roods while two vultures tear his liver; Tantalus standing in water that shrinks from his chin, under trees that toss their fruit away; Sisyphus shouldering his shameless stone up a hill, the sweat pouring from his limbs, the stone rolling down again.

Last he sees the phantom of Heracles—the man himself feasts with the immortal gods—and the phantom knows him, and speaks of the labour of fetching the hound of Hades. Then the innumerable dead press in with a terrible cry. Pale fear takes ULYSSES, lest Proserpine should send up from the house of Hades the head of that awful monster Gorgon.

(SOUND: the wailing rising, his sword shaking in his hand)

He hastens back to his ship and orders his men on board at once and to loose the hawsers.

EXT. RIVER OCEANUS - DAY

They embark and take their places. The ship goes down the stream of the river Oceanus. They have to row at first. Mist on the water. The two rivers fall behind.

(SOUND: oars in a black stream, then a fair wind finding the sail)

Presently a fair wind springs up.

