EXT. AEAEA SHORE - DAY

The ship is drawn onto the sands of Aeaea, where there is dawn and sunrise as in other places. ULYSSES, dark-bearded, broad-shouldered, and his men sleep by the hull until day. CIRCE, the beautiful dread goddess, comes down with her maids, bringing bread, meat, and mellow wine.

(SOUND: a fair morning surf, cups set in sand)

They eat. Then ULYSSES sends men for the body of Elpenor. They cut firewood where the headland runs out over the sea, burn the corpse and the armour, raise a barrow, and plant upon the tomb the oar he used to pull.

CIRCE takes ULYSSES by the hand and leads him apart from the others.

CIRCE
So you have done your going down to the house of Hades. You must listen now to what I am going to say. Heaven itself, indeed, will recall it to your recollection.

CIRCE (CONT'D)
First you will come to the Sirens, who enchant all who come near them. If any one unwarily draws in too close and hears the singing of the Sirens, his wife and children will never welcome him home again.

CIRCE (CONT'D)
The Sirens sit in a green field and warble him to death with the sweetness of their song. There is a great heap of dead men's bones lying all around, with the flesh still rotting off them.

CIRCE (CONT'D)
Therefore pass these Sirens by, and stop your men's ears with wax that none of them may hear. If you like you can listen yourself, but you must get them to bind you as you stand upright on a cross-piece half way up the mast.

CIRCE (CONT'D)
And they must lash the rope's ends to the mast itself, that you may have the pleasure of listening. If you beg and pray the men to unloose you, they must bind you faster.

She marks the sea with a finger in the air.

CIRCE (CONT'D)
When your crew have taken you past these Sirens, I cannot say for certain which you will take, for you will have to consider for yourself.

CIRCE (CONT'D)
I will lay the two alternatives before you, and you must consider them. On the one hand there are some overhanging rocks against which the deep blue waves of Amphitrite beat with a terrific roar.

CIRCE (CONT'D)
One rock reaches into the sky, a dark cloud upon it that never leaves. No man could climb it, not even if he had twenty hands and feet, for it is as smooth as though it had been polished.

CIRCE (CONT'D)
In the middle of it there is a large cavern, looking West. You must take your ship this way, but the cave is so high up that not even the stoutest archer could send an arrow into it.

CIRCE (CONT'D)
Inside it Scylla sits and yelps with a voice that you might take to be that of a young hound, but in truth she is a dreadful monster. No one, not even a god, could face her without being terror-struck.

CIRCE (CONT'D)
She has twelve mis-shapen feet, and six necks of the most prodigious length. On each of these is a frightful head with three rows of teeth. She lurks inside her den and fishes from it.

CIRCE (CONT'D)
No ship ever yet got past her without losing some men. The other rock is lower, and there is a great fig tree growing on it. Under it Charybdis sucks the dark water down.

CIRCE (CONT'D)
Three times in the day does she vomit it out, and three times she sucks it down. See that you be not there when she is sucking, for not even Neptune could save you.

CIRCE (CONT'D)
Hug the Scylla side, and let your ship go on her way as fast as you can, for you had better lose six men than your whole crew.

ULYSSES
I will not disguise it from you. Cannot I somehow manage to escape Charybdis myself, and at the same time keep Scylla off when she is trying to harm my men?

CIRCE
You dare-devil. You are always wanting to fight somebody or something. Will you not bow your head under the cruel necessity?

CIRCE (CONT'D)
Against Scylla there is no defence. Flight is the only thing for it. If you delay to arm yourself by the shipside, you may make another spring and catch another six.

CIRCE (CONT'D)
Then you will come to the island of the two pastures. The cattle and sheep of the Sun are there, seven herds of cattle and seven flocks of sheep, fifty in each, and they never die.

CIRCE (CONT'D)
If you leave these flocks unharmed and think of nothing but of getting home, you may yet after much hardship reach Ithaca. If you harm them, I forewarn you of the destruction both of your ship and of your men.

She sends them to the ship. The north wind takes the sail.

EXT. SIREN SEA - DAY

A green meadow on a low island. Bones and rotting flesh in the grass. Two women sit there. ULYSSES, dark-bearded, stands upright on a cross-piece half way up the mast, lashed with rope's ends to the timber. His men have beeswax kneaded warm in their ears. He has told them only of the Sirens, and that they must bind him tighter if he begs.

(SOUND: oars, then a singing so sweet the sea itself seems to lean)

NARRATOR (V.O.)
Come here, they sang, renowned Ulysses, honour to the Achaean name, and listen to our two voices.

NARRATOR (V.O.)
No one ever sailed past us without staying to hear the enchanting sweetness of our song, and going on his way a wiser man. We know all the pains that the Greeks and Trojans took on the plain of Troy.

ULYSSES nods and begs with his brows to be set free. The men bind him tighter and bend to the oars until the song dies astern.

EXT. SCYLLA STRAIT - DAY

Smoke, and a great wave, and the roar of breakers. The men let the oars fall, frightened. ULYSSES goes up and down, cheering them, bidding the steersman hug the cliff and keep clear of the smoke and the wave.

(SOUND: water roaring down, then up, a hound-yelp from high rock)

He does not tell them of SCYLLA. He arms and stands in the bows, forgetting CIRCE's word that fighting will not help. They watch CHARYBDIS: she sucks the salt water down till they see the sand and the rock at the bottom, then vomits it out again as a cauldron boils on a great fire, spray on the cliffs.

While they stare at the whirlpool, SCYLLA darts her heads from the cave. She takes six men, the strongest, from the ship, like a fisherman flipping fish on the land. They call to ULYSSES by name in their death-agony. He sees their hands and feet still moving as they are drawn up, and hears them scream.

(SOUND: six shrieks, then chewing in the high cave)

It is the most pitiful sight he sees in all his voyaging.

EXT. THRINACIA SHORE - DAY

The island of the Sun. Cattle and sheep of the god Hyperion, unharmed, immortal, herded by nymphs. ULYSSES hears the lowing and the bleating and remembers TEIRESIAS and CIRCE. He would pass by.

(SOUND: cattle lowing, a south wind rising)

His men are exhausted. One of them speaks for the rest, calling him hard, saying they cannot row all night, and that they should land, cook supper, and put out at dawn. ULYSSES knows the god is brewing mischief. He makes them swear a strong oath not to touch a head of the cattle or sheep, but to eat the food CIRCE gave them.

They beach in a sheltered harbour, near sweet water. They beach the ship and eat. Then they weep for their poor comrades whom SCYLLA ate. When they have done weeping, they sleep.

In the third watch the south wind falls on them and blows for a whole month. They eat the last of CIRCE's meat and wine. Hunger takes them. They hunt birds and fish with bent hooks, and their bellies pinch them.

ULYSSES goes inland to pray to the gods, hoping one will show him a way. He washes his hands where there is a shelter from the wind, and prays. They shed a sweet sleep upon his eyelids.

While he sleeps, the same man who spoke before gathers the others.

NARRATOR (V.O.)
Listen to me, he said, my poor comrades. All deaths are bad enough, but there is none so bad as famine. Let us take the best of the Sun's cattle and offer sacrifice.

NARRATOR (V.O.)
If we ever get back to Ithaca, we will build a rich temple to the Sun. If he is angry and wrecks us, I would rather drown at once than pinch to death upon a desert island.

They drive in the best of the cattle. They stand round them and pray, using water for wine, for they have no wine left. They slaughter, flay, cut out the thigh bones, wrap them in fat, and lay raw meat upon them. They have no barley meal. They roast the inward meats and spit the rest.

ULYSSES wakes and walks back toward the ship. The savour of roast fat meets him. He cries out to the immortals.

ULYSSES
Father Jove, and all you other gods who live in everlasting bliss, you have done me a cruel mischief by the sleep into which you have sent me. My men have plotted mischief behind my back.

Lampetie of the long robes runs to the Sun with the news. The Sun takes it to the other gods.

NARRATOR (V.O.)
Father Jove, the Sun said, I shall go down into Hades and shine among the dead, if the cattle are not paid for.

NARRATOR (V.O.)
Sun, Jove answered, shine as you always do over the deathless gods and mortal men. I will shatter their ship with a thunderbolt when they are on the open sea.

The hides begin to crawl. The flesh on the spits bellows, both roast and raw. There is a sound as of cattle.

(SOUND: hides dragging themselves, meat bellowing on the spits)

For six days ULYSSES's men feast on the best of the Sun's cattle. On the seventh the wind falls. They put to sea.

EXT. OPEN SEA - DAY

No land. When they are out of sight of the island, Jove sends a dark cloud. The sea turns black under it. The wind hits. The forestays of the mast break. The mast falls aft and strikes the steersman, so that he drops from the deck like a diver. Thunderbolt on the ship. She fills with sulphur. The men are washed overboard like sea-crows, and Jove takes from them their day of returning.

(SOUND: stays snapping, a thunderbolt, men in the water)

ULYSSES goes down the vessel till the sea has stripped her from about him. He lashes keel to mast with the backstay, and sits on them, driven before the wind.

EXT. SCYLLA STRAIT - DAY

Night. The wreck runs back into the strait. ULYSSES, alone on the lashed timbers, sees CHARYBDIS sucking down the salt water. He springs for the fig tree, catches it, and clings like a bat, with nowhere to plant his feet and no way to climb. He holds on until she vomits the mast and keel up again, at the hour a man rises from the assembly to go to supper. He drops, straddles the timbers, and paddles with his hands.

(SOUND: the whirlpool drinking the sea, then the wreck coughing up)

SCYLLA does not see him. He would not have escaped death if she had.

EXT. OPEN SEA - NIGHT

Nine days the gods carry him on the bare timbers. His skin is salt and broken. On the tenth night land shows: Ogygia, the woodland isle, the cave, the vine.

(SOUND: timber under a man's chest, a long fetch of sea)

INT. ALCINOUS PALACE - HALL - NIGHT

Torches. The bronze walls. ULYSSES, hyacinth-haired again in the telling, sits among the Phaeacians. The tale is done.

(SOUND: a torch-wick, the hall not breathing)

ULYSSES
Hence I was carried along for nine days till on the tenth night the gods stranded me on the Ogygian island, where dwells the great and powerful goddess Calypso.

ULYSSES (CONT'D)
She took me in and was kind to me, but I need say no more about this, for I told you and your noble wife all about it yesterday, and I hate saying the same thing over and over again.

