INT. ALCINOUS PALACE - HALL - NIGHT

The covered cloister holds its peace, enthralled by the charm of his story. ALCINOUS, king of the Phaeacians, is the first to speak. ULYSSES, hyacinth-haired, sits still among the gifts already given.

(SOUND: a torch settling, no one else breathing)

ALCINOUS
Ulysses, now that you have reached my house I doubt not you will get home without further misadventure no matter how much you have suffered.

ALCINOUS (CONT'D)
To you others, however, who come here night after night to drink my choicest wine and listen to my bard, I give this bidding.

ALCINOUS (CONT'D)
We are used to sending men home, and have sent many another. Clothes, gold, and all the other presents that you have brought here lie ready for our guest.

ALCINOUS (CONT'D)
Let us now each one of us give him a large tripod and a cauldron. We will recoup ourselves by the levy we shall make upon the people. It would be too hard on one man to give so much.

They agree, go home to bed, and sleep.

EXT. PHAEACIAN ASSEMBLY - DAWN

Rosy-fingered Dawn. The twelve kings bring the bronze down to the ship that has never yet made a voyage. ALCINOUS himself goes on board and stows the gifts under the benches, that the crew may not be hindered when they row. They return to the house and make another feast.

(SOUND: bronze set under thwarts, a last ox falling)

The sun goes down. ULYSSES speaks to the queen, who sits spinning by the fire.

ULYSSES
O queen, fare well, and remember me when I am gone, for you have had much trouble on my account. I am going home, and I pray heaven I may find my wife still there with her friends and my son.

A herald leads him to the ship. Arete sends women after him with a change of clothes, bread, and wine. They lay a rug and a linen sheet on the deck, that he may sleep sound. He goes on board and lies down in silence. Each man takes his place on the benches. They unmoor and throw the water back with their oars.

EXT. OPEN SEA - NIGHT

The ship runs like a team of four stallions under the whip, hoofs lifted high. So does the Phaeacian vessel lift her stern and cut the waves. ULYSSES sleeps a sleep so deep, so sweet, and so like death that he does not stir, a man who has suffered much.

(SOUND: oars in perfect time, a hull that barely speaks)

The brightest star that heralds day rises. The ship stands in to land.

EXT. ITHACA COVE - DAWN

The harbour of Phorcys, the old man of the sea: two bold headlands, wind-proof, a single entrance. At the head, an olive of spreading leaves. Near it a pleasant shadowy cave, sacred to the nymphs that are called Naiads: stone bowls and jars where bees hive, great stone looms where the nymphs weave sea-purple, two mouths, one toward the North for men, one toward the South for gods.

(SOUND: keel on sand, oars shipped)

The Phaeacians lift ULYSSES, still sleeping, bed and all, and set him on the sand. They lift out the gifts from under the benches and set them by the olive, a little away from the road, lest any passer-by steal them before he wakes. Then they put out for home.

INT. HALL OF OLYMPIAN JOVE - DAY

JOVE sits throned, heavy-limbed, white-bearded. Neptune stands before him, still furious, come from the sea.

(SOUND: distant thunder under the floor)

NARRATOR (V.O.)
Father Jove, he said, I shall no longer be held in any sort of respect among you gods, if mortals like the Phaeacians, who are my own flesh and blood, show me no regard.

NARRATOR (V.O.)
I said Ulysses should suffer many afflictions before he got home. They have taken him over the sea and left him in Ithaca, and have given him presents that he never would have got from Troy.

JOVE
What, O Lord of the Earthquake, are you talking about? The gods are by no means wanting in respect for you.

JOVE (CONT'D)
If any man is taking his own way, and paying you insufficient honour, you can always punish him as you please. Do as you like about it.

NARRATOR (V.O.)
I should have done so at once, Neptune answered, but I always look to you for a lead. I should like to stave their ship in when it is just in sight of their own town, and to bury their city under a high mountain.

JOVE
My good friend, do as you now propose. Wait till the ship is close in, turn it into a rock that looks like a ship, and let the people wonder. Then you can bury their city if you like.

EXT. PHAEACIAN ASSEMBLY - DAY

The Phaeacian vessel comes in sight of her own town, running fast. Neptune goes down, strikes her with the flat of his hand, and roots her in the sea, a stone, still in the shape of a swift ship. He goes away.

(SOUND: a hollow slap on timber, then stone, a cry from the shore)

The Phaeacians look at one another. ALCINOUS remembers the old prophecy: that Neptune would one day be angry because they gave safe-conduct to all men, and would stove a ship in, and bury their city under a mountain.

ALCINOUS
Alas, the old prophecy of my father has come true. Now therefore let us all do as I say. Stop giving people escorts when they come here, and sacrifice twelve picked bulls to Neptune.

ALCINOUS (CONT'D)
If we can appease him he may have mercy on us, and may not bury our city under the high mountain.

They prepare the bulls, and stand round the altar, looking at the stone in the fairway.

EXT. ITHACA COVE - DAY

ULYSSES wakes on his native soil and does not know it. MINERVA has shed a mist about him, that she may tell him everything without his wife and townsmen and friends knowing him till the suitors have paid for their crimes. The paths, the harbours, the tall rocks, the trees, all look strange.

(SOUND: surf in the two-headed harbour, a man groaning)

He starts up, looks at his native land, and groans.

ULYSSES
Alas, what country have I come to now? Are they cruel and savage, not fearing the gods, or are they kindly disposed and hospitable?

ULYSSES (CONT'D)
Where shall I put all this treasure, and which way shall I go? I wish I had stayed with the Phaeacians, or that I had come to some other great chief who would have been good to me and sent me home.

He counts the tripods, the cauldrons, the gold, the good woven clothes. Nothing is missing. He walks groaning by the sea.

MINERVA comes near him in the likeness of a young shepherd, a delicate boy, such as the sons of kings are, a double mantle over her shoulders, sandals on her shining feet, a javelin in her hand.

MINERVA
My friend, you must be very simple, or must have come from ever so far off, not to know this country. It has a name all the world over.

MINERVA (CONT'D)
It is rugged and not good for driving horses, but it is not a stony island for all that. It is not so very wide, but it is good for corn, and it has wine, rain, and a rich watering of dew.

MINERVA (CONT'D)
It is good for goats and cattle. There is good timber of all sorts, and watering-places that never fail. The name of this country, stranger, is Ithaca, and it is known even as far as Troy.

ULYSSES's heart leaps, but he will not say so. He invents a tale: he is from Crete, he has killed a man, Phoenicians were to have landed him at Pylos or Elis, the wind took them here, they left him sleeping and went on.

MINERVA smiles and strokes him with her hand. She takes the form of a tall, handsome woman, skilled in all the ways of good needlework, grey-eyed, known to him at last.

MINERVA
He must be indeed a shifty lying fellow who could surpass you in all manner of craft even if you had a god for your antagonist. Dare-devil, sly, crafty, never tired of fraud.

She is half scolding, half proud.

MINERVA (CONT'D)
Even in your own country you will not leave off your tricks and your lying tales. But we will say no more, for we both of us know knavery.

MINERVA (CONT'D)
Nay, you did not know me, Pallas Minerva, daughter of Jove, who am always with you in all your troubles. I made the Phaeacians take to you so kindly.

MINERVA (CONT'D)
I am come here now to talk matters over with you. I have also hidden your country from you, to try you. I want to know whether you are still the same, or whether the years have robbed you of your cunning.

ULYSSES
It is hard for a man to know you, goddess, however well he may be on the look-out. You take first one shape and then another.

ULYSSES (CONT'D)
When I was in the Trojan country you were kind to me. After we had sacked Priam's city and gone away you took no notice of me. I never saw you on board my ship.

ULYSSES (CONT'D)
Tell me whether I am really in Ithaca, or whether you are only amusing yourself and putting me off with a lie.

MINERVA
You are always taking something of that sort into your head. I cannot make you out. Any one else would have gone running off to see his wife and children, but you do not seem to care to know anything about them till you have turned your wife inside out.

MINERVA (CONT'D)
I never had any doubt about you, but I knew you would come home, though with the loss of all your men. I did not want to quarrel with my uncle Neptune.

She scatters the mist. He knows the land: the harbour, the cave, the spreading olive. He kisses the earth, and prays to the Naiad nymphs with a promise of gifts if MINERVA will let him live and his son grow up.

MINERVA
Take heart, and do not trouble yourself about that. Let us at once hide your things in the cave and see what we can do for you.

They enter the cave of the nymphs by the northern mouth and stow the tripods, cauldrons, gold, and clothes in the stone jars. MINERVA sets a stone against the door. They sit at the foot of the sacred olive and plan the death of the suitors.

MINERVA
This is how you are to do. I will begin by disguising you, so that no human being shall know you.

MINERVA (CONT'D)
I will cover your body with wrinkles, take the bright hair from your head, throw dirty rags about you, and put out the fire in your eyes. The suitors will take no notice of you.

MINERVA (CONT'D)
Then go to your swineherd, who has been always then and is still loyal. You will find him with the pigs by the fountain near the rock of the ravens. Stay with him to-night.

MINERVA (CONT'D)
I will go to Sparta and fetch your son Telemachus, who went there to try and get news of you.

ULYSSES
Why did you not tell him, seeing you knew all about it? Did you want him too to go wandering about and suffer, while others eat up his estate?

MINERVA
Do not be uneasy about him. I sent him that he might be well spoken of for having gone. He is in no sort of difficulty. He is staying quite comfortably with Menelaus.

MINERVA (CONT'D)
The suitors have put out to sea and are lying in wait for him, but I do not think they will find him.

She touches him with her wand. His flesh withers on his bones. His bright hair goes. She puts out the fire in his eyes. She throws about him a filthy tattered shirt and a tunic begrimed with smoke, an undressed deer skin as an outer garment, a staff, and a wallet all in holes, with a twisted thong to sling it over his shoulder.

(SOUND: the wand, cloth settling on a changed body)

When the pair have thus laid their plans they part. The goddess goes straight to Lacedaemon to fetch TELEMACHUS.

