EXT. EUMAEUS STEADING - DAY

A rough track up through wooded country, over the crest of the mountain. ULYSSES comes as MINERVA left him: wrinkles, the bright hair gone, smoke-filthy rags, an undressed deer skin, a staff, a wallet all in holes on a twisted thong.

The steading sits by the yards the swineherd built in his master's absence, without help from Penelope or old Laertes: quarried stone, a palisade of split oak, a hedge of thorn. Twelve sties, fifty sows in each. The boars lie apart, three hundred and sixty of them, the suitors eating into the number every day. Four dogs, fierce as wild beasts, sleep with the swineherd.

(SOUND: dogs exploding into bark, then stones rattling)

EUMAEUS, loyal, older, weathered, sits in front of his hut cutting out a pair of sandals from a piece of good brown leather. The dogs fly at the beggar. EUMAEUS drops the hide, shouts, and drives them off with a shower of stones.

EUMAEUS
Old man, the dogs were likely to have been too much for you, and then you would have been the death of me. I have other things to vex me already.

He takes the stranger into the hut.

INT. EUMAEUS HUT - DAY

Brushwood strewn for a seat, and over it the shaggy wild-goat skin that is the swineherd's own bed. ULYSSES sits, wrinkles deep, rags begrimed with smoke. EUMAEUS, weathered, loyal, is glad.

(SOUND: a young pig squealing, then fat in the fire)

EUMAEUS
It is not right that I should sit here while you are on your feet. Strangers and beggars are from Jove, and a small gift is welcome.

He kills a young pig, singes it, joints it, and roasts all the meat on spits. He sets it before ULYSSES, hot on the spits, with a basket of bread, and mixes wine in a bowl of ivy-wood.

EUMAEUS
Eat, stranger, and make the best of what is here. The suitors eat the fat hogs, and care nothing for the eye of heaven, nor for pity.

EUMAEUS (CONT'D)
The blessed gods love not such shameful doings. They honour justice and the righteous acts of heaven. Even enemies, when they land and Jove grants them spoil, fill their ships and go home.

EUMAEUS (CONT'D)
Fear of heaven is strong upon them. These men must know that my master has died a terrible death, and yet they will not pay their court honestly, nor go back to their own houses.

EUMAEUS (CONT'D)
They lie here wasting his substance without mercy. Every day they sacrifice not one victim nor two only, and they draw the wine madly.

ULYSSES
My friend, you are a man of sense and feeling. May Jove and the other gods grant you your heart's desire for the kind way in which you have treated me.

EUMAEUS
Stranger, though a still poorer man should come here, it would not be right for me to insult him. All strangers and beggars are from Jove.

EUMAEUS (CONT'D)
I have little to give, but I give it gladly. It is the way of servants to be afraid when they have young lords over them. My true master would have given me a house and a piece of land and a good wife.

He names the man he still serves in his heart.

EUMAEUS (CONT'D)
He would have done it if he had grown old here. But he is gone. I wish the whole race of Helen had perished, for she has been the death of many a good man.

EUMAEUS (CONT'D)
It was to go to Ilius under king Agamemnon that my master went, to fight the Trojans.

ULYSSES eats and drinks. Then he asks, as a man who would make a story good.

ULYSSES
Who was this master of yours, that bought you and paid for you? You say he died out fighting for Agamemnon. Tell me his name. I may have seen him. Jove and the other gods know whether I can give you any news of him.

EUMAEUS
Old man, no traveller who comes here with news of Ulysses will get his wife and son to believe him.

EUMAEUS (CONT'D)
They have been too often taken in by vagabonds who will not speak the truth. My mistress asks them all manner of questions and weeps, as a woman must when her husband is far away.

EUMAEUS (CONT'D)
You too, old man, would soon make up a story if some one would give you a cloak and shirt. As for Ulysses, he is dead and gone. I shall never find so kind a master again, not even if I were to go back to my father's house.

EUMAEUS (CONT'D)
I do not so much mourn for him as for Telemachus, his son, whom he left behind him a baby. I thought the boy would be second to no man. Then some god took his wits, or some man did, and he went to Pylos.

ULYSSES
I will take my oath that Ulysses will return. Let Jove, the first and mightiest of the gods, be my witness, and this hospitable table, and the hearth of blameless Ulysses to which I have come.

He lays his hand on the ivy-wood bowl.

ULYSSES (CONT'D)
Ulysses shall return while this very moon is waning and the next is waxing, and shall take his revenge upon all those who are here eating up his estate and paying court to his wife.

EUMAEUS
Old man, I shall get no good of your oath, nor will Ulysses ever come home. Drink in peace, and let us talk of something else. Do not keep on reminding me of all this.

EUMAEUS (CONT'D)
My heart runs over when any one speaks of my honoured master. As for your oath, we will let it be. But tell me, and tell me true, who and whence are you? Where is your town, and who are your parents?

EUMAEUS (CONT'D)
What manner of ship did you come in, and how did the sailors bring you to Ithaca? I do not suppose you came here on foot.

ULYSSES
I will tell you all about it. If you and I were to stay here and have food and wine enough to last us, we might sit here for a whole twelvemonth and still not finish the story of the sorrows I have endured.

He gives himself another name, another island.

ULYSSES (CONT'D)
I am from Crete, a rich man's son, though not the son of his true wife. My father was Castor, and was held in honour as a god among the Cretans. When he died his true sons divided his estate and gave me only a small portion.

ULYSSES (CONT'D)
I took a wife from a wealthy house, for I was not a poor man nor a coward. I loved ships and wars and spears and arrows. Heaven put this love in my heart, for different men take their pleasure in different ways.

He tells of nine sea-raids, of leading Cretans to Troy, of Egypt afterward, of seven years gathering wealth there, of a knavish Phoenician who would have sold him, of a wreck, of Thesprotia, of a king who showed him the treasure of Ulysses and said the man himself was gone to Dodona to ask Jove's will, whether to return openly or in secret.

ULYSSES (CONT'D)
He swore to me, as he poured his drink-offering, that the ship was launched and the crew ready to take Ulysses to his own country. I came away before him. Thesprotian sailors were to have landed me at Dulichium.

ULYSSES (CONT'D)
They plotted to sell me. They stripped me and gave me these rags. When they left their ship to eat on the beach here in Ithaca, I slipped my bonds and swam off, hiding in a thicket till they went. Some god has brought me to your hut.

EUMAEUS
Poor man, you have moved me with this tale of your misfortunes. But you will not get me to believe about Ulysses. Why should you lie in this way?

EUMAEUS (CONT'D)
I know all about my master's return, and that the gods hate him in that they have not killed him among the Trojans, or in the arms of his friends when the war was over.

EUMAEUS (CONT'D)
Then the Achaeans would have made a mound over his ashes, and he would have won great glory for his son. Now the storm-winds have spirited him away.

EUMAEUS (CONT'D)
I have nothing to do with inquiries. I never go to town unless Penelope sends for me when a messenger comes with news. They all sit round and ask questions, both those who grieve and those who eat up the estate.

EUMAEUS (CONT'D)
I do not care to ask, and I have not asked, ever since a man from Aetolia took me in with his story. He said he had seen Ulysses with Idomeneus in Crete, refitting his ships, and that he would be here by summer or harvest, with his men.

EUMAEUS (CONT'D)
Old man, now you have fallen in with me, I shall not treat you badly. You shall not want for clothes nor for anything else, but do not try to please me with lies, or with any such flattery. I shall not believe you.

ULYSSES
You are very determined not to believe me. I will make a bargain with you. If your master comes back, give me a cloak and shirt and send me to Dulichium, where I want to go.

ULYSSES (CONT'D)
If he does not come as I say he will, set your men on to throw me down from a cliff, that other beggars may beware of bringing lying tales.

EUMAEUS
That would be nice for me, and good among men, if I were to take you into my hut and then murder you. I should have to say my prayers to Jove in good earnest.

EUMAEUS (CONT'D)
It is supper time. My men will be in presently. We can get a good meal ready.

INT. EUMAEUS HUT - NIGHT

The other herdsmen come in with the fat hogs. They pen them for the night. The night is bad: the West wind, rain, a wind that always brings rain. EUMAEUS, weathered, loyal, goes out and cuts firewood. They bring in the best hog, five years old, for the stranger's sake. EUMAEUS does not forget the immortals. He throws hairs from the hog's head into the fire and prays that ULYSSES may come home. They roast and feast. He gives the chine, the portion of honour, to his guest.

(SOUND: rain on the hut, the West wind, fat dripping)

ULYSSES
Eumaeus, I wish you may be as dear to Jove as you are to me, for you have done me good in spite of my being in such distress.

EUMAEUS
Eat, my poor unhappy friend, and make the best of what is here. Heaven gives and takes away according to its will, for it can do whatever it chooses.

They eat. When they have had enough, ULYSSES, seeing that the swineherd is in a good humour and wanting a cloak to sleep in, tries him with a story.

ULYSSES
Listen to me, Eumaeus, and all of you. I am going to boast for a moment. It is the wine that makes me. Wine sets even a wise man singing and laughing, and it makes him dance and say things he had better not.

ULYSSES (CONT'D)
I was a man then. We were lying in ambush under the walls of Troy. The leaders were Menelaus and Ulysses and I, a third with them. When it was midnight and the night was very cold, the north wind fell, and there was a frost.

ULYSSES (CONT'D)
The ice set hard upon our shields. The others had cloaks. I had left mine behind, like a fool, and had nothing but my shield. In the third watch I nudged Ulysses.

ULYSSES (CONT'D)
I told him I was dying of cold. In a moment he had a plan. He told me to keep still. Then he whispered to the others that he had had a dream, and that we were too far from the ships.

ULYSSES (CONT'D)
He sent a man running back for reinforcements. The man jumped up, left his cloak, and went. I lay in it till morning. I wish I were as young and strong as I was then.

EUMAEUS
The story you have told is a very good one. You shall want for neither cloak nor anything else till to-morrow. Then you will have to shake your own rags about your shoulders. We have not many spare cloaks here, nor many shirts.

He makes up a bed close to the fire, throws sheep and goat skins over it, and when ULYSSES lies down he spreads a thick mantle over him, which he keeps for a change when the wind is very bad.

ULYSSES sleeps. The young men sleep beside him. EUMAEUS does not like sleeping away from his pigs. He slings his sword over his brawny shoulders, puts on a thick cloak to keep out the wind, takes the skin of a large and well-fed goat, and a javelin in case of attack from men or dogs.

EXT. EUMAEUS STEADING - NIGHT

Rain. The North wind. The pigs camp under an overhanging rock that gives them shelter. EUMAEUS, weathered, loyal, lies down there among them, armed, the goat-skin over him, and keeps his watch in his master's absence.

(SOUND: rain on rock, pigs shifting, the North wind)

