EXT. MENELAUS PALACE - GATE - NIGHT

The forecourt of the great house. TELEMACHUS, young, dark-haired, wonderfully like his father about the head and eyes, lies awake. Nestor's son sleeps hard beside him. MINERVA stands over TELEMACHUS, grey-eyed, no longer in disguise.

(SOUND: a night fountain, one man turning on stone)

MINERVA
Telemachus, you should not remain so far away from home any longer, nor leave your property with such dangerous people in your house. They will eat up everything you have among them.

MINERVA (CONT'D)
You should press your father Menelaus to send you home at once, or you may find that you have cause to be sorry.

MINERVA (CONT'D)
Her father and brothers are urging Penelope to marry Eurymachus, who has given her more than any of the others, and has been greatly increasing his gifts.

MINERVA (CONT'D)
See that she does not carry off some of your property from the house without your knowing it. You know what women are—they always want to do the best they can for the man who marries them.

MINERVA (CONT'D)
When you have got the great deep between yourself and the island, sail at night. One of the gods who are on Olympus will send you a fair wind.

MINERVA (CONT'D)
As soon as you get to Ithaca send your ship and men on to the town, and go to the swineherd yourself, the man who is in charge of your pigs. Stay with him for the night.

MINERVA (CONT'D)
Then send him to Penelope to tell her that you have got back safe from Pylos.

She goes. TELEMACHUS wakes his companion with a foot and says they must be yoking at once. The other, still heavy with sleep, tells him they cannot drive in the dark, and that they should wait for Menelaus and the gifts. TELEMACHUS yields till dawn.

INT. MENELAUS PALACE - HALL - DAY

Rosy-fingered Dawn. MENELAUS, travel-worn under his wealth, comes out to them in a jacket and a great cloak, and asks why they are up so early. Are they going home?

(SOUND: a fountain in the court, horses being led)

TELEMACHUS
Menelaus, son of Atreus, I want to go home at once. When I came here I left my property without protection, and I am afraid of staying too long.

MENELAUS does not keep him. He has the women set a meal, and goes to his store-room for gifts: a two-handled cup, and a mixing-bowl of silver with a gold rim. HELEN, lovely as Diana, goes to her chests and takes the largest and most beautifully embroidered robe, the one that shines like a star, that she has made with her own hands.

MENELAUS
Telemachus, if you insist on going, I will not keep you. I should be angry with another man if he sent me away when I was his guest. I will let you go, and will give you the finest and most precious present I have.

He sets the cup in the young man's hands. HELEN lays the robe before him.

HELEN
I too, my son, have a present for you. I have made this robe with my own hands. Keep it for your bride, against the time of your marriage. Until then, let your mother take care of it.

They eat. The horses are yoked. As they leave the gate, an eagle flies to the right, carrying a white goose from the yard, and the people follow shouting. The eagle turns back toward the house. They are glad, and their hearts are comforted.

TELEMACHUS
Menelaus, what does this mean? Is the omen for us, or for you?

MENELAUS thinks how to read it. HELEN is quicker.

HELEN
Listen, and I will prophesy, as the gods have put it in my heart. As this eagle came from the mountain where he was bred and born, and snatched up the goose, even so shall Ulysses come back and take his revenge.

HELEN (CONT'D)
He shall come back either openly or in secret, and shall visit every one of the suitors with this doom.

TELEMACHUS
May Jove so grant it. I would vow to you as to a god all the rest of my days.

They drive all day. At sundown they reach Pherae and lie at the house of Diocles. Another dawn, another drive, until they come down to the city of Pylos.

EXT. PYLOS SHORE - DAY

The black ship waits. TELEMACHUS, dark-haired, his father's look about the head and eyes, will not go up to Nestor's house, lest the old man keep him against his will. He asks his companion to drop him at the ship and make his excuses. The other agrees, drives to the hull, unloads the gold and raiment, and turns the horses back toward the city.

(SOUND: gear set on deck, surf, a man running)

A stranger comes running from the inland road, a seer, fleeing because he has killed a man in Argos. He asks who they are, and begs a place on board. TELEMACHUS takes him as a guest, without asking more. They stow, sacrifice, and wait for evening.

INT. EUMAEUS HUT - DAY

Meanwhile, in the hut, ULYSSES, wrinkled, bare-scalped, smoke-filthy rags, deer skin, staff, holed wallet, sits with EUMAEUS, weathered, loyal. He would test the swineherd still further.

(SOUND: pigs at the sties, a knife on wood)

ULYSSES
Eumaeus, I mean to go to-morrow to the town and beg, so as not to be a burden on you and your men. Advise me well, and send a guide with me.

ULYSSES (CONT'D)
I will go about the city on my own account, if I can pick up a meal and a cup of wine. I would then go to the house of Ulysses and tell what I know to the wise Penelope.

ULYSSES (CONT'D)
I might go among the suitors and see if they will give me a dinner. I could do any kind of work they want. Heaven has given me all manner of skill.

EUMAEUS
Heaven help me, what put such a notion as that into your head? You would go to your certain death if you did. The suitors are proud and violent.

EUMAEUS (CONT'D)
Their serving men are not at all like you. They are young and well dressed, and their heads and faces are always greased. Stay here. No one is annoyed by your presence, not I, nor any of my men.

EUMAEUS (CONT'D)
When Telemachus comes he will give you a cloak and shirt, and will send you wherever you want to go.

ULYSSES
I hope Telemachus may come, as you say. Tell me now about the mother of Ulysses, and about his father whom he left on the threshold of old age when he set out for Troy. Are they still living, or are they already dead and in the house of Hades?

EUMAEUS
I will tell you all about them. Laertes is still living, but he prays night and day to Jove now that he may die, for he is exceedingly sorry about the loss of his wife, and about his son.

EUMAEUS (CONT'D)
She died of grief for her son when he did not come back. It was a terrible grief to me. As long as she was alive, kind as she was, I liked asking after her.

ULYSSES
Who was your father, and who was your mother? Where is your town? How did you come to be bought by Laertes? Did they take you in a raid, or did pirates sell you?

EUMAEUS
My friend, since you ask me, listen and enjoy your wine. The nights are long. There is time for sleep, and there is time for talk.

EUMAEUS (CONT'D)
There is an island called Syra, over above Ortygia, where the sun turns. It is not very thickly peopled, but it is a good land, with much cattle, wine, and wheat.

EUMAEUS (CONT'D)
There is no hunger there, nor any sickness. When men grow old, Apollo of the silver bow comes with Diana, and kills them with his painless shafts.

EUMAEUS (CONT'D)
I was the son of the king. Phoenicians came, greedy knaves. There was a Sidonian woman in my father's house, tall and handsome, whom pirates had stolen. One of the sailors seduced her.

EUMAEUS (CONT'D)
When the ship was loaded she took me with her, three gold goblets in her hands. We sailed six days. On the seventh Diana killed her, and she dropped like a sea-gull into the hold.

EUMAEUS (CONT'D)
They threw her overboard. I was left alone, broken-hearted. The wind and current took us to Ithaca. Laertes bought me with his own money. That is how I came to see this country.

ULYSSES
Eumaeus, I have heard the story of your misfortunes with the most lively interest and pity, but Jove has given you good as well as evil, for you have fallen in with a kind master.

They talk on, then sleep in the hut.

EXT. OPEN SEA - NIGHT

TELEMACHUS's ship runs before a fair wind MINERVA sends. The fugitive seer sits among the crew. They pass Crouni and Chalcis. The sun sets. They make Elis. From there they steer for the flying islands, wondering whether they shall escape death or be taken.

(SOUND: night wind in the sail, the strait running)

Dawn. Ithaca shows. A hawk, Apollo's messenger, flies on their right and tears a dove. Feathers fall between the ship and TELEMACHUS. The seer calls him apart and reads the bird: the blood of Ithacan kings is the strongest in the islands, and his house shall be royal forever. TELEMACHUS is glad, and promises him a welcome.

EXT. ITHACA FIRST POINT - DAY

They row in at the first point they reach, not the town harbour. They land, lift out the food and the wine, and the clothes. TELEMACHUS, dark-haired, his father's look about the head and eyes, tells his crew to take the ship on to the city. He will visit the herdsmen and be in town by evening. He gives the seer to a trusted crewman, to lodge him at home until he comes.

(SOUND: keel on pebbles, hawsers loosed)

They thrust the ship off and make on towards the city as they have been told. TELEMACHUS strides inland as fast as he can.

EXT. EUMAEUS STEADING - DAY

The homestead where the countless herds of swine are feeding. The palisade of split oak. The excellent swineherd's yards. TELEMACHUS comes down the track toward the hut, still walking hard, the dust of the road on him.

(SOUND: pigs, a distant dog beginning to note him)

