INT. EUMAEUS HUT - DAY

Daybreak. A fire. ULYSSES, wrinkled, bare-scalped, smoke-filthy rags, deer skin, and EUMAEUS, weathered, loyal, get breakfast ready. The other men have gone out with the pigs.

(SOUND: a fire catching, then feet on the yard and no bark)

The dogs do not bark. They fawn. ULYSSES hears the feet.

ULYSSES
Eumaeus, I hear footsteps. I think it is some comrade or some one of your acquaintance, for the dogs are fawning and not barking.

TELEMACHUS, young, dark-haired, wonderfully like his father about the head and eyes, stands in the doorway. EUMAEUS lets the cups fall. He goes straight to the boy, kisses his head and both his eyes and both his hands, as a father kisses a son returned from far away after ten years of pain.

(SOUND: cups hitting earth, EUMAEUS weeping)

EUMAEUS
So you are come, Telemachus, light of my eyes that you are. I made sure I was never going to see you any more. Come in, my dear child, and sit down, that I may look my fill at you.

EUMAEUS (CONT'D)
You do not often visit us herdsmen. You stay in the town, as if you liked the noise of the suitors.

TELEMACHUS
I will tell you truly. I have come from Pylos and have brought my ship to Ithaca. I have come here to see you first, and to hear whether my mother is still in the house, or whether some one else has married her.

TELEMACHUS (CONT'D)
So that Ulysses' bed is without bedding and covered with cobwebs.

EUMAEUS
She is still in the house, with a heavy heart, and she weeps both night and day.

TELEMACHUS comes in and crosses the stone threshold. His father rises from his seat to give it him. TELEMACHUS will not take it.

TELEMACHUS
Sit down, stranger. There is another seat. My man here will make us one.

EUMAEUS strews brushwood and a fleece. TELEMACHUS sits. The swineherd sets bread and the roast meats left from yesterday, and mixes wine in an ivy-wood bowl. They put forth their hands.

TELEMACHUS
Father, who is this stranger? How did you come by him? Did some ship bring him, or did he come here on foot?

EUMAEUS
I will tell you the truth, my child. He says he is a Cretan, and that he has been a wanderer in many cities. He has run away from a Thesprotian ship and come to my steading. I put him in your hands. Do as you like with him.

TELEMACHUS
Eumaeus, I am much distressed by what you have told me. How can I take this stranger into my house? I am as yet young, and I have no stomach for fighting a man who does me wrong.

TELEMACHUS (CONT'D)
If the stranger stays here, I will send him clothes and all he wants to eat, and I will see that he comes to no harm. I cannot have him going among the suitors. They are too violent.

ULYSSES
My friend, I will answer you. If I were as young as you are, I would rather die fighting in my own house than go on looking at these disgraceful doings.

ULYSSES (CONT'D)
Are you cowed, or do the people hate you? Have you any reason to complain of your brothers? I wish I were young, son to Ulysses, or Ulysses himself. I would go to the house and be the death of some of them.

TELEMACHUS
I will tell you truly. The people as a whole are not angry with me. I have no brothers. Jove has made our family run in single heirs.

TELEMACHUS (CONT'D)
Arceisius had an only son Laertes, and Laertes an only son Ulysses. Ulysses left me here when I was a child, his only son. That is why my house is full of enemies.

He turns to the swineherd.

TELEMACHUS (CONT'D)
Go at once, Eumaeus, and tell Penelope that I am safe and have come from Pylos. I will stay here. Come straight back when you have told her. Do not go to Laertes.

TELEMACHUS (CONT'D)
Tell my mother to send the housekeeper to him as fast as she can, and let her give him the news.

EUMAEUS
I understand and heed you. Am I to go to Laertes as well with the same news? He has been in great sorrow about Ulysses, and about you. He has left off eating and drinking, and does not look after his farm.

TELEMACHUS
It is hard, but we must leave him for the present. If men could have everything their own way, I should choose first the return of my father.

EUMAEUS takes his sandals, slings them on, and goes.

EXT. EUMAEUS STEADING - DAY

The yard. The dogs. MINERVA comes to the hut door in the likeness of a tall, handsome woman, grey-eyed. ULYSSES sees her. The dogs see her and slink away, whimpering, to the other side of the yard. TELEMACHUS does not see her. She signs with her brows.

(SOUND: dogs whimpering, a still yard)

ULYSSES goes out. She speaks close.

MINERVA
Telemachus, noble son of Ulysses, it is now time for you to tell your son, and not to keep him in the dark any longer. Then hatch your plot for the destruction of the suitors, and come to the town.

MINERVA (CONT'D)
I will not be long in joining you, for I am very eager for the fight.

She touches him with her golden wand. The rags fall from his presence. He is taller, younger, the dark beard restored, the skin clean, the fire back in his eyes.

INT. EUMAEUS HUT - DAY

ULYSSES goes in again. TELEMACHUS stares and drops his eyes, afraid it is a god.

(SOUND: a stool scraped back)

TELEMACHUS
Stranger, you are no longer like what you were when I first saw you. You are changed. You are now like some one of the gods who live in heaven. Be gracious, that we may offer you acceptable sacrifice and golden gifts. Spare us.

ULYSSES
I am no god. Why take me for one? I am your father, on whose account you grieve and suffer so much at the hands of lawless men.

He kisses his son and lets fall a tear. TELEMACHUS cannot yet believe.

TELEMACHUS
You are not my father. You are some god who is trying to beguile me, that I may weep and suffer still more.

TELEMACHUS (CONT'D)
No mortal man could work such a change by taking thought. A moment ago you were an old man in filthy rags. Now you are like one of the gods who live in heaven.

ULYSSES
Telemachus, you ought not to be so immeasurably astonished at my being here. There is no other Ulysses who will come hereafter.

ULYSSES (CONT'D)
Such as I am, it is I, after much suffering and much wandering. I have come in the twentieth year to my own country.

ULYSSES (CONT'D)
This is the work of Minerva, who makes me what she will—for she has the power—sometimes as a beggar, and sometimes as a young man in good clothes. It is easy for the gods to glorify a man or to make little of him.

He sits. TELEMACHUS throws his arms about his father, and both cry aloud, more loudly than birds of prey—eagles, or vultures with hooked claws—whose young have been taken from the nest before they are fledged.

(SOUND: their crying, a pig far off, the fire talking)

When they can speak, TELEMACHUS asks how he came, what sailors, for he knows his father did not come on foot. ULYSSES tells him of the Phaeacians, the gifts in the cave, and MINERVA's sending him here.

ULYSSES
I am now in your hands. Tell me the number of the suitors, that I may consider whether we two can face them alone, or whether we must get help.

TELEMACHUS
Father, I have always heard of your renown, both as a fighter and as a counsellor, but this is too much. Two men cannot fight against so many and so strong.

TELEMACHUS (CONT'D)
There are fifty-two chosen youths from Dulichium, and they have six servants. From Same there are twenty-four. From Zacynthus there are twenty. From Ithaca itself there are twelve, all of them picked men.

TELEMACHUS (CONT'D)
They have Medon the bard, and a divine minstrel, and two servants who carve at table. If we face all these inside the house, I fear it will be a bitter and bloody piece of work.

ULYSSES
Do you consider whether Minerva and her father Jove will be help enough, or whether I am to try and think of some one else?

TELEMACHUS
Those two are good helpers, I grant you. They sit up in the clouds and rule over gods and men.

ULYSSES
They will not keep long out of the fray when we come to close quarters with the suitors. Go home early to-morrow. Mix with them. The swineherd will bring me to the town later in the day, in the likeness of an old miserable beggar.

ULYSSES (CONT'D)
If they use me brutally, endure it. Even if they drag me through the house by the feet, or throw things at me, look on and do nothing.

ULYSSES (CONT'D)
You may speak gently to them, and bid them leave off. They will not listen. Tell no one that I have come back—not Laertes, nor the swineherd, nor any of the servants, nor Penelope herself.

ULYSSES (CONT'D)
You and I will find out the temper of the women. We will also make trial of some of the men, and see who is for us and who is against us.

TELEMACHUS
Father, you will come to know me by and by. I do not think I am a fool. I think you will find me not without sense. Still, I do not think this trial of the men will do us any good.

TELEMACHUS (CONT'D)
Leave that, and think of something else. You will take a long time going to every man's farm, while the suitors sit at ease in the house and eat you out of house and home.

TELEMACHUS (CONT'D)
I would have you consider whether we can do the work alone. As for the women, I can tell you which of them are guilty and which are honest. I am not so sure of the men. We will see to that later.

INT. ULYSSES HOUSE - GREAT HALL - DAY

Meanwhile TELEMACHUS's ship comes into the town harbour. A herald from the crew and EUMAEUS from the country both go to PENELOPE with the same news: her son is back from Pylos. The SUITORS' hearts sink. They go outside the great wall, by the gate, and hold a council. ANTINOUS, handsome, insolent, the cruel mouth tight, is the first to speak.

(SOUND: a murmur of young men, bronze at their hips)

ANTINOUS
Here is a fine piece of work. Telemachus has done us a fine turn. We said he would never bring this voyage off, and he has got away in spite of us.

ANTINOUS (CONT'D)
Let us launch a ship, the best we have, and get a crew together, and send them to look out for the others and tell them to come back at once.

A man from the harbour is already there: he has seen the ship inside the haven, and the ambush coming home. They look at one another. ANTINOUS sits, then rises again.

ANTINOUS
This is bad. The people are no longer our friends. Let us be up and kill Telemachus in the country, away from the city, or on the road. We must do it before he calls the Achaeans in assembly.

ANTINOUS (CONT'D)
He will stand up and tell them how we plotted to murder him and failed. They will not take it well. Let us make an end of him, and divide his estate. The house can go to his mother and the man who marries her.

A quieter suitor speaks against killing a prince unless Jove gives a sign. The rest fall in with him. They go back inside and sit down.

PENELOPE, dark-haired, stately, worn with waiting, comes to the cloister, veiled, a maid on either side. She stands by a bearing-post and speaks to ANTINOUS.

PENELOPE
Antinous, insolent and wicked plotter. They say you are the best speaker and counsellor of your age in Ithaca, but you are nothing of the kind.

PENELOPE (CONT'D)
Madman, why do you plot against the life of Telemachus, and take no heed of suppliants, though your own father came here as a fugitive when the people were angry with him?

PENELOPE (CONT'D)
They would have killed him and torn him limb from limb, and Ulysses stayed their hands. Now you eat up his house, pay court to his wife, and try to kill his son. I will have no more of it.

A smoother suitor answers for them all, swearing no man shall touch TELEMACHUS while he lives, though he cannot speak for heaven. PENELOPE goes upstairs and weeps for her husband till MINERVA sheds sleep upon her eyelids.

INT. EUMAEUS HUT - LATER

MINERVA has touched ULYSSES again. The wrinkles are back, the rags, the deer skin, the fire gone from his eyes. EUMAEUS comes in. The dogs fawn about him. He tells his news: the messengers from the ship have already told the queen; he had no need. On his way he saw a ship full of armed men coming into the harbour, and he thinks it is the suitors, back from their ambush.

(SOUND: the fire, a man setting down his staff)

TELEMACHUS looks at his father and smiles, so that EUMAEUS cannot see it.

When they have finished their work and the meal is ready, they eat it, and every man has his full share. As soon as they have had enough to eat and drink, they lie down to rest and enjoy the boon of sleep.

