INT. EUMAEUS HUT - DAWN

Rosy-fingered Dawn. TELEMACHUS, young, dark-haired, like his father about the head and eyes, binds on his sandals and takes a strong spear. ULYSSES sits in wrinkles, smoke-filthy rags, deer skin, staff, holed wallet. EUMAEUS, weathered, loyal, is already at the fire.

(SOUND: leather pulled taut, a spear-butt on stone)

TELEMACHUS
Old man, I am now going to the town, that my mother may see me, for I am sure she will not leave off crying till she does. I shall give these instructions to the swineherd.

TELEMACHUS (CONT'D)
Take the stranger to the town as soon as he has had his breakfast. Let him go the round of the place and get a dinner if he can. I cannot have everybody coming to burden me.

TELEMACHUS (CONT'D)
If this makes him angry, so much the worse for him. I like to tell the truth.

ULYSSES
I do not want to stay here. A beggar is better off in a town than in the country. I am not of an age to stay about a farm and obey a farm bailiff. Go your way. This man will take me as soon as I have felt the warmth of the fire and the sun is up.

TELEMACHUS leaves, spear in hand, two swift dogs at his heel. MINERVA sheds a grace upon him that all who see him marvel.

INT. ULYSSES HOUSE - GREAT HALL - DAY

The high timbered hall. TELEMACHUS sets his spear in the stand among his father's weapons. EURYCLEA, grey-haired, straight-backed, is the first to see him. She bursts into tears and goes straight to him. The other maids kiss his head and shoulders.

(SOUND: women crying out, a spear seated among spears)

PENELOPE, dark-haired, stately, worn with waiting, comes from her room, looking like Diana or Venus, and weeps as she throws her arms about her son and kisses his head and both his beautiful eyes.

PENELOPE
So you are come, Telemachus, light of my eyes that you are. I never thought to see you again when you went off in your ship to Pylos, secretly, and against my wish, to hear about your father. Tell me now what you saw.

TELEMACHUS
Mother, do not make me weep nor stir the sorrow in my breast. I have just escaped with my life. Go upstairs with your maids and wash. Put on clean clothes and vow a full hecatomb to the gods if Jove will grant us our revenge.

TELEMACHUS (CONT'D)
I will go into the assembly to fetch a guest who came here with me. I sent him on with my crew and told Piraeus to take him home and look after him till I came.

She goes, wondering, and prays. TELEMACHUS goes out and fetches the seer. They bathe and sit to meat. PENELOPE comes down again, spinning, and will not be put off.

PENELOPE
Telemachus, I am going upstairs to lie down on that bed which I have never ceased to water with my tears from the day Ulysses set out. You did not choose to tell me anything of your father's journey before the suitors came back into the house. Tell me now what you heard.

TELEMACHUS
I will tell you the truth. We went to Pylos and to Nestor, who received me in his high house. He said he had heard nothing of Ulysses, living or dead, from any man on earth.

TELEMACHUS (CONT'D)
He sent me on with a chariot to Menelaus. There I saw Helen, for whose sake the Argives and Trojans suffered so much. Menelaus asked why I had come. I told him.

TELEMACHUS (CONT'D)
He was very angry about the suitors. He said he had heard from the old man of the sea that Ulysses was in an island, in the house of the nymph Calypso, who keeps him there by force, and he cannot get away, for he has no ships.

The seer sitting at table lifts his head. He tells the queen that Ulysses is already in his own country, sitting or moving, and that he is making a plan to punish the suitors. He swears it by Jove and by the board and hearth of Ulysses. PENELOPE answers that it may be so, and that if it is, the seer shall know her gratitude.

Outside, the SUITORS throw the disc and the javelin on the levelled ground in front of the house, as they have always done.

EXT. ROAD TO TOWN - DAY

ULYSSES, wrinkled, rags begrimed with smoke, deer skin, holed wallet, and EUMAEUS, weathered, loyal, take the rough track down toward the city. EUMAEUS gives him a staff to lean on. The path is stony.

(SOUND: staff on stone, goats' bells ahead)

At the fountain built by Ithacus, Neritus, and Polyctor, where the townspeople draw water, they meet a goatherd driving the finest goats of the flock for the suitors' dinner. He is a man of Melantheus's house, insolent, a favourite of the young lords. He speaks roughly to them, and asks why the swineherd brings this walking pest to town. He aims a kick at ULYSSES's hip.

ULYSSES considers whether to strike him dead with the staff or to dash his head against the ground. He endures, and says nothing. EUMAEUS lifts his hands and prays that Apollo may bring ULYSSES home to pay this fellow out. The goatherd curses him and goes on ahead, into the house, and sits among the suitors, opposite ANTINOUS.

EXT. GATEWAY OF ULYSSES HOUSE - DAY

The stone gateway. A dungheap, where the mules and oxen leave their droppings, till the servants take it to the fields. On it lies a dog, old, full of ticks: ARGOS, whom ULYSSES bred before he went to Troy, and never hunted. The young men used to take him out after wild goats, deer, and hares. Now he lies neglected.

(SOUND: a faint tail in dust)

As soon as he is aware of ULYSSES he wags his tail and drops his ears, but he has no strength to come nearer his master. ULYSSES looks aside and wipes away a tear, which EUMAEUS does not see.

ULYSSES
Eumaeus, what a noble hound that is over yonder on the manure heap. His build is splendid. I wonder whether he is as fine a fellow as he looks, or if he is only one of those dogs that come begging about a table.

EUMAEUS
This hound belonged to a man who has died in a far country. If he were what he was when Ulysses left for Troy, you would be astonished at his pace and his strength.

EUMAEUS (CONT'D)
He would let nothing get away from him in the forest. Now he has fallen on hard times. His master is dead and gone, and the women take no care of him.

EUMAEUS (CONT'D)
Servants never do their work when their master's hand is no longer over them, for Jove takes half the good out of a man when he makes a slave of him.

They pass into the house. As soon as he has seen his master, ARGOS dies.

(SOUND: the tail stopping, flies on the heap)

INT. ULYSSES HOUSE - GREAT HALL - DAY

TELEMACHUS sees EUMAEUS first and nods. He takes a whole loaf and as much meat as his hands will hold from the basket at his feet, and tells the swineherd to give them to the stranger. ULYSSES sits down on the ashen threshold, inside the door, leaning against a bearing-post of cypress that a carpenter once planed and made true to the line.

(SOUND: the hall's roar, cups, a lyre somewhere)

EUMAEUS sets the food in his hands. TELEMACHUS sends him also a message: go the round of the suitors and beg. Shame is no good companion for a needy man. ULYSSES prays that TELEMACHUS may be blessed, and begins on his food.

When he has eaten, he goes among the SUITORS from left to right, holding out his hand, as if he had been a beggar all his days. Some give, pitying him. They ask each other who he is. The goatherd says the swineherd brought him. ANTINOUS, handsome, insolent, the cruel mouth, turns on EUMAEUS.

ANTINOUS
You famous swineherd, why did you bring this man to town? Have we not tramps and beggars enough already to pester us as we sit at meat? Are you not satisfied with them, that you must ask this fellow as well?

EUMAEUS
Antinous, you have no right to speak so. Who ever goes out of his way to invite a stranger from a foreign country, unless it is a craftsman, a prophet, a healer of disease, a builder, or a bard?

EUMAEUS (CONT'D)
Such men are asked all over the world. No one asks a beggar who will only be a burden. You are hardest of all the suitors on the servants of Ulysses, and on me most of all. I do not care, so long as Penelope and Telemachus are in the house.

TELEMACHUS
Hush, do not answer him. Antinous has the bitterest tongue of all the suitors, and he makes the others worse.

He looks at ANTINOUS.

TELEMACHUS (CONT'D)
Antinous, you look after my interests as a father after those of his son. I wish you would give the stranger something yourself, instead of being so hard on others. I do not grudge it. I told the man to do as he has done.

ANTINOUS
Telemachus, you are very high and mighty. If all the suitors would give him as much as I will, he would not come here again for three months.

He seizes the stool from under his table, where he rests his feet.

ULYSSES stands before him.

ULYSSES
Give me something, friend. You seem to me to be the most distinguished of the suitors. You look like a king. Therefore you should give me a better portion than the others.

ULYSSES (CONT'D)
I too was a rich man once, and had a fine house, and was often giving to wanderers, whatever they might be. I had many servants and plenty of everything that people have who live well.

ULYSSES (CONT'D)
Jove took all away from me. He sent me off with roving pirates to Egypt. I stayed there seven years and gathered much wealth. Then a knave from Phoenicia beguiled me to Libya, meaning to sell me.

ANTINOUS
What god has sent us this pest? Stand off, in the middle of the floor, away from my table, or you shall be haled to Egypt or to Cyprus for a slave. You are a bold and impudent beggar.

ULYSSES
Of a truth you look as if you were not going to be the last man in the world. You sit at another man's table, and cannot bring yourself to give me even a bit of bread from a well-stocked house.

ANTINOUS's anger rises. He seizes the stool and throws it. It hits ULYSSES on the right shoulder, at the base. ULYSSES stands firm as a rock. He says nothing. He shakes his head in silence, brooding on his revenge, and goes back to the threshold with his wallet.

(SOUND: wood on bone, a hush, then the suitors' uneasy laugh)

He speaks to them all.

ULYSSES
Hear me, suitors of the illustrious queen. A man feels neither sore nor sorry if he is hit when he is fighting for his own possessions. But Antinous has hit me because I am poor and because I am driven to beg.

ULYSSES (CONT'D)
If there are gods and furies to avenge a beggar, may the end of his life come upon Antinous before his marriage.

ANTINOUS
Sit where you are, and eat your meat in peace, or go somewhere else, or the young men will hale you by hand or foot through the house till you are stripped.

The others are angry with ANTINOUS. One of them says it is not right to strike a poor tramp. What if he is some god from heaven? Gods go about cities in all sorts of shapes, to see which men keep the laws and which do not. TELEMACHUS nurses a great sorrow in his heart for the blow, but he lets no tear fall, and shakes his head in silence, brooding on his revenge.

INT. ULYSSES HOUSE - UPPER CHAMBER - DAY

PENELOPE, dark-haired, veiled, hears of the blow. She speaks among her maids.

(SOUND: the loom still, her breath tight)

PENELOPE
I hope the archer Apollo will strike him as he has struck that wretched tramp. May he never live to see his wedding.

She sends for EUMAEUS.

PENELOPE (CONT'D)
Go, good Eumaeus, and tell the stranger to come here. I want to see him and ask him if he has heard anything about Ulysses, or seen him with his own eyes. He looks like a man who has travelled far.

EUMAEUS
If these Achaeans would only keep quiet, you would be charmed with the tale of his adventures. I had him three days and three nights in my hut. He is like a father to me.

EUMAEUS (CONT'D)
He says he is an old friend of Ulysses, and that he comes from Crete, where the descendants of Minos live. He says he has heard of Ulysses as being alive and on his way home, in the rich country of the Thesprotians, and that he is bringing much treasure.

PENELOPE
Go and call him. Let him tell me this himself. The others may sit at the doors or in the house and make merry, for their hearts are light. Their corn and wine lie at their own houses, bread and meat for their servants, but they come here and waste us day after day.

EUMAEUS goes down.

INT. ULYSSES HOUSE - GREAT HALL - DAY

EUMAEUS comes to the threshold and speaks low to the beggar. ULYSSES, the right shoulder already darkening under the rag, does not rise yet.

(SOUND: the suitors' noise, a stool still on its side)

EUMAEUS
Father stranger, Penelope sends for you. The suitors will not have it so. She wants to ask you about her husband. If she finds you are speaking the truth, she will give you a cloak and shirt.

ULYSSES
I understand. I could tell Penelope the whole truth about her husband, for I know all about him. We have suffered together. But I am afraid of this crowd of cruel suitors.

ULYSSES (CONT'D)
Just now, when that man struck me and hurt me, Telemachus could not stop it. Tell Penelope to wait till sundown. Then she can ask me about her husband's return, and she can give me a seat nearer the fire. My clothes are in a very bad state.

EUMAEUS takes the answer upstairs, then comes back to TELEMACHUS.

EUMAEUS
I am going away, my dear child, to look after the pigs and the farm. What is done here is your concern. Look after the stranger, and see that he comes to no harm. We two will have to put up with a good deal if you are killed.

TELEMACHUS
Go, and look after the pigs. I would not be the death of you. In the morning come and bring victims for sacrifice. Leave the rest to heaven and me.

On this EUMAEUS takes his seat again, and when he has finished his dinner he leaves the courts and the cloister with the men at table, and goes back to his pigs. As for the suitors, they presently begin to amuse themselves with singing and dancing, for it is now getting on towards evening.

