INT. ULYSSES HOUSE - GREAT HALL - NIGHT

Evening. The SUITORS have been singing and dancing. A common tramp comes in who used to go begging all over the city, notorious as an incorrigible glutton and drunkard. He has no strength nor stay in him, but he is a great hulking fellow to look at. The town boys call him Irus, because he runs errands; his real name is Arnaeus.

(SOUND: the hall still loud, a heavy tread on the threshold)

He tries to drive ULYSSES, wrinkled, smoke-filthy rags, deer skin, from his own door, and tells him to get up or he will drag him out by the foot. The young men egg them on. ANTINOUS, handsome, insolent, laughs and sets a prize: a goat's paunch stuffed with blood and fat, already on the fire. The winner shall have it, and shall beg here alone. The loser shall be packed off.

ULYSSES
Friends, there is no way a worn old tramp can fight a younger man. Still, the belly is a cursed thing and will not let a man hide his hunger. I am ready. Swear that none of you will hit me a foul blow to help this fellow.

They swear. TELEMACHUS adds his own word, that the stranger is his guest, and that no man shall strike him. ULYSSES binds his rags about his loins. MINERVA makes his limbs stouter. A great thigh shows, unexpected. The hulking tramp's courage fails. ANTINOUS threatens him with being shipped to King Echetus, the bane of all mankind, if he flinches.

They put up their hands. The tramp aims at the right shoulder. ULYSSES hits him on the neck under the ear and crushes the bone. Blood runs from his mouth. He falls bleating in the dust, gnashing, drumming the ground with his feet.

(SOUND: bone giving under the ear, a bleat, feet drumming)

The SUITORS throw up their hands and die with laughter. ULYSSES seizes a foot and drags him through the doorway to the court, props him against the courtyard wall, puts a staff in his hand, and tells him to sit there and keep the dogs and pigs off, and not to try to be king of the beggars or he may fare worse.

He comes back and sits on the threshold. The SUITORS gather round, laughing, and wish him well. They send him the paunch. A quieter suitor, Amphinomus, brings a cup of gold, toasts him, and wishes him fortune.

ULYSSES
Amphinomus, you seem to me to be a man of good understanding, as indeed you may well be, seeing whose son you are. I have heard your father well spoken of.

ULYSSES (CONT'D)
You can see for yourself that the gods do not give a man prosperity for ever. I too was a rich man once. I did many foolish things, yielding to my own great strength and force, and trusting in my father and my brothers.

ULYSSES (CONT'D)
Therefore a man should not be lawless, but should keep quiet and take whatever fate heaven may have in store for him. I see the suitors wasting the estate and paying no respect to the wife of the man, who, believe me, will not be long away from his friends and his own country.

ULYSSES (CONT'D)
He is very near. May some god take you home before he comes, for when he does come there will be bloodshed, and you will not escape if you are still in the house.

He pours a drop and drinks, then puts the cup back into the young man's hands. Amphinomus goes through the hall heavy at heart, shaking his head, for he sees mischief coming. Even so he does not escape his fate. MINERVA has bound him to fall on TELEMACHUS's spear.

INT. ULYSSES HOUSE - UPPER CHAMBER - NIGHT

MINERVA puts it into PENELOPE's head to show herself to the suitors, that she may be more esteemed by her husband and son than she had been. PENELOPE, dark-haired, worn with waiting, laughs a mirthless laugh.

(SOUND: a loom left, her sudden laugh)

PENELOPE
Eurynome, I have changed my mind. I want to show myself to the suitors, although I detest them. I should also like to give my son a hint that he had better not have anything more to do with them. They speak fairly enough but they mean mischief.

Her housekeeper tells her to wash and anoint her face, and not to go down with her eyes still stained. PENELOPE will not hear of it. The gods destroyed her looks, she says, when her husband went away. She bids two maids come with her, for she will not go among men alone. Then she lies down. Sweet sleep takes her. MINERVA sheds grace over her: she makes her taller, and fuller in the face, and whiter than new-sawn ivory.

INT. ULYSSES HOUSE - GREAT HALL - NIGHT

PENELOPE comes down, looking like Diana or Venus, a maid on either side, a veil before her face. She stands by a bearing-post of the cloister. The SUITORS go weak at the knees. Each prays that he may lie beside her.

(SOUND: the hall falling quiet, a veil moving)

PENELOPE
Telemachus, I fear you are no longer so discreet and well conducted as you used to be. When you were younger you had a greater sense of propriety.

PENELOPE (CONT'D)
Now that you are grown up, and a stranger would take you for the son of a rich man, your conduct is most unbecoming. How has it come about that you have let the stranger be so disgracefully used?

TELEMACHUS
I cannot complain, mother. I understand all about it. I know when a thing is right and when it is wrong. I am not as old as I look. I cannot always get my way.

TELEMACHUS (CONT'D)
Those men are too strong for me. I am glad the stranger beat that hungry vagabond. I wish the suitors were all beaten as soundly, and were sitting still, some in the house and some outside, with their heads drooping and their limbs unstrung.

A suitor praises her, and says that if all the Achaeans in Iasian Argos could see her, there would be still more men dining in her house, so surpassingly good-looking she is, and so wise. PENELOPE answers him.

PENELOPE
Eurymachus, heaven robbed me of all my beauty whether of face or figure when the Argives set sail for Troy and my dear husband with them.

PENELOPE (CONT'D)
If he were to return and look after my affairs, I should both be more respected and show a better presence to the world. As it is, I am oppressed with care, and with the afflictions which heaven has seen fit to heap upon me.

PENELOPE (CONT'D)
When he left me he took me by the right hand at the wrist and said, Lady, I do not expect that all the Achaeans will come safe home from Troy, for the Trojans fight well.

PENELOPE (CONT'D)
So I do not know whether heaven will permit me to return. Look after my father and mother as at present, and when you see our son grown up, marry whom you will, and leave this your house.

PENELOPE (CONT'D)
This is all going on as he told me. The night shall come when I must take a hateful marriage. Jove has taken from me all hope of happiness. And what is it that you suitors do?

PENELOPE (CONT'D)
You look on while you eat your way through a great man's house. When men want to pay their court to a lady of standing, they bring oxen and sheep and make a banquet for the friends of the lady, and they give her splendid presents. They do not eat up another man's estate.

ANTINOUS
We will each one of us give you a present, Queen Penelope, and then you will not be able to complain that we sit here day after day without giving you anything.

ANTINOUS (CONT'D)
We will not, however, go back to our own homes till you have married the best man among us, whoever he may be.

The others applaud. Each man sends a herald for a gift. ANTINOUS gives a large and beautiful robe, with twelve gold brooches to hold it. Another gives a chain of curious work, amber beads strung with gold. Another a pair of rich earrings. Another a necklace. PENELOPE then goes upstairs again, and her maids go with her, carrying the presents.

The SUITORS turn to dance and song, and wait for evening to come on. When it is dark they set up three braziers in the court to give them light, and pile them with dry faggots and chips, mixing them with resinous wood. The maids of ULYSSES take it in turns to keep the fires going.

ULYSSES
You maids of Ulysses, who has long been away, go to the apartments in which your mistress lives. Sit with her and amuse her, or card the wool and spin. I will see to the light for all these people.

ULYSSES (CONT'D)
They may stay till morning if they like, but they will not beat me. I can hold my own against them.

The maids laugh and look at one another. One of them, Melantho, whom PENELOPE raised as her own child and used to give toys to, and who has no sorrow for her mistress, rebukes the beggar and tells him to go and sleep at the bronze-smith's, or she will throw a torch at him. ULYSSES scowls and threatens to tell TELEMACHUS, who will cut her into little pieces. The women slink away through the house, for they fear him.

He takes his stand by the burning braziers, looking after the lights, and keeps his eye on all the men. MINERVA will not yet let the suitors cease from their insolence, that ULYSSES's anger may grow yet keener.

A suitor jeers that the wine has got into the stranger's head, or that he is proud because he beat the tramp. He offers to take him to Epirus, to King Echetus, who will cut off his nose and ears and draw out his vitals for the dogs. ULYSSES gives him a black look and says nothing. TELEMACHUS speaks out.

TELEMACHUS
Eurymachus, if you have a mind to give me a present, I will take it. I will not have this stranger ill-used in my house. I am host here.

The SUITORS bite their lips and marvel at the boldness of his speech. Amphinomus takes the word and says they have had their meal, and that when they have made their drink-offerings they should go home to bed. The present matter can wait. It is for TELEMACHUS to decide about his guests.

They all agree. A servant mixes wine and water and hands it round. They make their drink-offerings to the blessed gods. Then, when they have drunk each as much as he is minded, they take their several ways, each of them to his own abode.

(SOUND: cups set down, benches shoved back, feet leaving the cloister)

