INT. ULYSSES HOUSE - GREAT HALL - NIGHT

The cloister is empty of suitors. ULYSSES, wrinkled, smoke-filthy rags, deer skin, ponders how, with MINERVA's help, he may kill them. TELEMACHUS, young, dark-haired, waits.

(SOUND: the house settling, one brazier still talking)

ULYSSES
Telemachus, we must get the armour together and take it down inside. Make some excuse when the suitors ask you why you have removed it.

ULYSSES (CONT'D)
Say you have taken it to be out of the way of the smoke, inasmuch as it is no longer what it was when Ulysses went away, but has become soiled and begrimed with soot.

ULYSSES (CONT'D)
Add to this more particularly that you are afraid Jove may set them on to quarrel over their wine, and that they may do each other some harm which may disgrace both banquet and wooing, for the sight of arms sometimes tempts people to use them.

TELEMACHUS calls EURYCLEA, grey-haired, straight-backed, and bids her shut the women in their room while he lays by his father's armour, which the smoke has tarnished. She asks who will go with him with a light, if he will have none of the maids. He says the stranger shall. She locks the doors of the women's rooms.

Father and son set to work bearing the helmets, bossed shields, and sharp spears down from the walls. Before them MINERVA, holding a gold lamp, makes a most beautiful light. TELEMACHUS cries out.

TELEMACHUS
Father, I see a great marvel. The walls and the crossbeams, the door posts and the supporting pillars, are all as though they were on fire. This must be the work of a god.

ULYSSES
Hush, and keep your thoughts to yourself. This is the manner of the gods who live in heaven. Go now to bed. I will stay here and provoke the maids and your mother. She will ask me questions.

TELEMACHUS goes out by torchlight to his own room, lies down, and waits for morning.

INT. ULYSSES HOUSE - GREAT HALL - NIGHT

PENELOPE, dark-haired, stately, comes down from her room like Diana or Venus. They set a seat for her by the fire, overlaid with a fleece. Her maids come from their cloister and take away the tables, the cups, and the braziers, raking the embers onto the floor and piling fresh wood upon them.

(SOUND: tables dragged, embers raked, a chair set)

The same maid who insulted him before, Melantho, rebukes him again for lingering, and tells him to go and sleep at the bronze-smith's. ULYSSES scowls and reminds her that he has already warned her. PENELOPE hears, and turns on the girl.

PENELOPE
Bold and impudent, I hear you. You shall smart for it. You knew quite well, for I told you myself, that I was going to see the stranger and ask him about my husband, for whose sake I am in such continual sorrow.

She has a chair set for ULYSSES opposite her own. He sits. She speaks first.

PENELOPE
Friend, let me ask you first of all, who and whence are you? Tell me of your town and parents.

ULYSSES
Madam, there is no one in the whole world who will find fault with you, for your fame reaches heaven itself, like that of a blameless king who rules over many mighty men, upholding justice.

ULYSSES (CONT'D)
The earth yields her increase to him, the trees are loaded with fruit, the ewes bring forth, and the sea abounds with fish, by reason of his good government, and his people do well under him.

ULYSSES (CONT'D)
Nevertheless, ask me about anything else you like, but do not ask me my name or my descent, for that will fill me with tears. I am a man of many sorrows.

PENELOPE
Stranger, heaven robbed me of all beauty the day my husband sailed for Troy. If he were to return and look after my affairs I should both be more respected and show a better presence to the world.

PENELOPE (CONT'D)
As it is, I am oppressed with care. All the chief men of the islands, Dulichium, Same, and the woodland island of Zacynthus, as also those of Ithaca itself, are eating me out of house and home.

PENELOPE (CONT'D)
I pay no attention to suitors, nor to the needs of guests, nor to those who are my suppliants, nor to the needs of the people who come to me on business, but I waste my heart away in longing for Ulysses.

She tells him of the web: the great tambour frame, the pall for Laertes, the three years of weaving by day and unpicking by night, the fourth year when the maids told, and she had to finish it.

PENELOPE (CONT'D)
Now I cannot escape the marriage. My parents are very urgent. My son chafes at the drain on the estate. He can take care of it himself now. He is a man.

PENELOPE (CONT'D)
I still, however, ask you about your family. You cannot have sprung from a rock or an oak, as they say in old stories.

ULYSSES
If you persist, I will tell you, though it will make me suffer as I have not suffered since I first began to roam. I am from Crete, a rich country. There are many men in it, and ninety cities.

ULYSSES (CONT'D)
I am son to the rich Deucalion, and brother to King Idomeneus, who went to Troy. My own name is Aethon. It was there that I saw Ulysses and gave him hospitality.

ULYSSES (CONT'D)
The wind had taken him to Crete as he was making for Troy. He stayed twelve days. I gave him barley meal, and wine, and oxen for sacrifice, and I entertained his men.

He describes, as a man who saw them, the thick double mantle of purple, the golden brooch with two sheathes, and on it a hound holding a spotted fawn between its fore paws, the fawn struggling as the hound gripped it. He describes also the herald who went with Ulysses, a little older, round in the shoulders, dark-skinned, curly-headed, Eurybates, whom Ulysses honoured above his other men.

PENELOPE weeps as she hears, as snow melts on the high hills when the south-east wind thaws it, and as the rivers run full. She weeps for her husband, who is sitting by her. ULYSSES pities her, but his eyes stay as dry as horn or iron. He hides his tears by cunning.

(SOUND: her weeping, the fire, no sound from him)

PENELOPE
Now I am sure, sir, that you were once his guest. The clothes you speak of are the very ones I gave him. I folded them myself, and put the gold brooch on, to be an ornament for him. I shall never welcome him home again.

ULYSSES
Take heart, and do not be so much dismayed. Ulysses is not dead. I will tell you the truth, and will hide nothing. I have heard of him as being alive and on his way home, in the rich country of the Thesprotians.

ULYSSES (CONT'D)
He is bringing much treasure with him. His ship and his men he lost on the wine-dark sea, as he was leaving the island of Thrinacia. Jove and the Sun were angry with him because his men had killed the cattle of the Sun.

ULYSSES (CONT'D)
The Thesprotians sent him on his way. I swore to the king I would not tell. Ulysses, he said, had gone to Dodona, to learn Jove's will from the high oak, whether to return openly or in secret.

He lays his hand toward the table.

ULYSSES (CONT'D)
I will take my oath. Let Jove, the first and mightiest of the gods, be my witness, and the hearth of blameless Ulysses to which I have now come. Ulysses shall return while this very moon is waning and the next is waxing.

PENELOPE
May Jove so grant it. If you would have me take you to my heart, I will have you washed and given a good bed. In the morning you shall sit with Telemachus and dine. If my words come to nothing, some poor tramp will have been well treated.

ULYSSES
Cloaks and rugs have been hateful to me ever since I left the snowy hills of Crete. I will lie as I have lain through many a sleepless night. I do not like having my feet washed.

ULYSSES (CONT'D)
I will not let any of these waiting-women touch my feet, unless there is some old and respectable woman who has gone through as much trouble as I have. I will not mind if she does so.

PENELOPE
I have an old woman, a most respectable person, who was the nurse of that unhappy man, and held him in her arms when she was giving him the breast. She is very feeble now. She shall wash your feet.

She calls EURYCLEA.

PENELOPE (CONT'D)
Come, wash the feet of this man, who is of an age with your master. Ulysses must by this time have just such hands and feet as he has, for men grow old quickly in misfortune.

EURYCLEA covers her face with her hands and weeps.

EURYCLEA
My dear child, I cannot think of any one who has suffered as you have. Many strangers have come here, but I say that I have never seen one so like Ulysses as you are, in body, voice, and feet.

ULYSSES
Old woman, that is what every one says who has seen us both. People have always said we were very like one another.

She brings the basin, mixes the cold water with the hot. ULYSSES sits at the hearth and turns quickly toward the dark, for he is afraid she will know the scar if she handles him. She comes near and begins to wash him. At once she knows the scar.

EXT. MT. PARNASSUS - DAY

A boar's white tusk. A young man, dark-haired, broad-shouldered, hunting with his mother's father Autolycus on Parnassus. The boar charges from his lair. The young man strikes, and the tusk scores him above the knee, a long deep gash. They bind it. The scar remains.

(SOUND: a boar crashing brush, a cry, then a basin in another room)

INT. ULYSSES HOUSE - GREAT HALL - NIGHT

EURYCLEA's hands are on that old wound. The basin falls. His foot strikes the cup. Water spreads on the floor.

(SOUND: bronze basin ringing on stone, water spreading)

EURYCLEA
It is Ulysses. I did not know you till I had handled the very feet of my master.

She looks toward PENELOPE. Joy and grief take her at once. Her eyes fill. She is about to speak. ULYSSES's hand is on the old woman's throat, pulling her close.

ULYSSES
Nurse, do you wish to be the ruin of me, you who nursed me at your own breast? Be silent, and keep it to yourself.

ULYSSES (CONT'D)
If you do not, I will not spare you when I have made an end of the suitors in this house, not even though you are my own nurse.

EURYCLEA
My child, what are you talking about? You know me too well. I will keep as close as stone or iron.

EURYCLEA (CONT'D)
I will also tell you which of the women in the house dishonour you, and which are innocent.

ULYSSES
You need not tell me. I will take note of them myself. Keep your mouth shut, and leave the rest to heaven.

He lets her finish the washing in a fresh basin. She rubs him with oil. PENELOPE, whom MINERVA has turned aside, has seen nothing. She speaks again from her chair.

PENELOPE
Stranger, I have still a little thing to ask you. It will soon be time for rest. Heaven has ordained the lot of man to bear trouble, but I have an especial sorrow.

PENELOPE (CONT'D)
By day I attend to my work and to the business of the house. At night, when others sleep, I lie still, and my heart is torn with many anxieties.

PENELOPE (CONT'D)
As a lion is in fear among a pack of men who are hemming him in, even so is my mind divided. Shall I stay with my son and keep everything as it is, or shall I marry the best of these suitors and go?

PENELOPE (CONT'D)
My son, while he was a child, would not hear of my leaving. Now that he is grown up he begs me to go, for he is vexed at the way they are eating up the estate.

She leans forward.

PENELOPE (CONT'D)
Listen to a dream I had, and interpret it for me if you can. I have twenty geese about the house that eat mash out of a trough, and I love to look at them.

PENELOPE (CONT'D)
A great eagle with hooked beak came from the mountain and broke all their necks and killed them. I wept and cried aloud, and the Achaean ladies came and stood about me.

PENELOPE (CONT'D)
The eagle came back and perched on a projecting rafter. He spoke with a human voice. Be of good courage, daughter of Icarius. This is no dream, but a vision of good that shall surely come to pass.

PENELOPE (CONT'D)
The geese are the suitors, and I was once the eagle, but now I am your husband, come back to you, and I will destroy them. Then I woke, and I looked, and saw the geese still eating mash at the trough.

ULYSSES
Madam, it is impossible to make any other interpretation of this dream than the one the eagle itself has given you. Ulysses is coming, and he will destroy the suitors.

PENELOPE
Dreams, sir, are awkward and confusing things. Not all of them come true. There are two gates, one of horn and one of ivory. Those that come through the ivory gate cheat us.

PENELOPE (CONT'D)
Those that come through the gate of horn mean something to those that see them. I do not think my dream came through the horn. If it had, we should have cause to hope.

PENELOPE (CONT'D)
I will now tell you of another matter. It is coming on to dawn. I am going to set up a challenge. Whoever can string the bow of Ulysses most easily, and send an arrow through each of twelve axes, him will I follow.

PENELOPE (CONT'D)
I will quit this house of my wedded life, so beautiful, so filled with all manner of wealth, and I think I shall remember it even in my dreams.

ULYSSES
Wife of Ulysses, do not put off this contest, for Ulysses will return before ever they can string the bow and shoot through the iron.

PENELOPE
If you will sit here and talk to me, sir, I should never wish to sleep. Still, people cannot do without sleep for ever. Heaven has appointed a time for all things.

PENELOPE (CONT'D)
I will therefore go upstairs and recline upon that couch which I have never ceased to flood with my tears from the day Ulysses set out for the city with a hateful name.

She goes upstairs, not alone, but attended by her maidens. In her own room she laments her dear husband till MINERVA sheds sweet sleep over her eyelids.

INT. ULYSSES HOUSE - UPPER CHAMBER - NIGHT

PENELOPE, dark-haired, lies on the bed she has watered with tears since the day he sailed. Sleep takes her at last. Below, in the hall, the beggar sits by the dying fire, the scar hidden again under the rag.

(SOUND: a house at the bottom of the night, one last ember)




INT. ULYSSES HOUSE - GREAT HALL - NIGHT

ULYSSES lies in the cloister on an undressed bullock's hide, with sheepskins the suitors have worn thrown over it. He cannot sleep. The faithless women go out laughing to their lovers. He tosses like a man turning a paunch of blood and fat before a hot fire, first one side, then the other, thinking how, single-handed, he shall kill so many.

(SOUND: women's laughter receding, the hide whispering under him)

MINERVA comes from heaven and stands over him, grey-eyed, in a woman's shape.

MINERVA
Why are you still awake, most unfortunate of all men? This is your house, and this is your wife, and this is your son, a man whom any one might be glad to have for his own.

ULYSSES
It is all very well for a god to talk. A man is bound to think. How can I kill these wicked suitors, single-handed? And if I do, how shall I escape their kindred afterward?

MINERVA
You are faint-hearted. Men trust a weaker friend than I am, a mere mortal. I am a goddess, and I will stand by you to the last. Even though there were fifty bands of men surrounding us, you should drive them all off.

She sheds sleep upon his eyes and goes back to Olympus.

Toward dawn he hears PENELOPE weeping in her room. He prays, with his hands lifted.

ULYSSES
Father Jove, if you gods have brought me over land and sea to my own country after you had afflicted me so sorely, let some one of those who are now waking give me a word of omen, and let Jove himself show me a sign.

Jove thunders from a clear sky. A woman grinding at the mill, the weakest, leftover at her task, stops the mill and speaks.

(SOUND: thunder in a clear night, the grindstone stopping)

NARRATOR (V.O.)
Father Jove, she said, you have thundered from a clear sky. Grant that this may be the last day the suitors dine in the house of Ulysses.

ULYSSES is glad at the omen.

INT. ULYSSES HOUSE - GREAT HALL - DAY

Rosy-fingered Dawn. Servants light fires. TELEMACHUS, dark-haired, comes down and asks EURYCLEA how they have treated the stranger. She says he sat drinking wine as long as he would, and that the women were told to make him a bed, but he lay in the cloister on an undressed hide like a forlorn outcast.

(SOUND: fires catching, sheep and goats driven in)

EUMAEUS, weathered, loyal, comes with three fat pigs. A goatherd brings goats and insults the beggar again. A cattleman, Philoetius, drives in a heifer and goats from the mainland. He asks who the stranger is, weeps for the likeness to his master, and says he has never found so kind a lord. ULYSSES swears, by this hospitable table, that Ulysses shall return while this moon is waning. The cattleman vows he would fight. They stall the beasts.

The SUITORS come in. They still talk of killing TELEMACHUS. An eagle flies on their left with a dove in its talons. A quieter among them says their plot will not prosper. They go in to dinner. TELEMACHUS seats ULYSSES inside the hall, at a little table, and warns them not to lay hands on him.

A brutal young man, Ctesippus, says they have had their fill of gifts, and throws an ox-foot from the basket. ULYSSES ducks. It hits the wall. TELEMACHUS springs up.

TELEMACHUS
Ctesippus, it is as well for you that you missed. If you had hit him I would have run you through with my spear, and your father would have had to see to your funeral rather than to your wedding.

The SUITORS bite their lips. Then MINERVA makes them laugh as if they were mad. They laugh with jaws not their own. The meat they eat is dabbled with blood. Their eyes fill with tears. The seer who came with TELEMACHUS stands and tells them he sees night upon their heads, and the walls and the beams bloody, and the porch full of ghosts hurrying down to Erebus, and the sun gone out of heaven. He leaves the house. They mock him. PENELOPE has set her chair opposite and hears everything.

INT. ULYSSES HOUSE - STORE-ROOM - DAY

PENELOPE, dark-haired, stately, goes to the treasure of her lord. She takes from its peg the great back-bent bow and the quiver, and a chest of iron for the axes. She sits with the bow on her knees and weeps aloud.

(SOUND: the store-room doors, her weeping on the horn)

Then she goes down to the suitors, veiled, a maid on either side.

INT. ULYSSES HOUSE - GREAT HALL - DAY

She stands by a bearing-post, the bow in her hands.

(SOUND: the hall falling quiet)

PENELOPE
Listen to me, you suitors who persist in wasting the estate of a great chief while he is still alive, and in paying your court to me. I bring you the bow of divine Ulysses.

PENELOPE (CONT'D)
The man who strings this bow most easily, and sends an arrow through each of the twelve axes, him will I follow, and quit this house of my wedded life, so beautiful, so filled with wealth.

TELEMACHUS sets up the axes in a straight line, all in one trench, stamped firm, and wonders at himself that he can set them so true. He tries the bow. Three times he makes it quiver. He would have strung it on the fourth, but ULYSSES signs to him. He sets it down.

TELEMACHUS
Alas, I shall be a coward and a weakling all my life, or else I am too young. Come, you who are stronger than I, try the bow and let us make an end of the contest.

The SUITORS try, in order, after they have warmed and greased the bow. They fail. ANTINOUS, handsome, insolent, would leave it till to-morrow, for it is a public holiday in honour of Apollo, and they can go on sacrificing. They agree. ULYSSES goes out with EUMAEUS and the cattleman.

EXT. GATEWAY OF ULYSSES HOUSE - DAY

Outside the gates. ULYSSES, still in wrinkles and smoke-filthy rags, tests the two men.

(SOUND: a yard empty of suitors, pigs far off)

ULYSSES
Would you be men to help Ulysses if he came back, or would you be for the suitors? Tell me what is in your mind.

They both pray that some god may bring him home. He says, then, that he is here. He shows the scar of the boar's tusk. They weep and would cry aloud. He checks them.

ULYSSES (CONT'D)
Stop, lest some one hear you. This is what you have to do. You, swineherd, shall bring me the bow. Tell the women to bar the door of their room. You, cattleman, shall bar the gate of the courtyard.

They go back in.

INT. ULYSSES HOUSE - GREAT HALL - DAY

ULYSSES asks, as a man who once had strength, to try the bow, to see if his hands are what they were. ANTINOUS and others shout that it shall not be given him. PENELOPE says if he strings it she will not marry him; she will only give him clothes and a sword and send him where he will.

TELEMACHUS
Mother, no one has a better right than I to give or to refuse the bow. Go to your room and see to your own work, the loom and the distaff. The bow shall be for men, and for me above all others, for it is I who am master here.

She goes, wondering, and weeps upstairs until sleep takes her. EUMAEUS, weeping, takes up the bow. The SUITORS shout at him. TELEMACHUS threatens. The swineherd puts the bow into the beggar's hands.

EURYCLEA is told to bar the women's doors. The cattleman bars the outer gate.

ULYSSES turns the bow every way, fearing worms have eaten the horn. The SUITORS mock. He strings it as a skilled bard strings a new peg of his lyre, without effort. He plucks the string. It sings like a swallow.

(SOUND: the bowstring's clear note, then thunder from a clear sky)

He takes an arrow that lies by, lays it on the bridge, and from his seat sends it clean through every axe-ring from the first onward.

ULYSSES
Telemachus, the stranger who sits in your hall has not disgraced you. The hour has come to get supper ready for the Achaeans, while it is daylight, and then we will dance and be merry.

TELEMACHUS girds on his sword, takes his spear, and stands by his father's seat, armed with gleaming bronze.

ULYSSES strips his rags. He leaps onto the threshold, pours the arrows out at his feet, and looks along the hall.

ULYSSES
Dogs, did you think that I should not come back from Troy? You have wasted my substance, have forced my women servants to lie with you, and have wooed my wife while I was still living.

He nocks.

ULYSSES (CONT'D)
You have feared neither the gods nor the indignation of men hereafter. The hand of death is now upon you.

He shoots ANTINOUS through the throat as the young man lifts a two-handled cup of gold. The shaft goes clean through. Blood bursts from his nostrils. He kicks the table from him and knocks the food to the floor. The cup falls from his hand.

(SOUND: the bow's snap, the cup ringing, benches going over)

The SUITORS spring up, looking to the walls for armour that is not there. They think it an accident. ULYSSES tells them they are wrong.

ULYSSES
You dogs, you never thought I would come back. Now you shall pay for all.

One of them tries to bargain, to lay the blame on dead Antinous, to repay the cattle. ULYSSES will take no ransom. They rush him. TELEMACHUS kills one. He runs to the store of arms and brings shields, spears, helmets for his father, for EUMAEUS, for the cattleman. The goatherd slips out to fetch armour for the suitors. They catch him on the second trip, bind him, and hoist him to a beam to suffer.

MINERVA appears as Mentor, then as a swallow in the roof-beam. Spears miss ULYSSES by her will. The SUITORS fall in heaps. The floor runs with blood. Groans fill the cloisters.

(SOUND: bronze on bone, men slipping in blood, the swallow's cry)

When it is done they lie in the blood and dust like fishes hauled onto a beach. The bard and the herald are spared at TELEMACHUS's word; they creep to the altar of Jove in the outer court.

ULYSSES calls EURYCLEA. She would raise the cry of triumph. He stops her.

ULYSSES
Old woman, rejoice in silence; restrain yourself, and do not make any noise about it. It is an unholy thing to vaunt over dead men.

He asks which of the women have been faithless. She names twelve of fifty. TELEMACHUS, EUMAEUS, and the cattleman make the twelve clean the gore, then take them out and hang them in a row from a ship's cable, their feet twitching a little, not for long. They take the goatherd into the courtyard and cut off his nose, ears, hands, and feet, and throw his body to the dogs.

Fire and sulphur cleanse the hall. EURYCLEA fetches the faithful women. They crowd ULYSSES, kissing his head and shoulders, and a longing comes over him to weep, for he knows them all.

INT. ULYSSES HOUSE - UPPER CHAMBER - NIGHT

EURYCLEA, grey-haired, trembling with joy, goes up to wake PENELOPE.

(SOUND: the door-thong, PENELOPE starting)

EURYCLEA
Wake up, Penelope, my dear child, and see with your own eyes something that you have been wanting this long time past. Ulysses is in the house, and has killed the suitors.

PENELOPE
My good nurse, the gods have made you foolish. Why do you wake me out of a sleep that had taken possession of my eyes? If any other of the maids had told me this, I should have had her well punished.

EURYCLEA
I am not laughing at you, dear child. It is quite true. The stranger whom they insulted in the cloister is Ulysses. Telemachus knew, but kept his father's secret.

PENELOPE comes down, doubtful still.

INT. ULYSSES HOUSE - GREAT HALL - NIGHT

She sits opposite ULYSSES in the light of the fire, and is silent, looking at him. Sometimes she thinks she knows him; sometimes the rags and the misery defeat her. TELEMACHUS rebukes her hardness.

PENELOPE
My son, I am stupefied. I cannot speak, nor ask him anything, nor look him in the face. If he is indeed Ulysses, we shall know one another better than others can, for there are tokens which we two alone know.

ULYSSES smiles.

ULYSSES
Let your mother test me. She is cold because I am filthy and in rags. Let us consider how it will be when the town hears of the killing.

He bathes. MINERVA sheds beauty over him again, taller, stouter, hair like hyacinth. He comes back in a clean tunic, like an immortal. PENELOPE still holds off.

ULYSSES
Madam, the gods have given you a harder heart than any other woman. No other wife could bear to keep her husband at arm's length when he had come back after twenty years of toil.

PENELOPE
Stranger, I am not proud or indifferent. I know what you were like when you left Ithaca. Euryclea, take his bed outside the room that he himself built.

ULYSSES
Wife, I am much displeased at what you have just been saying. Who has been taking my bed from the place in which I left it?

Anger lights him. This is the token.

ULYSSES (CONT'D)
It was I who made it, and no other man. There was a young olive growing in the inner court. I built the bedpost of it, inlaid it with gold, silver, and ivory, and stretched ox-hide thongs dyed purple.

PENELOPE's knees fail. Her heart melts. She runs to him, throws her arms about his neck, and kisses his head.

(SOUND: her breath breaking, his arms closing)

PENELOPE
Do not be angry with me, Ulysses. I have been shuddering all the time through fear that someone might come and deceive me. Now you have told me the secret of our bed, which no one ever saw but you and I and one maid.

He weeps as he holds the wife he has won. She clings as a swimmer clings to land after the sea. Dawn would have risen on their tears, but MINERVA holds the night long in the west, and stays gold-throned Dawn at Oceanus.

They go at last to the bed the living olive still anchors. He tells her, in the dark, of Calypso, of the Phaeacians, of the wanderings, until a deep sleep takes him. Then MINERVA lets Dawn rise.

EXT. MEADOW OF ASPHODEL - NIGHT

Meanwhile Mercury leads the ghosts of the suitors down, gibbering like bats in a cave. They pass Oceanus, the rock Leucas, the gates of the sun, to the meadow of asphodel.

(SOUND: a thin squealing of souls, as of bats in a hollow)

The ghost of Achilles and the ghost of Agamemnon stand among the famous dead. The suitors' ghosts tell how the beggar strung the bow and how the floor seethed with their blood. Agamemnon's ghost blesses Ulysses for such a wife, and curses the memory of Clytemnestra.

EXT. LAERTES ORCHARD - DAY

ULYSSES, TELEMACHUS, EUMAEUS, and the cattleman leave the town in a darkness MINERVA sheds, and come to the well-tilled farm of LAERTES, reclaimed with infinite labour. ULYSSES sends the others to kill a pig for dinner. He finds his father alone in the great vineyard, hoeing. LAERTES is very old, a dirty patched shirt, oxhide thongs on his legs, leather sleeves, a goatskin cap, looking very woe-begone.

(SOUND: hoe in earth, bees in the vines)

ULYSSES stands under a tall pear and weeps. Then he goes forward, crafty still, and praises the garden, and asks whose bondman this old man is, and whether this is Ithaca, and speaks of a guest he once entertained: Ulysses, son of Laertes.

LAERTES sheds tears. He fills both hands with dust and pours it over his grey head. ULYSSES's nostrils quiver. He springs forward, flings his arms about him, and kisses him.

ULYSSES
I am he, father, about whom you are asking—I have returned after having been away for twenty years. But cease your sighing. I have been killing the suitors in my house.

LAERTES
If you really are my son Ulysses, and have come back again, you must give me such manifest proof of your identity as shall convince me.

ULYSSES
First observe this scar, which I got from a boar's tusk when I was hunting on Mt. Parnassus.

He points along the rows.

ULYSSES (CONT'D)
Furthermore I will point out to you the trees which you gave me. You gave me thirteen pear trees, ten apple trees, and forty fig trees; you also said you would give me fifty rows of vines.

LAERTES's strength fails. He would swoon if his son did not hold him. When he comes to himself he lifts his hands.

LAERTES
O father Jove, then you gods are still in Olympus after all, if the suitors have really been punished for their insolence and folly.

They go to the house. The old woman washes LAERTES and anoints him. MINERVA makes him taller and stouter. They sit to meat. Dolius and his sons come in from the thorn-fence and stand astonished. ULYSSES bids them sit and eat. They kiss his hands.

EXT. ITHACA FIELD BEFORE LAERTES HOUSE - DAY

Rumour runs through the town. The people take up their dead. In the place of assembly, Eupeithes, father of Antinous, calls for vengeance. Some listen to wiser counsel and go home. The rest arm and follow toward the farm.

(SOUND: a town in grief, then bronze, then feet on the road)

MINERVA asks JOVE what he will do. JOVE answers that they shall swear a solemn covenant: Ulysses shall rule, and the others shall forgive the massacre of their sons and brothers. Peace and plenty shall reign.

She darts down from Olympus.

At the farm they arm: ULYSSES, TELEMACHUS, the two herdsmen, Dolius and his sons, LAERTES and Dolius grey-haired but armed. They sally. MINERVA comes in the form and voice of Mentor.

ULYSSES
Telemachus, now that you are about to fight in an engagement which will show every man's mettle, be sure not to disgrace your ancestors.

TELEMACHUS
You say truly, my dear father, and you shall see, if you will, that I am in no mind to disgrace your family.

LAERTES
Good heavens, what a day I am enjoying. My son and grandson are vying with one another in the matter of valour.

MINERVA comes close to the old man.

MINERVA
Son of Arceisius—best friend I have in the world—pray to the blue-eyed damsel, and to Jove her father; then poise your spear and hurl it.

She infuses vigour. LAERTES prays, poises, and hurls. The spear goes through Eupeithes' helmet. He falls, armour rattling. ULYSSES and TELEMACHUS fall on the front line.

They would kill them all, but MINERVA raises her voice.

MINERVA
Men of Ithaca, cease this dreadful war, and settle the matter at once without further bloodshed.

Pale fear seizes them. Their arms drop. They flee toward the city. ULYSSES gives a great cry and swoops after them like an eagle. JOVE sends a thunderbolt that falls just in front of MINERVA.

(SOUND: thunderbolt, bronze hitting earth, the run for the city)

MINERVA
Ulysses, noble son of Laertes, stop this warful strife, or Jove will be angry with you.

He obeys her gladly. She takes again the form and voice of Mentor, and makes a covenant of peace between the two contending parties.

EXT. GATEWAY OF ULYSSES HOUSE - EVENING

The same stone gateway. Evening light on the olives. Smoke rises now from the chimneys of the house, the smoke a man might long to see from the middle of the sea. Within, a man and a woman sit where the blood has been washed away. The night MINERVA lengthened is over. The island holds its peace.

(SOUND: a single night-bird, the hush after rain)

NARRATOR (V.O.)
So Minerva, daughter of Jove, made peace between them, and Ulysses came to his own again.

> FADE OUT.

THE END
